OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026", "title": "Every four-dimensional Kakeya set has full Hausdorff dimension", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "074", "family_title": "Kakeya in three and four dimensions", "abstract_html": "We prove the four-dimensional Hausdorff-dimension Kakeya conjecture: every subset of ℝ<sup>4</sup> containing a unit line segment in every direction has Hausdorff dimension four. No compactness or regularity assumption is imposed on the set or its witnessing line family.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^4\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A Kakeya set in \\(\\mathbb R^4\\) is a set containing a unit line segment in every direction.</p><p class=\"li\">No measurability or compactness assumption is imposed.</p><p class=\"li\">Hausdorff dimension is defined using countable covers:\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_\\delta^s(K)\n=\\inf\\left\\{\\sum_i(\\operatorname{diam}U_i)^s:\nK\\subset\\bigcup_iU_i,\\ \\operatorname{diam}U_i\\le\\delta\\right\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal H^s(K)=\\lim_{\\delta\\downarrow0}\\mathcal H_\\delta^s(K).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Let \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^4\\). Suppose that for every \\(e\\in S^3\\) there is \\(a_e\\in\\mathbb R^4\\) such that\n\\[\n\\{a_e+te:0\\le t\\le1\\}\\subset K.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\dim_H K=4.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Original location: Section \\(10.8\\), Completion of the proof.</p><p>Suppose \\(\\dim_HK&lt;4\\), and choose \\(\\sigma&gt;0\\) with \\(\\dim_HK&lt;4-\\sigma\\). Proposition \\(3\\) gives a version-\\(2\\) exact cap-class problem with positive horizon, using only outer slope measure and finite witness selections.</p><p>Optimize dimension cap, time rate, and narrowness by Proposition \\(6\\). The critical cap satisfies \\(d_*&lt;1\\), and \\(k&gt;0\\). Proposition \\(9\\) has already excluded bad version-\\(1\\) problems, supplying the scalar zero-horizon input used in Proposition \\(11\\).</p><p>For version \\(2\\):</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(\\ell=0\\), Proposition \\(9\\) gives candidates on every substantial residual, and Proposition \\(7\\) produces a forbidden actual improvement.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(0&lt;\\ell&lt;\\infty\\), Propositions \\(11\\)–\\(13\\) either give an immediate candidate or reduce to the horizontal model with \\(k=\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(2k=1\\), Proposition \\(14\\) gives the forbidden candidate.</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(2k&gt;1\\), Proposition \\(16\\) returns to wide intervals and invokes Proposition \\(12\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(2k&lt;1\\), Proposition \\(15\\) first reaches the full-time parameter scale; Proposition \\(16\\) then returns to wide intervals and invokes Proposition \\(12\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Proposition \\(17\\) excludes \\(\\ell=\\infty\\).</p><p>In each finite-narrowness case, Proposition \\(7\\) supplies the original-resolution atlas contradicting critical-path extremality. Every optimized narrowness regime is therefore impossible. This contradicts the positive-horizon problem from Proposition \\(3\\).</p><p>Thus \\(\\dim_HK\\ge4\\). The ambient upper bound gives \\(\\dim_HK=4\\), without compactness, measurability, or stickiness assumptions.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Section \\(11.1\\), unnumbered dimensional consequences", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every Kakeya set in \\(\\mathbb R^4\\) has packing dimension \\(4\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^4\\) is a Kakeya set.</p><p class=\"li\">For bounded \\(K\\), let \\(N_\\delta(K)\\) be the least number of radius-\\(\\delta\\) balls covering \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Lower and upper Minkowski dimensions are the lower and upper limits of\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log N_\\delta(K)}{\\log(1/\\delta)}\n\\quad\\text{as }\\delta\\downarrow0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every Kakeya set in \\(\\mathbb R^4\\) has packing dimension \\(4\\). Every bounded Kakeya set in \\(\\mathbb R^4\\) has both lower and upper Minkowski dimension \\(4\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem \\(1\\) gives \\(\\dim_HK=4\\). Use\n\\[\n\\dim_HK\\le\\dim_PK\\le4.\n\\]\nFor bounded \\(K\\), also use\n\\[\n\\dim_HK\\le\\underline{\\dim}_MK\n\\le\\overline{\\dim}_MK\\le4.\n\\]\nAll stated dimensions equal \\(4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Section \\(11.2\\), unnumbered projection consequence", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\dim_H E\\ge4. \\) No compactness or measurability assumption is required.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(D\\ge4\\) is an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^D\\) contains a unit segment in every direction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\dim_H E\\ge4.\n\\]\nNo compactness or measurability assumption is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a four-dimensional linear subspace \\(V\\subset\\mathbb R^D\\), and let \\(P_V\\) be orthogonal projection. For every unit \\(v\\in V\\), a witnessing segment \\(a+[0,1]v\\subset E\\) projects to\n\\[\nP_Va+[0,1]v.\n\\]\nHence \\(P_VE\\) is a Kakeya set in \\(V\\simeq\\mathbb R^4\\). Theorem \\(1\\) gives\n\\[\n4=\\dim_H(P_VE)\\le\\dim_HE.\n\\]\nProjection does not increase covering diameters, so no measurability assumption is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary \\(11.1\\), General direction-set bound", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\dim_H E\\ge1+\\dim_H D. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Unoriented directions in \\(\\mathbb R^4\\) are identified with \\(\\mathbb{RP}^3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(D\\subset\\mathbb{RP}^3\\) is nonempty.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^4\\) contains a nondegenerate line segment in every direction in \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Neither \\(D\\) nor \\(E\\) is required to be measurable, and segment lengths may vary.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\dim_H E\\ge1+\\dim_H D.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Invoke Keleti–Máthé \\([20,\\text{ Theorem }1.4]\\): there is a compact Besicovitch set \\(B\\subset\\mathbb R^4\\) such that\n\\[\n\\dim_HE\\ge\\dim_HB-(3-\\dim_HD).\n\\]\nTheir convention gives a unit segment in every direction. Theorem \\(1\\) implies \\(\\dim_HB=4\\). Substitute to obtain\n\\[\n\\dim_HE\\ge1+\\dim_HD.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "Section \\(11.3\\), unnumbered line-segment extension principle", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\dim_H\\left(\\bigcup_{S\\in\\mathcal S}S\\right) = \\dim_H\\left(\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\right). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal S\\) is a nonempty family of nondegenerate segments in \\(\\mathbb R^4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal L\\) is the family of their supporting full lines.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\dim_H\\left(\\bigcup_{S\\in\\mathcal S}S\\right)\n=\n\\dim_H\\left(\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the same-dimensional implication in Keleti–Máthé \\([20,\\text{ Section }1.2]\\), which derives the line-segment extension principle from the general direction-set bound. The required bound is Theorem \\(4\\). Therefore extending every segment in the given family to its supporting line preserves the Hausdorff dimension of the union.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 6", "orig": "Corollary \\(11.2\\), Four-dimensional Nikodym dimension", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every such Nikodym set satisfies \\( \\dim_H\\Omega=4. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\((M^4,g)\\) is a Riemannian manifold of constant sectional curvature in the normalized local setting of Gao–Liu–Xi.</p><p class=\"li\">The injectivity radius is at least \\(10\\), and the relevant geodesic segments have unit length.</p><p class=\"li\">For a Borel set \\(\\Omega\\subset M\\) and \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\), define\n\\[\n\\Omega_\\lambda^\\star\n=\n\\left\\{x\\in M:\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\text{there is a unit geodesic segment }\\gamma_x\\text{ through }x,\\\\\n|\\gamma_x\\cap\\Omega|_g\\ge\\lambda|\\gamma_x|_g\n\\end{array}\n\\right\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Omega\\) is a Nikodym set when \\(\\Omega_\\lambda^\\star\\) has positive Riemannian volume for every \\(\\lambda\\) sufficiently close to \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every such Nikodym set satisfies\n\\[\n\\dim_H\\Omega=4.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem \\(1\\) applies to compact Euclidean Kakeya sets in Gao–Liu–Xi’s convention and supplies their lower-bound parameter \\(d=\\alpha=4\\). Their Theorem \\(1.5\\) transfers this Kakeya Hausdorff lower bound to Nikodym sets on constant-sectional-curvature \\(4\\)-manifolds:\n\\[\n\\dim_H\\Omega\\ge4.\n\\]\nThe ambient manifold bound gives the reverse inequality. No variable-curvature conclusion is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 7", "orig": "Corollary \\(11.3\\), Fixed-chart curved Kakeya dimension", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^4\\) and, for every \\(y\\in Y\\), there is \\(\\omega_y\\in B_\\rho^3\\) such that \\( \\Gamma_y^\\phi((\\omega_y,0))\\subset E, \\) then \\( \\dim_H E=4. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Write \\(X=(x,t)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\times\\mathbb R\\), and fix\n\\[\nU=B_\\rho^3\\times(-\\rho,\\rho),\\qquad Y=B_\\rho^3.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">One smooth phase, defined on a neighborhood of \\(U\\times Y\\), has the form\n\\[\n\\phi(x,t;y)=x\\cdot y+\\psi(t;y),\\qquad \\psi(0;y)=0.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nG_0(X;y)=\\bigwedge_{j=1}^3\\partial_{y_j}\\nabla_X\\phi(X;y),\n\\qquad\nD_y=G_0(X;y)\\cdot\\nabla_X,\n\\]\nidentifying the wedge with its normal vector.</p><p class=\"li\">Throughout the chosen neighborhood, assume\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}\\nabla_X\\nabla_y\\phi=3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\det\\left(\n\\nabla_y^2\\langle\\nabla_X\\phi(X;y),G_0(X;y_0)\\rangle\n\\big|_{y=y_0}\n\\right)\\ne0,\n\\]\nand Bourgain’s condition\n\\[\nD_y^2\\partial_{y_i y_j}^2\\phi\n=C(X;y)D_y\\partial_{y_i y_j}^2\\phi\n\\qquad(1\\le i,j\\le3),\n\\]\nwith the same scalar \\(C\\) for every \\(i,j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the full local core\n\\[\n\\Gamma_y^\\phi(z)\n=\\{X\\in U:\\nabla_y\\phi(X;y)=\\nabla_y\\phi(z;y)\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(E\\subset\\mathbb R^4\\) and, for every \\(y\\in Y\\), there is \\(\\omega_y\\in B_\\rho^3\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Gamma_y^\\phi((\\omega_y,0))\\subset E,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\dim_H E=4.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the translation-invariant phase,\n\\[\nG_0=(-\\partial_t\\nabla_y\\psi,1).\n\\]\nThe conditions reduce to\n\\[\n\\det\\nabla_y^2\\partial_t\\psi\\ne0,\\qquad\n\\nabla_y^2\\partial_t^2\\psi\n=c(t,y)\\nabla_y^2\\partial_t\\psi.\n\\]\nGao–Liu–Xi \\([11,\\text{ Corollary }4.2\\text{ and Theorem }1.12]\\) show that \\(c\\) depends only on \\(t\\) locally and provide one phase-dependent coordinate change, after shrinking time and a nonempty direction ball:\n\\[\n\\kappa(u,s)=(u+B(\\alpha(s)),\\alpha(s)),\n\\qquad\n\\alpha(0)=0,\\quad\\alpha'(s)\\ne0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi(\\kappa(u,s);y)=u\\cdot y+sh(y)+q(y)+f(s),\n\\qquad \\det\\nabla_y^2h\\ne0.\n\\]</p><p>The core through \\((\\omega_y,0)\\) is\n\\[\n\\{(\\omega_y-\\nabla_y\\psi(t;y),t)\\in U\\}.\n\\]\nPut \\(V=U\\cap(\\mathbb R^3\\times\\alpha(I))\\) and \\(F=\\kappa^{-1}(E\\cap V)\\). Each anchor lies in the open set \\(V\\), so its core has a nontrivial closed segment there. The transformed gradient equation is\n\\[\nu=(\\omega_y-B(0))-s\\nabla h(y).\n\\]\nThus \\(F\\) contains a positive-length straight segment in every direction\n\\[\n\\nu(y)=\\frac{(-\\nabla h(y),1)}\n{\\sqrt{1+|\\nabla h(y)|^2}}.\n\\]\nThe nonsingular Hessian makes this direction map a local diffeomorphism, so its image contains a nonempty open patch of \\(S^3\\), hence a direction set of dimension \\(3\\) in \\(\\mathbb{RP}^3\\). Theorem \\(4\\) gives \\(\\dim_HF=4\\), allowing direction-dependent lengths.</p><p>A smooth local diffeomorphism preserves Hausdorff dimension of arbitrary subsets by a countable cover of bi-Lipschitz coordinate neighborhoods. Hence\n\\[\n4=\\dim_HF=\\dim_H(E\\cap V)\\le\\dim_HE\\le4.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 8", "orig": "Corollary \\(11.4\\), Rank-one quadratic curved Kakeya sets", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Suppose that for every \\(y\\in[-1,1]^2\\), some \\(w_y\\in W_B\\) satisfies \\( \\{(w_y+(tI_2+t^2B)y,t):t\\in I_B\\}\\subset K. \\) Then \\( \\dim_H K=3. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(B\\) is a real \\(2\\times2\\) matrix with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}B=\\operatorname{rank}(B^2)=1.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed compact anchor ball \\(W_B\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) and fixed time interval\n\\[\nI_B=[-\\tau_B,\\tau_B]\n\\]\nspecified after equation \\((1.16)\\) of Nadjimzadah’s lifting paper.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\) is compact.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Suppose that for every \\(y\\in[-1,1]^2\\), some \\(w_y\\in W_B\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\{(w_y+(tI_2+t^2B)y,t):t\\in I_B\\}\\subset K.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\dim_H K=3.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem \\(4\\) supplies the linear Kakeya hypothesis in Nadjimzadah’s convention: a compact set in \\(\\mathbb R^4\\) containing a fixed nondegenerate graph segment for every slope in a three-dimensional parameter ball has dimension \\(4\\), because its directions contain an open patch.</p><p>Write \\(B=UV\\), with \\(U:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^2\\) injective and \\(V:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R\\) surjective. Define\n\\[\n\\pi(x,z,t)=(x+tUz,t).\n\\]\nNadjimzadah’s lifted segments are\n\\[\nL_{y,w,r}(t)=((w,r)+t(y-Ur,Vy),t),\\qquad t\\in I_B.\n\\]\nThey map to the prescribed full curves:\n\\[\n\\pi(L_{y,w,r}(t))\n=(w+ty+t^2UVy,t)\n=(w+(tI_2+t^2B)y,t).\n\\]</p><p>Since \\(B^2=U(VU)V\\) has rank \\(1\\), \\(VU\\ne0\\). The slope map\n\\[\n\\Theta(y,r)=(y-Ur,Vy)\n\\]\nhas\n\\[\n\\det D\\Theta\n=\\det\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&amp;-U\\\\V&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\n=VU\\ne0.\n\\]\nThus varying interior \\(y,r\\) gives an open four-dimensional linear slope patch. The lifting convention retains all \\(t\\in I_B\\).</p><p>Apply Nadjimzadah \\([23,\\text{ Theorem }1.3]\\), with \\(3+\\operatorname{rank}(B^2)=4\\). It gives lower Hausdorff dimension\n\\[\n3-\\operatorname{rank}B+\\operatorname{rank}(B^2)=3\n\\]\nfor the compact curved sets in the statement. The ambient bound gives equality. Compactness and full-curve containment on the fixed interval are retained hypotheses.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Definitions \\(2.1\\), \\(2.2\\), \\(2.6\\); Lemmas \\(2.3\\), \\(2.4\\), \\(2.5\\), \\(2.7\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After a tempered inverse-subpower selection and a subsequence, the observation densities admit homogeneous nondecreasing Lipschitz profiles.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">An exact problem is a sequence with resolution \\(N_0\\to\\infty\\), fixed length \\(L&gt;0\\), and scales\n\\[\na_r=N_0^{-r},\\qquad0\\le r\\le L.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A changeable factor \\(K_0\\ge1\\) is subpower when \\(\\log K_0/\\log N_0\\to0\\). Write \\(A\\sim_{\\log}B\\) for comparability up to such factors.</p><p class=\"li\">There are finitely many worlds \\(\\gamma\\), with weights \\(\\omega_\\gamma\\ge0\\), \\(\\sum_\\gamma\\omega_\\gamma=1\\). Each has a trajectory prior \\(\\nu_\\gamma\\), independent of uniform \\(t\\in[0,1]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The base trajectory is\n\\[\ny(t)=y_0+tV\\in\\mathbb R^j,\\qquad |y(t)|+|V|\\le K_0.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Version \\(1\\): \\(j=1\\), \\(w=w_0+w_1t+w_2t^2\\), \\(\\sup|w|\\le K_0\\), \\(B=1\\), \\(X=w\\), and tests are \\(w-F(t,y)\\), with \\(\\deg F\\le2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Version \\(2\\): \\(j=2\\), \\(w=w_0+tW\\), and a native quadratic \\(P_*\\), with \\(X=P_*(t,y,w)\\), satisfies\n\\[\n\\sup|X|\\le K_0,\\quad\n\\sup|(P_*)_w|\\le K_0B,\\quad\n|(P_*)_w|\\ge B/K_0\\text{ on selected incidences},\\quad\n|(P_*)_{ww}|\\le K_0B^2.\n\\]\nTests are arbitrary quadratics in \\((t,y,w)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Selected measures are\n\\[\nd\\mu_\\gamma=1_{E_\\gamma}\\,d\\nu_\\gamma\\,dt,\n\\qquad\n\\mu=\\sum_\\gamma\\omega_\\gamma\\mu_\\gamma,\n\\qquad\n\\mu(\\mathrm{all})\\ge K_0^{-1}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Temperedness means polynomial bounds in \\(N_0\\) for counts, ranges, coefficients, \\(B^{\\pm1}\\), inverse widths, and coordinate changes; priors have polynomially bounded piecewise constant densities on polynomially many semialgebraic pieces. Predicates use polynomially many bounded-degree conditions in bounded-dimensional tests. Indexed copies of coefficient space are permitted.</p><p class=\"li\">The depth-\\(r\\) observation is\n\\[\np_r=(\\gamma,t,y(t),[w(t)]_{a_r/B}).\n\\]\nIts within-world density uses \\(dt\\,dy\\) and counting measure on the hidden bin.</p><p class=\"li\">A density is typically \\(N_0^{-f}\\) when every fixed logarithmic tolerance fails only on power-small selected mass.</p><p class=\"li\">A datum is known by an observation when it lies in an observation-dependent deterministic subpower list after permitted pruning.</p><p class=\"li\">For fixed \\(S,\\eta&gt;0\\), the cap class \\(\\mathcal C(d)\\), \\(d&lt;1\\), consists of homogeneous problems satisfying\n\\[\nf(L)-f(r)\\le d(L-r)\n\\]\nand the terminal angular ceiling\n\\[\n\\rho(p_L,[V]_{a_u})\\le N_0^{-f(L)-Su+o(1)}\n\\qquad(0&lt;u\\le\\eta L)\n\\]\ntypically.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After a tempered inverse-subpower selection and a subsequence, the observation densities admit homogeneous nondecreasing Lipschitz profiles. Restrictions, subpower refinements, coordinate-grid overlaps, and angular coarsening have the properties established in Lemmas \\(1.1\\)–\\(1.4\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(1.1\\) and \\(1.2\\) justify finite refinements and tempered density selections. Lemma \\(1.3\\) constructs the homogeneous profiles. Lemma \\(1.4\\) supplies the angular estimates at coarsened observations with the required exception bookkeeping.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.3\\), Restriction, refinement, and caps", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\int_{\\{g'<\\epsilon g\\}}g'\\le\\epsilon\\int g. \\) If a discrete refinement has at most \\(D\\) possible labels on retained events, the refined mass at entries of density less than \\(\\epsilon g\\) is at most …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(g\\) is an unnormalized observation density; \\(g'\\) is its density after restriction.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\int_{\\{g'&lt;\\epsilon g\\}}g'\\le\\epsilon\\int g.\n\\]\nIf a discrete refinement has at most \\(D\\) possible labels on retained events, the refined mass at entries of density less than \\(\\epsilon g\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\epsilon D\\int g.\n\\]\nInverse-subpower restrictions and subpower-list refinements preserve typical logarithmic densities. Removed exceptional portions must be excluded before caps are summed against small conditional probabilities.</p>", "proof": "<p>Restriction gives \\(0\\le g'\\le g\\), so integrate \\(g'&lt;\\epsilon g\\) on the indicated set. For refinement, sum the corresponding inequality over at most \\(D\\) labels and integrate. Take \\(\\epsilon=N_0^{-\\tau}\\), absorbing subpower losses into a smaller positive power.</p><p>For the exceptional-set assertion, write the resulting density as \\(g_{\\mathrm{good}}+g_{\\mathrm{bad}}\\). States with \\(g_{\\mathrm{bad}}&gt;g_{\\mathrm{good}}\\) carry mass at most \\(2\\int g_{\\mathrm{bad}}\\); elsewhere the total density is at most twice the good density.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.4\\), Joint base flattening", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For some fixed finite \\(D\\), uniformly redistributing \\((t,y)\\) inside mesh cells of width \\(\\rho\\sim N_0^{-D}\\), while retaining all discrete entries jointly, changes the measure in total variation by \\(O(N_0^{-T})\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix tempered discrete labels and predicates of polynomial total count.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(T&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For some fixed finite \\(D\\), uniformly redistributing \\((t,y)\\) inside mesh cells of width \\(\\rho\\sim N_0^{-D}\\), while retaining all discrete entries jointly, changes the measure in total variation by \\(O(N_0^{-T})\\). A measurable posterior list selector can consequently be replaced, with this success error, by a cell-constant tempered list table.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use coordinates \\((t,y,V)\\) and the remaining trajectory coefficients, extending densities by zero. On a base-coordinate fiber, each polynomial predicate changes only at boundedly many roots; an identically zero restriction has no changes. Polynomially many predicates, polynomial density heights, and polynomial coefficient ranges give\n\\[\n\\|\\tau_h f-f\\|_{L^1}\\le N_0^C|h|.\n\\]\nIntegration in the remaining variables and summation over internal copies preserve this estimate. Within-cell averaging has the same bound by comparing pairs of points in one cell. Choose \\(D&gt;C+T+1\\).</p><p>On the flattened law the posterior distribution of discrete entries is constant within each base cell, for fixed conditioning entries. Choose a maximizing constant list there and transfer its success back by total variation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.5\\), Homogenization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After permitted selection and a subsequence, typical densities have exponents \\(f(r,u)\\) satisfying \\( 0\\le f(r',u)-f(r,u)\\le r'-r, \\) \\( 0\\le f(r,u')-f(r,u)\\le j(u'-u), \\) for \\(r'\\ge r\\), \\(u'\\ge u\\), and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the exact problems of Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Jointly observe \\((p_r,[V]_{a_u})\\) on prescribed compact nonnegative depth ranges.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After permitted selection and a subsequence, typical densities have exponents \\(f(r,u)\\) satisfying\n\\[\n0\\le f(r',u)-f(r,u)\\le r'-r,\n\\]\n\\[\n0\\le f(r,u')-f(r,u)\\le j(u'-u),\n\\]\nfor \\(r'\\ge r\\), \\(u'\\ge u\\), and\n\\[\nf(r,0)=f(r).\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(f(r)\\) is nondecreasing and \\(1\\)-Lipschitz.</p>", "proof": "<p>Test a slowly increasing finite part of a countable dense depth grid. Lemma \\(1.2\\) supplies a common sufficiently fine polynomial base mesh. Polynomial ceilings and entry counts permit deletion of power-small total mass at extremely low densities.</p><p>Quantize logarithmic densities into intervals of width \\(\\epsilon_{N_0}\\to0\\). Choose the number \\(m_{N_0}\\) of tested observations slowly enough that\n\\[\n\\bigl(O(\\epsilon_{N_0}^{-1})\\bigr)^{m_{N_0}}=N_0^{o(1)}.\n\\]\nOne density-type list carries inverse-subpower mass. Restrict to it and use Lemma \\(1.1\\) to control the resulting density loss. Total variation transfers this conclusion to exact-base densities.</p><p>Pass to a subsequence where the tested exponents converge. Hidden refinement has at most \\(K_0N_0^{r'-r}\\) children, and velocity refinement at most \\(K_0N_0^{j(u'-u)}\\). These yield the displayed inequalities. Extend from the dense grid by nesting. Since \\(|V|\\le K_0\\), adjoining a unit velocity bin costs only subpower choices.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.7\\), Angular caps under coarsening", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At the sampled angular entry, \\( \\mathbb P\\{[V]_{a_u}=v\\mid p_b\\}\\le N_0^{-Su+o(1)} \\) typically.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The terminal angular ceiling of \\(\\mathcal C(d)\\) holds.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(0\\le b&lt;L\\), \\(0&lt;u\\le\\eta L\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At the sampled angular entry,\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\{[V]_{a_u}=v\\mid p_b\\}\\le N_0^{-Su+o(1)}\n\\]\ntypically. The assertion is unchanged by normalizing total incidence mass. Good restricted measures may be prepared on slowly increasing finite grids with loss negligible relative to a specified inverse-subpower selection.</p>", "proof": "<p>Remove fine terminal entries where either the joint upper bound or the pure lower bound fails. For a specified angular bin, its good numerator is bounded by \\(N_0^{-Su+O(\\tau)}\\) times the old pure density. Sum numerators and denominators over fine hidden bins inside the coarse bin before division. Apply the omitted-mass comparison and subsequent-restriction estimate of Lemma \\(1.1\\). Sum finitely many power-small exceptions, then increase the grids and decrease tolerances slowly.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition \\(2.16\\), Initial horizon bound. Supporting original labels: Definition \\(2.8\\), Lemmas \\(2.9\\)–\\(2.13\\), Definition \\(2.14\\), Lemma \\(2.15\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every homogeneous exact problem satisfies \\( H(E)\\le L. \\) Chart saturation, normalization, lifting, and prior-mass budgets obey Lemmas \\(2.1\\)–\\(2.5\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a homogeneous exact problem from Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A chart at depth \\(r\\) has a time interval \\(I\\) of length \\(q=N_0^{-h+o(1)}\\), an affine center \\(y_c(t)\\), an invertible spatial matrix \\(D_c\\), and a permitted test \\(P_c\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(J_c=|\\det D_c|\\). On every assigned full trajectory piece,\n\\[\n\\|D_c\\|\\le qK_0,\\qquad\n\\sup_I|D_c^{-1}(y-y_c)|\\le K_0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sup_I|P_c|\\le K_0a_r,\\qquad\n\\sup_I|(P_c)_w|\\le K_0B,\\qquad\n|(P_c)_{ww}|\\le K_0B^2/a_r.\n\\]\nOn selected incidences, \\(|(P_c)_w|\\ge B/K_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Within each world and interval, trajectory assignments \\(A_c\\) are disjoint. Their prior and selected incidence masses are \\(\\nu_c\\) and \\(m_c\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A chart is true if\n\\[\nm_c/q\\ge K_0^{-1}n(r)J_c,\\qquad n(r)=N_0^{-f(r)}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A chart system has inverse-subpower total selected mass.</p><p class=\"li\">A known atlas has all chart-system properties and labels known by \\(p_L\\), but need not be true.</p><p class=\"li\">The minimum horizon \\(H(E)\\) is the infimum of terminal true-system time exponents, allowing subsequences and permitted further selections.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every homogeneous exact problem satisfies\n\\[\nH(E)\\le L.\n\\]\nChart saturation, normalization, lifting, and prior-mass budgets obey Lemmas \\(2.1\\)–\\(2.5\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(q\\sim a_L\\). In each world and \\(q\\)-block, group trajectories by midpoint \\(y\\)-bins of width \\(q\\) and midpoint \\(X\\)-bins of width \\(a_L\\). The joint label is known by \\(p_L\\). Use \\(w-b_G\\) in version \\(1\\), or \\(P_*-b_G\\) in version \\(2\\). Quadratic interpolation gives all terminal whole-block test bounds.</p><p>Translate the spatial bin and divide by \\(Rq\\), with \\(R\\) subpower; normalize block time. Put \\(J_0=(Rq)^j\\). After flattening at \\(\\rho\\sim N_0^{-D}\\), group-cell densities have floor \\(n(L)J_0\\) up to subpower losses.</p><p>For fixed \\(\\xi&gt;0\\), greedily select full-time spatial planks of normalized widths \\(r_i\\ge\\rho\\) when their remaining normalized incidence mass is at least\n\\[\nN_0^{-\\xi}n(L)J_0\\prod_i r_i.\n\\]\nAssign all eligible remaining trajectories and remove them. Each removal is at least reciprocal polynomial, so the process is tempered and finite.</p><p>If less than half the initial mass is covered, remove bad cells and short residual durations. Some residual group of original mass \\(m_G\\) retains mass at least \\(m_G/K_0\\), while its visited-cell count is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{m_G}{N_0^{-\\xi/4}n(L)J_0\\rho^{j+1}}.\n\\]\nEach used trace has inverse-subpower marked duration. For \\(j=1\\), add an independent static coordinate. Lemma \\(2.6\\) then supplies a plank of widths \\(u,v\\) with\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{used trajectory weight}}{uv}\n\\gtrsim_\\epsilon\nK_0^{-1}N_0^{-\\xi/4}D_m^{-\\epsilon}n(L)J_0.\n\\]\nIn the padded case, projection to the true coordinate preserves the density conclusion: a rectangle of area comparable to \\(uv\\) and projected width \\(W\\) has maximal vertical section at most \\(Cuv/W\\).</p><p>Every counted trace has inverse-subpower labeled duration. Choose \\(D\\epsilon&lt;\\xi/4\\); the resulting incidence mass exceeds the greedy threshold, a contradiction. Hence at least half the mass is covered. Let \\(\\xi\\downarrow0\\) slowly. The resulting true system has time exponent \\(L\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.9\\), Quadratic sublevel sets", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\{w:|Q(w)|\\le\\epsilon,\\ |Q'(w)|\\ge b\\} \\) has total length at most \\(4\\epsilon/b\\) and meets at most \\( C\\left(1+\\frac{\\epsilon}{b\\delta}\\right) \\) bins of width \\(\\delta\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(Q\\) is a real polynomial of degree at most \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\epsilon,b,\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\{w:|Q(w)|\\le\\epsilon,\\ |Q'(w)|\\ge b\\}\n\\]\nhas total length at most \\(4\\epsilon/b\\) and meets at most\n\\[\nC\\left(1+\\frac{\\epsilon}{b\\delta}\\right)\n\\]\nbins of width \\(\\delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The condition \\(|Q'|\\ge b\\) has at most two monotonicity intervals. On either, the inverse derivative estimate gives length at most \\(2\\epsilon/b\\). An interval meets at most two more grid bins than its length divided by \\(\\delta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.10\\), Saturation and label knowledge", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After an inverse-subpower restriction, \\( m_c/q\\sim_{\\log}\\nu_c\\sim_{\\log}n(r)J_c \\) for every used chart, and its label is known by \\(p_r\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A true chart system is given.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After an inverse-subpower restriction,\n\\[\nm_c/q\\sim_{\\log}\\nu_c\\sim_{\\log}n(r)J_c\n\\]\nfor every used chart, and its label is known by \\(p_r\\). The true floor persists.</p>", "proof": "<p>The assignment budget is\n\\[\nm_c\\le q\\nu_c,\\qquad\n\\sum_c\\omega_{\\gamma(c)}q\\nu_c\\le1.\n\\]\nDeleting charts with \\(q\\nu_c&gt;Hm_c\\) costs at most \\(H^{-1}\\). Remove exceptional \\(p_r\\)-entries above the density cap. Lemma \\(2.1\\) allows only subpower hidden bins at each chart base; the chart base volume is at most \\(K_0qJ_c\\). Thus its good mass is at most \\(K_0n(r)qJ_c\\). Discard charts whose full mass exceeds twice this bound, charging their loss to the omitted exceptional mass.</p><p>For label knowledge, let \\(T(p_r)\\) count charts whose geometric, value, and derivative tests cross an observation. Then\n\\[\n\\int T\\,d\\mu_{\\mathrm{cap}}\n\\le K_0\\sum_c\\omega_{\\gamma(c)}n(r)qJ_c\n\\le K_0\\sum_c\\omega_{\\gamma(c)}m_c.\n\\]\nMarkov’s inequality gives subpower lists. Implement them by Lemma \\(1.2\\), and discard charts losing more than half their selected mass.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.11\\), Restoring current floors and charging witnesses", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Deleting entries with \\( \\widetilde m_{c,e}<\\epsilon q\\nu_c/D \\) loses at most \\(\\epsilon\\) total weighted mass.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A chart layer satisfies the assignment budget.</p><p class=\"li\">Each chart has at most \\(D\\le K_0\\) appended entries, of current masses \\(\\widetilde m_{c,e}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Deleting entries with\n\\[\n\\widetilde m_{c,e}&lt;\\epsilon q\\nu_c/D\n\\]\nloses at most \\(\\epsilon\\) total weighted mass. Floors for finitely many layers can be imposed simultaneously with the sum of their allocated losses. For time subintervals, replace \\(q\\nu_c\\) by \\(|I'|\\nu_c\\).</p><p>Disjoint frozen witness submeasures of a fixed measure \\(\\lambda\\), supported in \\(U\\), have total mass at most \\(\\lambda(U)\\); multiplicity \\(D\\) gives \\(D\\lambda(U)\\). The cap must concern that same frozen measure.</p>", "proof": "<p>Sum the thresholds over entries and charts. For simultaneous layers, repeatedly remove every positive entry below its fixed threshold, reconsidering all layers after each deletion. Every deleted entry becomes permanently empty and is charged only once. The process terminates because there are finitely many entries. Time-subinterval budgets sum to \\(q\\nu_c\\). The witness assertion follows by summing submeasures, and does not provide a floor for an arbitrary later restriction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.12\\), Fullness of the largest spatial scale", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On an inverse-subpower selection, \\( \\|D_c\\|\\sim_{\\log}q. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The problem belongs to a cap class.</p><p class=\"li\">A chart system, true or not, has labels known by \\(p_L\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On an inverse-subpower selection,\n\\[\n\\|D_c\\|\\sim_{\\log}q.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(\\|D_c\\|/q\\le N_0^{-\\alpha}\\) on inverse-subpower mass, endpoint position bounds confine velocity, for each chart, to a ball of radius \\(K_0N_0^{-\\alpha}\\). Choose \\(0&lt;u&lt;\\alpha/2\\), \\(u\\le\\eta L\\). Terminal label knowledge supplies only subpower such angular bins per observation. Their good numerators have total at most \\(N_0^{-Su+o(1)}\\) times the old pure denominator. Integration makes the alleged mass power-small, a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.13\\), Chart normalization and lifting", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Normalization produces a homogeneous exact problem of length \\(L'=L-r\\), with \\( n'(L')\\sim_{\\log}n(L)\\frac{J_c}{\\nu_c}, \\qquad n'(s)\\le N_0^{o(1)}n(r+s)\\frac{J_c}{\\nu_c}. \\) The intermediate inequality is an …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A homogeneous problem of length \\(L\\) has a terminal-known atlas at depth \\(0\\le r&lt;L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Homogenize chart prior masses and Jacobians after discarding extremely light charts.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Normalization produces a homogeneous exact problem of length \\(L'=L-r\\), with\n\\[\nn'(L')\\sim_{\\log}n(L)\\frac{J_c}{\\nu_c},\n\\qquad\nn'(s)\\le N_0^{o(1)}n(r+s)\\frac{J_c}{\\nu_c}.\n\\]\nThe intermediate inequality is an equivalence if the parent label is known by \\(p_r\\). The same \\(\\mathcal C(d)\\), \\(S\\), and \\(\\eta\\) are preserved.</p><p>Terminal true systems lift. Intermediate true systems lift when the intermediate density equivalence holds. In version \\(2\\), the narrowness exponent\n\\[\n\\log_{N_0}\\frac{q^2}{J_c}\n\\]\nadds under composition.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nZ=\\sum_c\\omega_{\\gamma(c)}q\\nu_c,\\qquad\n\\omega_c'=\\omega_{\\gamma(c)}q\\nu_c/Z.\n\\]\nUse prior \\(\\nu_{\\gamma(c)}(\\cdot\\mid A_c)\\) and coordinates\n\\[\nt=t_c+q\\tau,\\qquad y=y_c(t)+D_cz.\n\\]\nIn version \\(1\\), set \\(w'=(w-F(t,y))/a_r\\). In version \\(2\\), retain \\(w\\), use \\(P_*'=P_c/a_r\\), and \\(B'=B/a_r\\). The coefficient changes have controlled constant Jacobians and preserve temperedness.</p><p>For transported hidden bins, the exact within-world density factor is\n\\[\n\\rho_c'=\\frac{qJ_c}{q\\nu_c}\\rho_c=\\frac{J_c}{\\nu_c}\\rho_c.\n\\]\nWorld weights and \\(Z\\) do not enter this factor. Terminal label knowledge, bounded grid overlap, and Lemma \\(1.1\\) give terminal equivalence and intermediate upper bounds. Own-depth label knowledge gives intermediate equivalence. New angular cells map into subpowerly many old angular cells, so summing good numerators preserves the angular cap. The endpoint profile cap follows from\n\\[\n\\log_{N_0}\\frac{n'(s)}{n'(L')}\n\\le f(L)-f(r+s)+o(1).\n\\]</p><p>A child with relative parameters \\(q_d,J_d,m_d'\\) lifts with\n\\[\nq_{cd}=qq_d,\\quad J_{cd}=J_cJ_d,\\quad\nm_{cd}=q\\nu_cm_d',\\quad\n\\frac{m_{cd}}{q_{cd}}=\\nu_c\\frac{m_d'}{q_d}.\n\\]\nThese formulas give the true floors. Tests lift by multiplication by \\(a_r\\), preserving quadratic degree and derivative units. Finally,\n\\[\n\\frac{q_{cd}^2}{J_{cd}}=\\frac{q^2}{J_c}\\frac{q_d^2}{J_d}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.6", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.15\\), Weighted full-time plank estimate; external input from OpenAI \\([24,\\text{ Lemma }2.3]\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(\\epsilon>0\\), suitable \\(\\eta_\\epsilon>0\\), \\(C_\\epsilon<\\infty\\) give \\( \\sum_i\\omega_i\\#J_i \\le C_\\epsilon AD^\\epsilon\\#E_{\\mathrm{vis}}. \\) Continuous weighted laws follow by fine coefficient rounding.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M_i(t)=b_i+tu_i\\in\\mathbb R^2\\), \\(0\\le t\\le1\\), are finitely many indexed bounded traces with positive weights \\(\\omega_i\\); repeated traces are permitted.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(D\\ge1\\), \\(\\rho=D^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Every full-time plank with arbitrary orthonormal spatial frame, affine center, and half-widths \\(a\\rho,b\\rho\\), \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le D\\), satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{i:M_i\\subset\\mathcal P}\\omega_i\\le Aab.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Each trace has at least \\(D^{1-\\eta_\\epsilon}\\) marked time bins of length \\(\\rho\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(E_{\\mathrm{vis}}\\) is the union of cells visited at marked-bin centers, allowing fixed enlargements.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), suitable \\(\\eta_\\epsilon&gt;0\\), \\(C_\\epsilon&lt;\\infty\\) give\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\omega_i\\#J_i\n\\le C_\\epsilon AD^\\epsilon\\#E_{\\mathrm{vis}}.\n\\]\nContinuous weighted laws follow by fine coefficient rounding. Subpower-bounded charts and inverse-subpower marked durations incur only subpower losses after a slow \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\) diagonal.</p>", "proof": "<p>The paper invokes the cited weighted full-trace estimate. For continuous laws, group sufficiently finely rounded coefficients and identical finite mark sets, assigning each group its prior mass. Fixed enlargements recover the original traces. A common subpower spatial rescaling puts subpower-bounded charts into a fixed bounded chart. The long-mark condition then holds for each fixed \\(\\epsilon\\), eventually; only afterward let \\(\\epsilon\\) decrease slowly.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Theorem \\(2.17\\), Reduction to a bad weighted problem", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are \\(0<d<1\\), \\(S,\\eta>0\\), and a tempered homogeneous version-\\(2\\) problem \\(E\\in\\mathcal C(d)\\) such that \\( H(E)>0. \\) The construction requires neither measurability of \\(K\\) nor a measurable witnessing line …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^4\\) contains a unit segment in every direction.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\dim_HK&lt;4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use exact problems, cap classes, charts, and horizons from Propositions \\(1\\) and \\(2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(0&lt;d&lt;1\\), \\(S,\\eta&gt;0\\), and a tempered homogeneous version-\\(2\\) problem \\(E\\in\\mathcal C(d)\\) such that\n\\[\nH(E)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe construction requires neither measurability of \\(K\\) nor a measurable witnessing line family.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(3.1\\) supplies the exact smoothed problem without measurable witness selection. Lemma \\(3.2\\) produces a cap-class endpoint and angular scale. Lemma \\(3.3\\) applies on every allowed subsequence and further selection, proving\n\\[\nH(E)\\ge\\sigma/6&gt;0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "finite outer-measure selection and smoothing in Theorem \\(2.17\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Along scales \\(\\delta=2^{-k}\\downarrow0\\), there are \\(M\\) bounded graph lines \\(x(t)=b+ts\\), \\(t\\in[0,1]\\), with \\(\\delta\\)-separated slopes and \\( ck^{-2}\\delta^{-3}\\le M\\le C\\delta^{-3}, \\) each spending at least …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(0&lt;\\sigma&lt;1\\) with \\(\\dim_HK&lt;4-\\sigma\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Along scales \\(\\delta=2^{-k}\\downarrow0\\), there are \\(M\\) bounded graph lines \\(x(t)=b+ts\\), \\(t\\in[0,1]\\), with \\(\\delta\\)-separated slopes and\n\\[\nck^{-2}\\delta^{-3}\\le M\\le C\\delta^{-3},\n\\]\neach spending at least \\(ck^{-2}\\) time in a union of at most \\(C\\delta^{-(4-\\sigma)}\\) radius-\\(\\delta\\) balls.</p><p>Independent \\(\\delta\\)-scale smoothing of all intercept and slope coordinates gives a tempered problem with inverse-subpower selected mass, full slope density at most \\(K_0\\), and conditional \\(y\\)-intercept density at most \\(C\\delta^{-2}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a bounded open slope chart. Countably many rational time intervals and bounded integer intercept boxes cover all possible witnesses; outer subadditivity gives one common interval and box with eligible slope set of positive outer measure. Rescale its interval to \\([0,1]\\).</p><p>Take ball covers with \\(\\sum_i r_i^{4-\\sigma}\\le1\\). Let \\(U_k\\) be the union of balls with \\(2^{-k-1}&lt;r_i\\le2^{-k}\\). Every eligible segment spends at least \\(ck^{-2}\\) time in some \\(U_k\\). A second outer-subadditivity argument supplies such a \\(k\\) whose eligible slopes have outer measure at least \\(c'ak^{-2}\\). A maximal finite \\(\\delta\\)-separated subset has the stated size. Choose one witness for each of these finitely many slopes.</p><p>Use their uniform mixture and perturb all six coefficients independently in small \\(\\delta\\)-boxes. Select incidences in fixed enlarged covering balls. Covered times survive every perturbation. Slope separation bounds the smoothed full-slope density by \\(Ck^2\\); independent intercept smoothing gives the conditional \\(y\\)-density bound. Finite indexed copies and polynomially many ball predicates establish temperedness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "density bounds and endpoint selection in Theorem \\(2.17\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( g(1,0)\\ge\\sigma,\\qquad g(0,0)\\le0,\\qquad g(r,u)\\le r+2-2u. \\) For sufficiently small \\(R,S>0\\), maximizing \\( g(r,u)-Rr+Su \\) gives \\(r_0>0\\), \\(u_0<4\\), \\(g(r_0,u_0)\\ge\\sigma/2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Set \\(N_0=\\delta^{-1}\\), initially \\(L=1\\), \\(P_*=w\\), \\(B=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Homogenize \\((p_r,[V]_{a_u})\\), \\(0\\le r\\le1\\), \\(0\\le u\\le4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nn(r,u)=N_0^{-f(r,u)},\\qquad\nm(r,u)=N_0^rn(r,u),\\qquad\ng(r,u)=r-f(r,u).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\ng(1,0)\\ge\\sigma,\\qquad g(0,0)\\le0,\\qquad\ng(r,u)\\le r+2-2u.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small \\(R,S&gt;0\\), maximizing\n\\[\ng(r,u)-Rr+Su\n\\]\ngives \\(r_0&gt;0\\), \\(u_0&lt;4\\), \\(g(r_0,u_0)\\ge\\sigma/2\\). A full-time crop and normalization in \\((y_0,V)\\) at width \\(a_{u_0}\\) yields a problem of length \\(r_0\\) in \\(\\mathcal C(1-R)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At depth \\(1\\), covering balls occupy observation-space measure at most \\(C\\delta^{\\sigma-1}\\), so dense mass forces \\(n(1,0)\\ge\\delta^{1-\\sigma}/K_0\\). The full-slope ceiling and conditional \\(y\\)-density imply joint ceiling \\(K_0N_0^{2-2u}\\), proving the third inequality.</p><p>For the second, full-time projected \\(y\\)-planks satisfy\n\\[\n\\nu\\{l:y_l([0,1])\\subset\\mathcal P\\}\\le K_0ab\\rho^2,\n\\]\nbecause endpoint containment confines velocity to a rectangle of that area. If \\(n(0,0)\\) grew by a fixed power \\(N_0^\\alpha\\), flattening and long-duration pruning would give at most \\(K_0N_0^{-\\alpha}\\rho^{-3}\\) visited cells and weighted average multiplicity at least \\(K_0^{-1}N_0^\\alpha\\rho^2\\). Lemma \\(2.6\\) bounds it by \\(C_\\epsilon K_0D_m^\\epsilon\\rho^2\\); choose \\(D\\epsilon&lt;\\alpha/2\\).</p><p>Choose \\(R+4S&lt;\\sigma/2\\), \\(R,S&lt;1/10\\). The maximizer is excluded from \\(r=0\\) and \\(u=4\\) by the displayed bounds. Maximality gives\n\\[\nf(r_0,u_0)-f(s,u_0)\\le(1-R)(r_0-s),\n\\]\n\\[\nf(r_0,u_0+u)-f(r_0,u_0)\\ge Su.\n\\]\nMake \\((y_0,V)\\)-crops \\(B\\) into conditional worlds and divide \\(y-y_B(t)\\) by \\(a_{u_0}\\). The crop label has bounded ambiguity given exact base and the old velocity bin. Thus\n\\[\nn_{\\mathrm{new}}(s)\\sim_{\\log}\nn(s,u_0)\\frac{a_{u_0}^2}{\\nu(B)}.\n\\]\nThe two maximality inequalities are precisely the endpoint and angular caps; take, for example,\n\\(\\eta=(4-u_0)/(2r_0)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "slope-volume bound, equations \\((2.33)\\)–\\((2.39)\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\frac{m_{\\mathrm{old},c}}q \\ge K_0^{-1}m(r_0,u_0)a_{r_0}J_{\\mathrm{phys}}, \\qquad |\\Sigma|\\le K_0\\frac{a_{r_0}J_{\\mathrm{phys}}}{q^3}. \\) Consequently every true terminal system has time exponent at least …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider any true terminal chart in the cropped problem.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q\\) be its time length and \\(J_{\\mathrm{phys}}=a_{u_0}^2J_{\\mathrm{new}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Sigma\\) consist of bounded slopes admitting a bounded intercept satisfying the chart’s whole-block spatial and small-value bounds, derivative upper bound, and a derivative of magnitude at least \\(K_0^{-1}\\) at some time.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac{m_{\\mathrm{old},c}}q\n\\ge K_0^{-1}m(r_0,u_0)a_{r_0}J_{\\mathrm{phys}},\n\\qquad\n|\\Sigma|\\le K_0\\frac{a_{r_0}J_{\\mathrm{phys}}}{q^3}.\n\\]\nConsequently every true terminal system has time exponent at least \\(\\sigma/6\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Multiply the cropped true floor by \\(\\nu(B)\\); the crop normalization cancels.</p><p>The geometric definition of \\(\\Sigma\\) has fixed semialgebraic format. Quantifier elimination, semialgebraic choice, and \\(C^1\\) cell decomposition provide an eligible intercept \\(b(s)\\), with bounded-format graph and full-dimensional \\(C^1\\) pieces covering \\(\\Sigma\\) up to a null set. For\n\\[\nF_t(s)=b(s)+ts,\n\\qquad\n\\det DF_t(s)=\\det(Db(s)+tI),\n\\]\nthe determinant is a monic cubic in \\(t\\). Its regular-fiber multiplicity is uniformly bounded by semialgebraic fiber complexity.</p><p>For each \\(s\\), exclude radius-\\(cq\\) intervals about the real parts of its three complex roots. Outside them,\n\\[\n|\\det DF_t(s)|\\ge c^3q^3.\n\\]\nThe affine function \\(P_w(t,b(s)+ts)\\), whose maximum is at least \\(K_0^{-1}\\), is below \\(\\epsilon/K_0\\) on at most \\(4\\epsilon q\\) time. Choose \\(c,\\epsilon\\) so the two excluded portions total at most \\(q/2\\).</p><p>The area formula and bounded regular multiplicity give\n\\[\nq^4|\\Sigma|\n\\lesssim\\int_I|F_t(\\Sigma_t)|\\,dt\n\\le K_0qJ_{\\mathrm{phys}}a_{r_0}.\n\\]\nThe last bound uses Lemma \\(2.1\\) in \\(w\\) at fixed \\((t,y)\\).</p><p>The original slope-density ceiling bounds eligible prior mass by \\(K_0|\\Sigma|\\), whereas it is at least \\(m_{\\mathrm{old},c}/q\\). Cancel \\(a_{r_0}J_{\\mathrm{phys}}\\):\n\\[\nm(r_0,u_0)\\le K_0q^{-3}.\n\\]\nSince \\(m(r_0,u_0)\\ge N_0^{\\sigma/2}\\), \\(q=N_0^{-h+o(1)}\\) forces \\(h\\ge\\sigma/6\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Corollary \\(3.4\\), Local matching of scalar graphs. Supporting original labels: Lemmas \\(3.1\\), \\(3.2\\), \\(3.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\(\\epsilon_*>0\\) such that, for large \\(R\\), \\(0<\\epsilon\\le\\epsilon_*\\), and \\(d\\le d_0\\), two lists of real-on-real algebraic sheets cannot simultaneously have:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix base dimension, algebraic degrees, real comparison balls with fixed holomorphic margins, and \\(d_0&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An algebraic sheet of degree at most \\(D\\) is holomorphic and satisfies \\(P(z,f(z))=0\\) for a nonzero polynomial of total degree at most \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M,\\Delta&gt;0\\), \\(R=M/\\Delta\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(\\epsilon_*&gt;0\\) such that, for large \\(R\\), \\(0&lt;\\epsilon\\le\\epsilon_*\\), and \\(d\\le d_0\\), two lists of real-on-real algebraic sheets cannot simultaneously have:\n\\[\n\\#\\mathcal F_\\kappa\\le R^{d+\\epsilon},\n\\]\neach sheet within \\(MR^\\epsilon\\) in norm of one common algebraic reference;\na subprobability pair/base law of mass at least \\(R^{-\\epsilon}\\), whose index-base marginals are bounded by \\(R^\\epsilon\\pi_{\\kappa,i}\\,dz\\), with probability weights \\(\\pi_{\\kappa,i}\\); and, on its events,\n\\[\n|f_1(z)-f_2(z)|\\le\\Delta R^\\epsilon,\n\\qquad\n\\|f_1-f_2\\|\\ge MR^{-\\epsilon}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Subtract one list member and divide by \\(M\\). Lemma \\(4.1\\) bounds all needed derivatives by \\(R^{C\\epsilon}\\). The pairwise norm gap supplies a bounded-order jet lower bound at each event. The zeroth-order value is too small to supply it. A finite set of directions detects a positive-order derivative.</p><p>Choose one direction, order, and fixed interior product box carrying a fixed fraction of mass. Fubini supplies a fiber interval with inverse-small-power mass; its two index-time marginals retain the required ceilings. Normalize and rescale that interval. Lemma \\(4.3\\), with \\(N=R\\) and sufficiently small \\(\\epsilon_*\\), gives a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.1\\), Algebraic analytic norm comparison", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed derivative order \\(q\\), \\( \\max_{|\\alpha|\\le q}\\|\\partial^\\alpha f\\|_K\\le C_q, \\qquad \\max_{|\\alpha|\\le D}|\\partial^\\alpha f(z)|\\ge c_K>0 \\quad(z\\in K). \\) For another fixed real ball \\(B_1\\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb C^n\\) is a fixed ball or polydisc.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B\\subset\\Omega\\cap\\mathbb R^n\\) is a fixed closed ball with nonempty interior.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\Subset\\Omega\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(f\\) is an algebraic sheet of degree at most \\(D\\), normalized by \\(\\|f\\|_B=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed derivative order \\(q\\),\n\\[\n\\max_{|\\alpha|\\le q}\\|\\partial^\\alpha f\\|_K\\le C_q,\n\\qquad\n\\max_{|\\alpha|\\le D}|\\partial^\\alpha f(z)|\\ge c_K&gt;0\n\\quad(z\\in K).\n\\]\nFor another fixed real ball \\(B_1\\) and measurable \\(E\\subset B\\), \\(\\theta=|E|/|B|&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_{B_1}+\\max_{|\\alpha|\\le q}\\|\\partial^\\alpha f\\|_{B_1}\n\\le C_q\\theta^{-C}\\|f\\|_E.\n\\]\nThe assertions are homogeneous, respect affine rescaling and parallel real slices, and imply finite-jet norm control.</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize the coefficient vectors of polynomial relations and pass to a limiting nonzero relation \\(P\\). It must involve the function variable: otherwise boundedness on \\(B\\) would make a nonzero base polynomial vanish on its real interior.</p><p>Away from the zero set of the leading coefficient of \\(P\\), a large root-free circle separates bounded roots from escaping roots. The chosen branches are bounded near one point of \\(B\\), hence along compact paths in the connected complement of that zero set. Cross the zero set by the maximum principle on small complex-line disks whose boundary circles avoid it. This proves local uniform boundedness.</p><p>Cauchy estimates give compactness with derivatives. Any limiting normalized sheet is nonzero. Remove the maximal power of the function variable from its relation; the remaining constant coefficient is a nonzero polynomial of degree at most \\(D\\). The vanishing order of the sheet is therefore at most \\(D\\). Compactness gives both uniform jet bounds.</p><p>A finite collection of real directions detects a derivative of order at most \\(D\\) of the real or imaginary part. The bound persists on fixed-radius neighborhoods. Divided differences on line slices yield\n\\[\n|\\{x\\in B_1:|f(x)|\\le u\\|f\\|_{B_1}\\}|\n\\le Cu^c|B_1|.\n\\]\nApply this to \\(E\\), compare fixed-ball norms, and use Cauchy estimates. The same compactness argument gives finite-jet control.</p><p>Fixed sums, differences, polynomial evaluations, affine restrictions, and the fixed derivatives of the simple linear or quadratic sheets used in the paper retain bounded-degree relations by elimination.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.2\\), Polynomial cost of continuation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On endpoint comparison balls of radius \\(c/A\\), continued-function norms compare in both directions with loss \\(A^C\\), independent of equation coefficients.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix dimensions, equation degrees, number of sheets, and number of pieces of a broken straight path in a bounded complex region.</p><p class=\"li\">Sheets are affine functions, linear roots, or simple quadratic roots with constant quadratic coefficient; fixed polynomial evaluations and differences are permitted.</p><p class=\"li\">Necessary linear coefficients and discriminants are nonzero throughout an \\(A^{-1}\\)-neighborhood of the path, \\(A\\ge2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On endpoint comparison balls of radius \\(c/A\\), continued-function norms compare in both directions with loss \\(A^C\\), independent of equation coefficients. Fixed derivatives and sufficiently long endpoint jets satisfy corresponding bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(A\\), cover the path by \\(O(A)\\) overlapping balls. Lemma \\(4.1\\) gives a finite coefficient-independent bound \\(C^{O(A)}\\) for endpoint norm ratios.</p><p>The best bound has a fixed-format semialgebraic description. Split complex parameters into real and imaginary parts. Nonvanishing on the tube is expressed by real quantification. For quadratic roots, choose a generic ray from zero in the discriminant plane. Along each straight path piece, bounded-degree polynomial argument variation gives boundedly many transversal ray crossings; their parity specifies continuation of the square-root sign. Endpoint norms are encoded by appending a straight segment to a variable endpoint-ball point.</p><p>Quantifier elimination makes the supremal ratio a one-variable semialgebraic function of \\(A\\). It is finite and locally bounded by the first argument, hence has polynomial growth. Reverse the path for the reverse bound. Lemma \\(4.1\\), including radius scaling, supplies derivatives and jets.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.3\\), Two-color matching", "depth": 1, "snippet": "It is impossible that every event satisfies \\( |f_{1,i_1}(t)-f_{2,i_2}(t)|\\le KN^{-1}, \\) \\( \\max_{1\\le j\\le D}|f_{1,i_1}^{(j)}(t)-f_{2,i_2}^{(j)}(t)|\\ge K^{-1}. \\) The impossibility remains uniform for sufficiently …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(D\\ge1\\), an integer \\(J&gt;D\\), and \\(d_0&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(N\\to\\infty\\), each of two indexed lists contains at most \\(N^{d+o(1)}\\) real \\(C^{J+1}\\) functions on a unit interval, \\(d\\le d_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All derivatives through order \\(J+1\\) are bounded by \\(K=N^{o(1)}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A probability event law on \\((i_1,i_2,t)\\) has each color marginal bounded by\n\\[\nK\\pi_{\\kappa,i}\\,dt,\\qquad \\sum_i\\pi_{\\kappa,i}=1.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>It is impossible that every event satisfies\n\\[\n|f_{1,i_1}(t)-f_{2,i_2}(t)|\\le KN^{-1},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\max_{1\\le j\\le D}|f_{1,i_1}^{(j)}(t)-f_{2,i_2}^{(j)}(t)|\\ge K^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe impossibility remains uniform for sufficiently small fixed-power losses in place of the displayed subpower losses.</p>", "proof": "<p>Pigeonhole derivative-gap exponents \\(\\beta_j\\), cutting off sufficiently small gaps. Then\n\\[\n\\beta_0\\ge1,\\qquad \\beta_j\\ge0,\\qquad\n\\beta_j=0\\text{ for some }1\\le j\\le D.\n\\]\nFor \\(0\\le a\\le1\\), define\n\\[\nr(a)=\\max_{1\\le j\\le J}\\frac{a-\\beta_j}{j},\n\\qquad\nl_j(a)=\\max_{0\\le x\\le a}\\{x-jr(x)\\}.\n\\]\nThese satisfy\n\\[\nl_0=a,\\quad0\\le l_j\\le\\beta_j,\\quad\nl_j-l_{j+i}\\le ir,\\quad\n(J+1-j)r(a)&gt;l_j(a)\\quad(a&gt;0).\n\\]\nOn an active \\(m\\)-piece,\n\\[\nr(a)=\\frac{a-\\beta_m}{m},\\qquad\nl_j(a)=a-jr(a)\\ (j\\le m),\\qquad\nl_j(a)&gt;a-jr(a)\\ (j&gt;m).\n\\]</p><p>Trim the finitely many active pieces and fix a small grid step \\(b\\), with \\(C&gt;5J\\), before constructing states. The state \\(F_a\\) records the nested time interval \\(I_a\\) of length \\(N^{-r(a)}\\), both arms’ center jets through order \\(J\\) at widths \\(N^{-l_j(a)}\\), and preceding states. Taylor translation is controlled by\n\\[\nK\\sum_{i=0}^{J-j}N^{-l_{j+i}(a)-ir(a)}\n+KN^{-(J+1-j)r(a)}\n\\le KN^{-l_j(a)}.\n\\]\nThe two arms’ bins are subpower neighbors. Hence\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{supp}F_1|\\le N^{d+r(1)+o(1)}.\n\\]</p><p>Select atom types whose preselection masses are \\(N^{-u_a+o(1)}\\). Then \\(u_a\\) is nondecreasing,\n\\[\nu_1\\le d+r(1),\n\\]\nand each retained parent has at most \\(N^{u_c-u_a+o(1)}\\) retained descendants at level \\(c&gt;a\\). This count uses preselection masses, not later conditional floors.</p><p>On an active piece, let the palette record the two \\(m\\)-th derivatives at the first interval center, at width \\(N^{-\\beta_m-D_1b}\\), \\(D_1&gt;5\\). Its initial conditional entropy is at most \\(2D_1b\\log N+O(1)\\). Chain rule and the bounded negative part of log likelihood show that, except on a proportion tending to zero of grid steps,\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(Z=z\\mid F_{a+Cb})\n\\le N^{b^{5/4}}\\mathbb P(Z=z\\mid F_a)\n\\]\noutside probability tending to zero with \\(b\\).</p><p>At a good step, make \\(m+1\\) traversals. Refresh an event conditional only on its finer state; then select one color and resample an endpoint conditional only on that arm’s index and \\(I_a\\). Before killing failures, event marginals are bounded by \\(2^p\\) times the selected event law. Coarse states remain in a subpower neighbor set of the starting state.</p><p>Subtract a starting Taylor polynomial and set\n\\[\ns=(t-t_a)N^{r(a)},\\qquad\nY_j=N^{l_j(a)}\\partial_t^j(\\varphi-T),\\quad j&lt;m.\n\\]\nThe dynamics are\n\\[\nY_j'=Y_{j+1}\\quad(j&lt;m-1),\\qquad\nY_{m-1}'=U+O(N^{-2b}).\n\\]\nThe palette and preceding higher jets determine \\(U\\) to the needed precision. Consecutive controls can be chosen \\(K^{-1}\\)-separated. Fine-state matching makes every low-jet switch error \\(O(N^{-2b})\\).</p><p>Kill failed likelihood tests and color-index/interval pairs below \\(\\pi_{\\kappa,i}|I_a|/\\log N\\). These losses are small by marginal domination. On surviving pairs, endpoint-time density is at most \\(K/|I_a|\\); require the internal times to be pairwise \\(K^{-1}\\)-separated in normalized units.</p><p>For each exact start, summing coarse palette pushforwards produces a control measure \\(\\Lambda\\), \\(\\Lambda(\\mathbb R)\\le K\\), such that, conditional on the entire past, the unnormalized successful step kernel obeys\n\\[\nQ_{\\mathcal H_p}(dU\\,ds)\n\\le KN^{b^{5/4}}\\Lambda(dU)\\,ds.\n\\]\nIterate this inequality backward without normalizing successful kernels or conditioning on future survival.</p><p>For fixed controls and final time \\(s\\), the endpoint control contribution is\n\\[\n\\frac1{(m-j)!}\\left[\nU_1(s-s_0)^{m-j}\n+\\sum_{p=1}^m(U_{p+1}-U_p)(s-s_p)^{m-j}\n\\right].\n\\]\nThe endpoint-map Jacobian equals\n\\[\n\\frac{\\prod_{p=1}^m|U_{p+1}-U_p|\n\\prod_{p&lt;q}|s_p-s_q|}\n{\\prod_{k=0}^{m-1}k!},\n\\]\nand is at least \\(K^{-1}\\) on retained paths. Regular fibers have bounded multiplicity.</p><p>One child state at \\(a+b\\) has endpoint target volume at most\n\\[\nKN^{-b(1/m+(m+1)/2)}.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(N^{u_{a+b}-u_a+o(1)}\\) possible targets. Change of variables and kernel domination therefore bound success by\n\\[\nN^{u_{a+b}-u_a-b(1/m+(m+1)/2)+(m+1)b^{5/4}+o(1)}.\n\\]\nThe positive averaged success forces\n\\[\nu_{a+b}-u_a\\ge b(1+1/m-o_b(1)).\n\\]\nFirst take the fixed-grid \\(N\\)-limit, then \\(b\\downarrow0\\), then remove trims. Summation gives\n\\[\nu_1\\ge1+r(1),\n\\]\ncontradicting \\(u_1\\le d+r(1)\\). Counterexamples with fixed-power tolerances tending to zero would give the excluded subpower sequence, proving uniformity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma \\(3.5\\), Terminal time extension", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are uniform constants \\(C<\\infty\\), \\(c>0\\) such that, for \\( 0<e<\\min(h,cL), \\) a retained subsequence admits a terminal-known atlas of time exponent \\(h-e\\), fitting at depth \\(L'\\), where …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d_0&lt;1\\) and angular parameters \\(S,\\eta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An exact problem \\(E\\in\\mathcal C(d)\\), \\(d\\le d_0\\), has length \\(L\\) and a true terminal system of time exponent \\(h&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are uniform constants \\(C&lt;\\infty\\), \\(c&gt;0\\) such that, for\n\\[\n0&lt;e&lt;\\min(h,cL),\n\\]\na retained subsequence admits a terminal-known atlas of time exponent \\(h-e\\), fitting at depth \\(L'\\), where\n\\[\n0&lt;L'&lt;L,\\qquad L-L'\\le Ce.\n\\]\nAssignments, tests, and selected incidences are tempered in the original exact sequence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(5.1\\)–\\(5.3\\) produce blocks of exponent \\(h-e\\) and thickness \\(\\delta N^{Ce}\\). Choose \\(c&gt;0\\) small enough that \\(L-Ce&gt;0\\) and \\(Re\\le\\eta L\\). The resulting known atlas has depth \\(L'\\) with \\(L-L'\\le Ce\\). All losses and output lists are in the original exact sequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "comparison of old terminal equations", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On new blocks of length \\(q_1\\sim N^eq_0\\), there is an inverse-subpower paired incidence law, a coarse moving frame \\(D\\), and simple local scalar graphs from both old labels whose discrepancies on their common …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Set \\(N=N_0\\), \\(\\delta=N^{-L}\\), and normalize the hidden derivative unit by \\(w\\mapsto Bw\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Prepare the old true system by Lemma \\(2.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Its time length is \\(q_0=N^{-h+o(1)}\\); its spatial radii are \\(q_0,q_0g\\) in version \\(2\\), with \\(g=N^{-\\beta}\\); set \\(g=1\\) in version \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(n_Q=n(L)q_0^jg\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On new blocks of length \\(q_1\\sim N^eq_0\\), there is an inverse-subpower paired incidence law, a coarse moving frame \\(D\\), and simple local scalar graphs from both old labels whose discrepancies on their common comparison domains are at most\n\\[\n\\delta N^{C_*e}.\n\\]\nThe opposite old label lists only subpowerly many possible frames \\(D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Sample a trajectory and two independent uniform times in a new block, requiring both old selected incidences. Jensen gives inverse-subpower pair mass; either time marginal is dominated by the old selected law.</p><p>There are at most \\(K_0N^e/n_Q\\) old labels per new block. Polynomial extrapolation gives\n\\[\n\\sup_I|P|\\le\\delta N^{C_0e},\\qquad\n\\sup_I|P_w|\\le N^{C_0e}.\n\\]\nExcept on power-small pair mass, each old derivative is at least \\(N^{-e}\\) at the opposite sampled time, by the affine sublevel estimate.</p><p>Remove pure-density exceptions on a depth grid and terminal/angular exceptions at depth \\(Re\\). If the two minor normals differ by more than \\(gN^{Re}\\), their velocity strips intersect in a ball of radius \\(K_0N^{-Re}\\). Summing the good angular numerators over opposite observations, hidden bins, and source labels bounds this exception by\n\\[\nK_0N^{(2+2C_0-SR)e}.\n\\]\nChoose \\(R\\) large.</p><p>Use \\(g^\\#=\\min(1,gN^{Re})\\). Round orientation, normal center velocity, and midpoint position at the scales defining a box of radii \\(q_1,q_1g^\\#\\). The partner label gives subpower compatibility lists. In its coordinates \\(z\\), the spatial Jacobian is at most \\(N^{C_1e}q_0^jg\\), and each fixed old-label/frame entry has unnormalized source-base density at most \\(N^{C_1e}n_Q\\). Delete entries below \\(n_QN^{-C'e}\\), using the old-label count.</p><p>For high curvature, replace \\(P\\) by its affine critical-center equation; otherwise\n\\[\nD=P_w^2-2P_{ww}P\n\\]\nis positive and between \\(N^{-O(e)}\\) and \\(N^{O(e)}\\) at the source. Heavy-entry projection volume and polynomial Remez bound its coefficients. A real net of radius \\(N^{-C_Ue}\\) supplies common stable complex balls and nearby branches with\n\\[\n|w-f(z)|\\le\\delta N^{O(e)}.\n\\]</p><p>If graph discrepancies have dyadic norm \\(M\\ge\\delta N^{C_*e}\\), group by frame, stable ball, and neighboring finite-jet bins at spacing \\(M\\). Each old index has \\(N^{O(e)}\\) options. The cap class bounds group mass by\n\\[\nN^{O(e)}n_Q(M/\\delta)^d.\n\\]\nHeavy marginal entries therefore give lists of size at most \\(N^{O(e)}(M/\\delta)^d\\), with index-base ceilings relative to probability index weights after group normalization. Proposition \\(4\\) excludes substantial discrepancy mass when \\(C_*\\) is sufficiently large.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "representative equations at a common complex anchor", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each frame \\(D\\), fixed complex anchor and detour choices, and each occurring finite anchor-jet bin \\(b\\), one may choose a representative real equation \\(\\widehat P_{D,b}\\), independent of the source time and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the frame and graph comparisons of Lemma \\(5.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each frame \\(D\\), fixed complex anchor and detour choices, and each occurring finite anchor-jet bin \\(b\\), one may choose a representative real equation \\(\\widehat P_{D,b}\\), independent of the source time and trajectory, such that retained source trajectories satisfy\n\\[\n\\sup_I|\\widehat P_{D,b}|\\le\\delta N^{C_2e},\n\\qquad\n\\sup_I|(\\widehat P_{D,b})_w|\\le N^{C_2e}.\n\\]\nExcept on power-small pair mass, its discriminant at the source has absolute value at least \\(N^{-C_3e}\\). The partner old label supplies a subpower list containing \\((D,b)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a random anchor \\(\\alpha_D\\) and random intermediate complex parameter \\(\\sigma_D\\). Along\n\\[\nz=z_0+s(\\alpha_D-z_0),\\qquad 0\\to\\sigma_D\\to1,\n\\]\npolynomial sublevels make endpoint discriminants inverse-power bounded except with power-small probability. Avoid small disks around the boundedly many discriminant roots. The path then has an inverse-power zero-free tube, and Lemma \\(4.2\\) propagates local graph agreement and finite jets with loss \\(N^{O(e)}\\).</p><p>Bin a sufficiently long anchor jet at spacing \\(\\delta N^{C_be}\\). The partner equation has at most two roots at the anchor, hence only boundedly many neighboring jet bins for each compatible frame. These are subpower lists, not merely \\(N^{O(e)}\\)-lists.</p><p>Choose the first eligible converted real source equation with a branch in each bin. Finite-jet norm control gives source/representative agreement near the anchor. Taylor expansion in \\(w\\), with \\(|\\widehat P_{ww}|\\le\\delta^{-1}\\), bounds\n\\[\n|\\widehat P(z,f(z))|\\le\\delta N^{O(e)},\\qquad\n|\\widehat P_w(z,f(z))|\\le N^{O(e)}.\n\\]\nContinue these evaluations back along the source-safe path; the representative branch itself need not be continued back. Near the source, the original derivative floor persists on an inverse-power segment, so the actual line stays within \\(\\delta N^{O(e)}\\) of its source branch. Polynomial extrapolation along the line gives the whole-block estimates.</p><p>The representative discriminant has an inverse-power value at its anchor, hence an inverse-power norm on a fixed real box. Polynomial sublevels bound its small-value set. For each source label there are only two anchor root/jet tuples, so at most boundedly many representatives to charge. Integrate their exceptional volumes against the source-entry density ceiling and sum the old-label count. Choose \\(C_3\\) large before the later curvature threshold.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "longer atlas construction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The representative data yield disjoint full-trajectory assignments on the \\(q_1\\)-blocks, inverse-subpower selected mass, actual old-\\(p_L\\) label lists, and admissible tests at thickness \\(\\delta N^{O(e)}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Lemma \\(5.2\\), keeping the source time averaged until the representative discriminant exception is estimated.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The representative data yield disjoint full-trajectory assignments on the \\(q_1\\)-blocks, inverse-subpower selected mass, actual old-\\(p_L\\) label lists, and admissible tests at thickness \\(\\delta N^{O(e)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Average over anchors, detours, and source time in each world/block, then freeze choices with success at least their average. At the fixed source time, the old source assignment determines a unique source label, frame, branch, and anchor bin. Merge source assignments with the same \\((D,b)\\). Require the whole-block geometric and representative bounds. Retain later original incidences whose old label lists \\((D,b)\\); this contains the frozen successes.</p><p>Choose \\(C_4&gt;C_3/2+2\\), \\(C_s&gt;C_2+C_4+1\\), and\n\\[\nT&gt;\\max(C_2+C_3+1,C_s+C_4+1).\n\\]\nIf \\(|\\widehat P_{ww}|\\le\\delta^{-1}N^{-Te}\\), the source discriminant forces the block derivative maximum into\n\\[\n[N^{-C_4e},N^{C_2e}].\n\\]\nBin that maximum dyadically and divide the equation by its lower bin endpoint. The value and curvature fit thickness \\(\\delta N^{C_se}\\). Remove times where the normalized affine derivative is below a suitably small reciprocal subpower; disjoint prior-times-time budgets make this loss negligible.</p><p>For larger curvature, use \\(w-F_{\\mathrm{ctr}}\\), fitting at thickness \\(\\delta N^{(T+C_2+1)e}\\), with derivative \\(1\\). Choose a curvature case carrying half the mass.</p><p>All stored jets have polynomial ranges. Continuation predicates have bounded semialgebraic format by Lemma \\(4.2\\); whole-block tests quantify only one time variable at a time. Inverse curvatures and derivative normalizations are used only above specified cutoffs. Thus all data are tempered. Undo the real base change and \\(w\\mapsto Bw\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition \\(4.3\\), Critical paths", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( 0<k\\le1, \\qquad \\sup_{0\\le r\\le1}|f_i(r)-f_i(0)-d_*r|\\to0. \\) For every fixed finite ordered target-depth grid ending at \\(1\\), there are nested true systems at actual depths tending to the targets, with cumulative …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a model version and \\(j,S,\\eta\\), and suppose cap-class problems with positive horizon exist.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nd_*=\\inf\\{d&lt;1:\\exists E\\in\\mathcal C(d),\\ H(E)&gt;0\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nk(d)=\\sup_{E\\in\\mathcal C(d)}H(E)/L,\n\\qquad\nk=\\lim_{d\\downarrow d_*}k(d).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize lengths to \\(1\\). A maximizing sequence has \\(d_i\\to d_*\\), \\(H(E_i)\\to k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In version \\(2\\), for a maximizing sequence let \\(\\omega\\) be the supremum of attainable limiting upper narrowness values of near-optimal terminal true systems, allowing all indicated subsequences and selections. Put \\(\\ell=\\inf\\omega\\) over maximizing sequences. In version \\(1\\), use \\(\\ell=0\\) formally.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n0&lt;k\\le1,\n\\qquad\n\\sup_{0\\le r\\le1}|f_i(r)-f_i(0)-d_*r|\\to0.\n\\]\nFor every fixed finite ordered target-depth grid ending at \\(1\\), there are nested true systems at actual depths tending to the targets, with cumulative time exponent tending to \\(kr\\) and segment rates tending to \\(k\\). Coarsened and remaining normalized problems are maximizing.</p><p>If \\(\\ell&lt;\\infty\\), paths can be chosen with cumulative and incremental narrowness \\(\\ell r+O(\\zeta)+o_i(1)\\), while the base sequence’s terminal narrowness supremum is at most \\(\\ell+\\zeta\\). If \\(\\ell=\\infty\\), each newly obtained maximizing sequence admits arbitrarily large relative narrowness on successive positive-length segments.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(6.1\\) and \\(6.2\\) establish horizon composition and positive time rate. Lemma \\(6.3\\) proves the linear relative profile, and Lemma \\(6.4\\) constructs the required nested maximizing paths and optimized narrowness alternatives.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.1\\), Composition of horizon costs", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( H(E)\\le h+H(F), \\qquad H(E)\\le H_{\\mathrm{out}}+k(d)(L-r). \\) These bounds include subsequences and permitted incidence restrictions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(E\\in\\mathcal C(d)\\) has length \\(L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A known atlas at depth \\(r&lt;L\\) has time exponent \\(h\\), and \\(F\\) is its normalized remaining problem in original exponent units.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(H_{\\mathrm{out}}\\) is the horizon infimum for coarsening to endpoint \\(r&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nH(E)\\le h+H(F),\n\\qquad\nH(E)\\le H_{\\mathrm{out}}+k(d)(L-r).\n\\]\nThese bounds include subsequences and permitted incidence restrictions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lift a terminal true system in \\(F\\) through the known atlas using terminal density equivalence in Lemma \\(2.5\\); time exponents add. For the second inequality, prepare near-optimal true systems of the coarsening with own-depth-known labels, normalize the remaining problem, and use its membership in \\(\\mathcal C(d)\\). The definition of \\(H(E)\\) already permits all resulting selections and subsequences.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.2\\), A positive critical time rate", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( 0<k\\le1. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(d_*,k(d),k\\) from Proposition \\(6\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n0&lt;k\\le1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition \\(2\\) gives \\(k\\le1\\). For a bad \\(E\\), choose \\(e&gt;0\\) small relative to \\(H(E)\\) and \\(L\\), and a terminal system with \\(h&lt;H(E)+e/4\\). Proposition \\(5\\) gives a known atlas of time \\(h-e\\), leaving length at most \\(Ce\\). Lemma \\(6.1\\) forces the remaining horizon above \\(3e/4\\), so its horizon/length ratio is at least \\(3/(4C)\\). Near \\(d_*\\), the extension constant is uniform for a fixed \\(d_0&lt;1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "relative profiles in Proposition \\(4.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every uniform subsequential limit of \\(f_i(r)-f_i(0)\\) is \\(d_*r\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\((E_i)\\) is a maximizing sequence.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every uniform subsequential limit of \\(f_i(r)-f_i(0)\\) is \\(d_*r\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If an interior secant on \\([a,b]\\) had slope below \\(d_*\\), choose \\(0&lt;d''&lt;d_*\\) above that slope and minimize \\(f_i(x)-d''x\\) there. Its minimizing endpoint \\(r_i\\) stays away from \\(a\\). The coarsened problem \\(G_i\\) has terminal slope cap \\(d''\\) on \\([a,r_i]\\), and a global cap strictly below \\(1\\), because for \\(x&lt;a\\),\n\\[\nf_i(r_i)-f_i(x)\n\\le r_i-x-(1-d'')(r_i-a).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(H(G_i)&gt;0\\), apply Proposition \\(5\\) with \\(e\\) so small that its remaining depth interval lies inside \\([a,r_i]\\). Normalization gives a positive-horizon problem in \\(\\mathcal C(d'')\\), contradicting the definition of \\(d_*\\). Thus \\(H(G_i)=0\\). Lemma \\(6.1\\) then gives\n\\[\nH(E_i)\\le k(d_i)(1-r_i),\n\\]\ncontradicting \\(H(E_i)\\to k&gt;0\\). If \\(d_*=0\\), monotonicity already excludes lower secants. The limiting endpoint cap and continuity force linearity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "nested systems and narrowness optimization in Proposition \\(4.3\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The nested true paths and narrowness alternatives in Proposition \\(6\\) exist, retaining full layer assignments, priors, and incidence witnesses separately from final path incidences.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the profile conclusion of Lemma \\(6.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite target-depth grid and, when \\(\\ell&lt;\\infty\\), \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The nested true paths and narrowness alternatives in Proposition \\(6\\) exist, retaining full layer assignments, priors, and incidence witnesses separately from final path incidences.</p>", "proof": "<p>For target \\(r&lt;1\\), minimize \\(f_i(x)-(d_*+\\epsilon_i)x\\) on \\([0,r]\\), with \\(\\epsilon_i\\downarrow0\\) slowly. The minimizer \\(r_i\\to r\\), and the coarsening has cap \\(d_*+\\epsilon_i\\). Composition bounds give\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{out}}\\le k(d_*+\\epsilon_i)r_i,\\qquad\nH(E_i)\\le H_{\\mathrm{out}}+k(d_i)(1-r_i),\n\\]\nso its relative horizon tends to \\(k\\). Normalize through near-optimal outer true systems; the remaining problems are also maximizing. Iterate finitely many times. Actual segment lengths, not merely their limits, are used in rescaling.</p><p>For finite \\(\\ell\\), choose a base maximizing sequence with terminal supremum at most \\(\\ell+\\zeta\\). At every segment choose near-optimal systems with relative narrowness at least \\(\\ell-\\zeta\\) in the limiting lower sense, passing to subsequences. Additivity of narrowness gives lower bounds at every node; subtract subsequent segment lower bounds from the terminal upper bound to obtain matching upper bounds.</p><p>For \\(\\ell=\\infty\\), apply its definition to every newly produced maximizing sequence and diagonalize arbitrarily large finite thresholds. Preserve earlier assignment priors and witnesses; later path restrictions are not asserted to saturate earlier layers.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition \\(4.7\\), Prepared tangent data; Proposition \\(4.11\\), Conversion of candidates to actual improvements. Supporting original labels: Corollary \\(4.4\\), Lemmas \\(4.5\\), \\(4.6\\), \\(4.8\\), \\(4.9\\), \\(4.12\\), Corollary \\(4.13\\), Definition \\(4.10\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The guarded diagonal admits the prepared laws, caps, lists, and breadth described below.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a finite-\\(\\ell\\) critical path from Proposition \\(6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A guarded tangent diagonal has stages \\(n\\to\\infty\\), depth units \\(\\epsilon_n\\to0\\), original resolutions \\(N_0\\), tangent resolutions\n\\[\nN=N_0^{\\epsilon_n},\n\\]\nand analytical factors \\(K=N^{o(1)}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The original tempered final path has mass \\(p_{\\mathrm{path}}\\ge K_{\\mathrm{path}}^{-1}\\), with \\(K_{\\mathrm{path}}=N_0^{o(1)}\\) within each fixed exact problem.</p><p class=\"li\">A substantial residual has a positive fraction of the original reference path mass; dense tangent mass means inverse-subpower mass.</p><p class=\"li\">In a tangent parent, \\(Q_s\\) has thickness \\(a=N^{-s}\\), time \\(q_s\\sim_{\\log,N}N^{-ks}\\), full assignment mass\n\\[\n\\nu_Q\\sim_{\\log,N}a^dJ_s,\\qquad d=d_*,\n\\]\nand \\(J_s\\sim_{\\log,N}q_s^jN^{-\\ell s}\\) in version \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A time candidate of gain \\(c&gt;0\\), on the same \\(Q_s\\)-block, fits a permitted quadratic at \\(a'=aN^{-c}\\), with whole-block value and derivative upper bounds \\(Ka'\\), \\(K\\), curvature at most \\(K/a'\\), derivative lower bound \\(K^{-1}\\) on counted incidences, and counted mass divided by \\(q_s\\) at least \\(K^{-1}N^{-dc}\\nu_Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0&lt;\\ell&lt;\\infty\\), a strip candidate has a row \\(R\\) with\n\\[\n|Y'-R\\cdot(1,v)|\\le KN^{-\\ell s-c}\n\\]\non pieces carrying counted mass divided by \\(q_s\\) at least \\(K^{-1}\\nu_Q\\), where \\(v=Z'\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The guarded diagonal admits the prepared laws, caps, lists, and breadth described below. If every substantial residual, on a further subsequence of every subsequence, supplies a time or strip candidate in a bounded tangent-depth range with fixed positive limiting gain, then an original known atlas exists that contradicts the critical-path exclusion. Its original mass is at least \\(p_{\\mathrm{path}}/\\log N_0\\), its slacks and lists are bounded by a fixed power of \\(K_{\\mathrm{path}}\\log N_0\\), and its complexity exponent is bounded by a scenario-dependent polynomial in the old path complexity exponent.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(7.1\\)–\\(7.5\\) construct the guarded prepared tangent setting and its conditional estimates. Lemma \\(7.6\\) separates analytical discovery from tempered original-law implementation. Lemmas \\(7.7\\) and \\(7.8\\) convert candidates on all substantial residuals into outputs excluded by Lemma \\(7.3\\). Lemma \\(7.9\\) supplies the isotropic breadth consequence used below.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Corollary \\(4.4\\), Depth-zero normalization and vanishing-depth nodes", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The normalized sequence is maximizing, without any bound on absolute density exponents.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A maximizing sequence undergoes depth-zero, horizon-zero known normalizations with actual subpower success and terminal knowledge.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The normalized sequence is maximizing, without any bound on absolute density exponents. Maximizing sequences also admit true nodes at positive depths \\(b_i\\to0\\), with time exponents tending to zero and the required slowly diagonalized relative-profile estimates.</p>", "proof": "<p>For normalized problems \\(F_i\\), lifting gives\n\\[\nH(E_i)\\le H(F_i)\\le k(d_i).\n\\]\nBoth bounds tend to \\(k\\). For vanishing-depth nodes, apply Proposition \\(6\\) at each fixed \\(b=1/m\\), take the outer index sufficiently late for its exact subsequences and error requirements, and then let \\(m\\) increase slowly.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.5\\), Excluded improvements", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The following are impossible:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A path node has cumulative depth \\(r_i\\), time exponent \\(h_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Gains \\(\\Delta_i&gt;0\\) have a uniform positive lower bound.</p><p class=\"li\">Labels are known by original \\(p_L\\), with original subpower coverage and slacks.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following are impossible:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A known atlas fitting at \\(r_i+\\Delta_i&lt;1\\), with time exponent at most \\(h_i+k\\Delta_i/2\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. In the finite-\\(\\ell\\) optimization, a known atlas at exactly the node’s depth and time, with extra narrowness \\(\\Delta_i\\gg\\zeta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Completing the first atlas gives\n\\[\nH(E_i)\\le h_i+k\\Delta_i/2+k(d_i)(1-r_i-\\Delta_i)\n\\le k-k\\Delta_i/2+o_i(1).\n\\]\nFor the second, the remaining normalized sequence still maximizes. Complete it with near-optimal true terminal systems of relative narrowness at least \\(\\ell-\\zeta\\). Additive narrowness exceeds the base sequence’s upper bound \\(\\ell+\\zeta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.6\\), Finite-sample exclusion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For all sufficiently late \\(N_0\\), no improving known atlas exists with:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a stage, outer exact problem, exact path subsequence, and finite original-coordinate complexity exponent \\(M^\\#\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a buffered forbidden gain \\(\\gamma_n\\epsilon_n&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently late \\(N_0\\), no improving known atlas exists with:\n\\[\n\\text{complexity}\\le N_0^{M^\\#},\\quad\n\\text{slacks and original lists}\\le(K_{\\mathrm{path}}\\log N_0)^n,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{mass}\\ge p_{\\mathrm{path}}/\\log N_0.\n\\]\nOne may choose the diagonal with\n\\[\nM^\\#=2^{(M_{\\mathrm{path}}+n)^2},\n\\quad\nG_n/\\log N_0\\le1/n,\n\\]\n\\[\nN_0^{-1}K_{\\mathrm{path}}\\log N_0\\le1/n,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\log((K_{\\mathrm{path}}\\log N_0)^n)}\n{\\epsilon_n\\log N_0}\\le1/n.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Infinitely many such outputs in one fixed exact sequence would be uniformly tempered and have actual original subpower lists, slacks, and reciprocal masses. Homogenization and a subsequence would preserve the buffered improvement, contradicting Lemma \\(7.2\\).</p><p>Choose stage/grid, then outer problem and path, then complexity guard, then a sufficiently late sample. For every fixed scenario \\(S\\), any polynomial complexity bound \\(P_S(M_{\\mathrm{path}})\\) is eventually below the declared exponential guard; any fixed slack power is eventually below \\(n\\). No resampling or preservation of horizon by a tangent limit is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "Proposition \\(4.7\\), preparation portion; Lemma \\(4.8\\), Likelihood truncation and finite-state conditioning", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Preparation retains \\(1-o(1)\\) relative reference mass and gives \\( \\lambda_A^0\\le K\\nu_A\\,dt,\\qquad \\pi_A(B_p)\\le Kp^S, \\qquad \\sum_Ap_AI_{\\lambda_A^0}([v];E)=o(\\log N). \\) Pure hidden/native-signal densities are at …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the guarded finite-\\(\\ell\\) diagonal.</p><p class=\"li\">Parent conditional reference probabilities are \\(\\lambda_A^0\\), with fixed weights \\(p_A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi_A\\) be the velocity marginal; use \\(v=V\\) for version \\(1\\) or \\(\\ell=0\\), and \\(v=Z'\\) for positive finite \\(\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E\\) denote a sufficiently deep end history.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Preparation retains \\(1-o(1)\\) relative reference mass and gives\n\\[\n\\lambda_A^0\\le K\\nu_A\\,dt,\\qquad \\pi_A(B_p)\\le Kp^S,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_Ap_AI_{\\lambda_A^0}([v];E)=o(\\log N).\n\\]\nPure hidden/native-signal densities are at most \\(Kp^d\\); in unit \\(Q_s\\)-base coordinates,\n\\[\nd(\\operatorname{base}_{Q_s})_*(\\lambda_A^0|Q_s)\n\\le Kq_s\\nu_Q\\,du.\n\\]\nPoint states and path labels have actual original observation lists, and sufficiently deep histories give only \\(K\\) point-state possibilities.</p><p>If \\(R_A^0(E,b)\\) has mutual information \\(o(\\log N)\\) in the weighted mixture, deletion of \\(o(1)\\) mass gives\n\\[\nR_{A,\\mathrm{good}}(E,b)\n\\le K\\lambda_{A,\\mathrm{pre}}(E)\\pi_A(b).\n\\]\nAppending at most \\(D\\le K\\) entries per history, then deleting masses below \\(\\lambda_{A,\\mathrm{pre}}(E)/K_1\\), costs at most \\(D/K_1\\). After summing to a state \\(C\\) with fixed reference mass \\(w_A(C)\\), and deleting current states below \\(w_A(C)/K_2\\), one obtains\n\\[\nT_A(b\\mid C)\\le KK_1K_2\\pi_A(b),\n\\]\nat cost at most \\(K_2^{-1}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Prepare density and angular masks at every potential parent before selecting an entropy window. Saturation and the normalization factor \\(J_A/\\nu_{\\mathrm{old}}(A)\\) convert original profile caps to \\(Kp^d\\). Remove angular exceptions before projecting velocity and summing numerators.</p><p>For positive narrowness, original largest-axis fullness also gives fullness of the descendant major-coordinate row. Orthogonalizing spatial rows produces tilted boxes and predictions\n\\[\nY'=A_s(1,v)+O(KN^{-\\ell s}),\n\\]\nwith actual original subpower row coefficients.</p><p>Normalize the surviving path once. Discard parents with too small success fractions using the full assignment budget. For disjoint depth windows, mutual-information increments about a fixed sufficiently fine original velocity bin sum to its entropy \\(O(U_n\\log N)\\). Since the number of windows is \\(\\gtrsim(D_n\\epsilon_n)^{-1}\\) and \\(U_nD_n\\epsilon_n\\to0\\), one window has information \\(o(\\log N)\\). Freeze its parent weights and reference laws.</p><p>Stable graph descriptions follow from discriminants. High curvature gives an affine critical center; low curvature gives simple branches on inverse-subpower cores. Delete light entries using \\(q_s\\nu_Q\\) budgets. Composing with the native test and applying Lemma \\(4.1\\) gives derivative-controlled graphs. Deep labels then localize base and signal states to \\(K\\) choices. Original \\(p_L\\) supplies actual lists because its hidden bin is much finer than every tangent mesh.</p><p>For likelihood truncation, the negative part of\n\\[\n\\log\\frac{R_A^0(E,b)}\n{\\lambda_{A,\\mathrm{pre}}(E)\\pi_A(b)}\n\\]\nhas bounded expectation. Its positive part has weighted expectation \\(o(\\log N)\\). Remove values above \\(\\delta_N\\log N\\), with \\(\\delta_N\\downarrow0\\) slowly. Markov gives the displayed joint numerator ceiling.</p><p>For appended entries, the threshold sum is \\(D/K_1\\). On surviving entries the joint numerator is at most \\(KK_1\\pi_A(b)\\) times its predeletion mass. Sum to \\(C\\). A further restriction retains this numerator but may shrink the denominator; the second threshold costs \\(K_2^{-1}\\) and restores the conditional bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.5", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.9\\), Retained duration and fixed-time prior caps", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Deleting complementary line–interval pairs costs at most \\(K_2^{-1}\\), and \\( \\nu(L_I\\cap C_t) \\le\\frac{K_2}{|I|} \\int_I\\int1_{C_\\tau^+}(l)G(l,\\tau)d\\nu(l)d\\tau. \\) The instantaneous restricted incidence mass is at most …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(d\\lambda(l,t)=G(l,t)d\\nu(l)dt\\), with \\(\\nu\\) a probability and \\(0\\le G\\le K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a dyadic time interval \\(I\\), put\n\\[\nL_I=\\left\\{l:\\int_IG(l,\\tau)d\\tau\\ge |I|/K_2\\right\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For fixed \\(t\\in I\\), assume \\(C_t(l)\\Rightarrow C_\\tau^+(l)\\) for every \\(\\tau\\in I\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Deleting complementary line–interval pairs costs at most \\(K_2^{-1}\\), and\n\\[\n\\nu(L_I\\cap C_t)\n\\le\\frac{K_2}{|I|}\n\\int_I\\int1_{C_\\tau^+}(l)G(l,\\tau)d\\nu(l)d\\tau.\n\\]\nThe instantaneous restricted incidence mass is at most \\(K\\nu(L_I\\cap C_t)\\) almost everywhere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Integrate the deleted duration bound over \\(\\nu\\) and sum \\(\\sum_I|I|=1\\). On \\(L_I\\cap C_t\\), the implication makes the indicated integral at least \\(|I|/K_2\\). Integrate over trajectories. The last assertion uses \\(G\\le K\\). Fine time partitions and bounded motion give the implication for enlarged point and velocity mesh constraints.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.6", "orig": "Lemma \\(4.12\\), Original-law recovery of finite lists", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a cell-table list, using only the base cell and original observed entries, of the same size and \\(\\mu\\)-success at least \\(m-2e\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mu\\) is an original unnormalized tempered path measure; \\(\\nu\\le\\mu\\) is an arbitrary measurable submeasure.</p><p class=\"li\">A finite geometric output-label map \\(A\\) is defined on the original law and agrees with the analytical labels on \\(\\nu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Original observation is \\(O=(x,o)\\), with exact base \\(x=(t,y)\\) and original observed discrete entries \\(o\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A measurable list rule \\(L(O)\\), of size at most \\(D_{\\mathrm{list}}\\), succeeds on \\(\\nu\\)-mass at least \\(m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\lambda\\) is the joint pushforward retaining original, path, and output tags; \\(F_\\rho\\lambda\\) is base-cell averaging and\n\\[\n\\|\\lambda-F_\\rho\\lambda\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\le e.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a cell-table list, using only the base cell and original observed entries, of the same size and \\(\\mu\\)-success at least \\(m-2e\\). For \\(e=O(N_0^{-1})\\), its complexity is bounded by a fixed scenario-dependent polynomial in original path complexity, independently of analytical masks and posterior-search complexity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Domination gives original success at least \\(m\\) for the analytical list. In each base cell and observed entry, choose the \\(D_{\\mathrm{list}}\\) labels of largest joint mass. On the averaged law this maximizes success pointwise. Two total-variation comparisons give \\(m-2e\\).</p><p>For quantitative complexity, replace the spatial intercept by \\(y(t)\\), a Jacobian-one change. Before marginalizing other coefficients, view the density as a vector indexed by all tags. Translation of one base coordinate crosses only polynomially many polynomial roots. If \\(H,T,D,R,J\\) bound density, predicate count, degrees, ranges, and copies, then\n\\[\n\\|\\tau_hf-f\\|_1\n\\le CH(TD+1)J(1+R)^q|h|.\n\\]\nMarginalization contracts \\(L^1\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\|\\lambda-F_\\rho\\lambda\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n\\le N_0^{B_S(M_{\\mathrm{path}})}\\rho.\n\\]\nChoose a polynomially bounded mesh exponent exceeding \\(B_S+2\\). The number of cells, observed entries, and labels is polynomial with a scenario-dependent polynomial exponent. Unknown tags never become conditioning data for the selector.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.7", "orig": "greedy coverage and strip conversion in Proposition \\(4.11\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On a subsequence, one fixed candidate scenario and one bounded-depth node have reference coverage \\( \\beta_n\\ge\\eta_0/(JG_n), \\qquad \\beta_n^{-1}=N^{o(1)}, \\qquad \\beta_n\\log N_0\\to\\infty. \\) Strip coverage produces an …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Every substantial residual supplies candidates as in Proposition \\(7\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On a subsequence, one fixed candidate scenario and one bounded-depth node have reference coverage\n\\[\n\\beta_n\\ge\\eta_0/(JG_n),\n\\qquad\n\\beta_n^{-1}=N^{o(1)},\n\\qquad\n\\beta_n\\log N_0\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nStrip coverage produces an original known atlas with the same node depth and time and a fixed positive tangent narrowness gain.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use a countable menu fixing candidate type, positive gain, bounded node range, and sufficiently small power slack \\(N^\\sigma\\). Greedily assign all free trajectories satisfying each selected candidate’s whole-block inequalities. Disjointness bounds the number per chart by \\(N^{dc+\\sigma}\\) for time candidates and \\(N^\\sigma\\) for strips.</p><p>If every fixed finite menu covered vanishing reference mass, remove a slowly growing menu’s assignments. The residual remains substantial and supplies a positive limiting-gain candidate compatible with one fixed scenario, contradicting maximality. Pigeonhole the finite menu and then at most \\(G_n\\) nodes.</p><p>For strips, compare each assigned row \\(R\\) with a sufficiently deeper true row \\(A_{\\mathrm{deep}}\\). Unless\n\\[\n\\|R-A_{\\mathrm{deep}}\\|\\le gN^{-c/2},\n\\qquad g=N^{-\\ell s},\n\\]\nthe allowed velocities lie in an interval of length at most \\(N^{-c/3}\\). The deep-history joint palette bound charges these misaligned rows by \\(KN^{\\sigma-Sc/3}=o(\\beta_n)\\).</p><p>Bin \\(R\\) at\n\\[\n\\tau=(J_s/q_s^2)N^{-c/4},\n\\]\nand bin \\(Y-R_1^bZ\\) at the block midpoint to width \\(q_s\\tau\\). The deeper row and exact base give actual original lists. The frame with columns\n\\[\nq_s(1,R_1^b),\\qquad q_s(0,\\tau)\n\\]\nhas determinant \\(J_sN^{-c/4}\\), preserving time, depth, and test. Store geometric priority assignments and apply Lemma \\(7.6\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.8", "orig": "time-candidate comparison and actual improvement in Proposition \\(4.11\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The covered law yields an original known atlas with positive tangent depth gain, additional time cost less than half \\(k\\) times that gain, original success at least \\(p_{\\mathrm{path}}/\\log N_0\\), and the guarded …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A fixed time-candidate scenario has the coverage of Lemma \\(7.7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Its gain is \\(c&gt;0\\), and its slack \\(\\sigma&gt;0\\) is sufficiently small.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The covered law yields an original known atlas with positive tangent depth gain, additional time cost less than half \\(k\\) times that gain, original success at least \\(p_{\\mathrm{path}}/\\log N_0\\), and the guarded complexity and actual-list bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>In unit \\(Q_s\\)-base coordinates, with \\(m_Q=p_Aq_s\\nu_Q\\), each candidate index has base marginal ceiling\n\\[\nm_QN^{-dc+O(\\sigma)}du.\n\\]\nIts initial mass witness therefore projects to volume at least \\(N^{-O(\\sigma)}\\).</p><p>Convert candidate and true tests to simple graphs, using affine critical centers for curvature at least \\(N^{-C\\sigma}/a_{\\mathrm{fit}}\\). Remez bounds discriminants from initial matching witnesses. Subdivide into time and spatial subboxes of relative size \\(\\rho\\sim N^{-C'\\sigma}\\), with actual path-slack lists. Stable branches approximate every assigned whole short piece.</p><p>Condition on\n\\[\nO=(p_L,Q_s,\\text{subbox},\\text{true branch})\n\\]\nand take two independent posteriors, retaining the unnormalized covered mass. Candidate values agree to \\(a'N^{O(\\sigma)}\\) at the common base. Delete light marginal entries. Their graphs are within \\(aN^{O(\\sigma)}\\) of the true graph on comparison domains by Lemma \\(4.1\\).</p><p>Set \\(\\epsilon=\\epsilon_*/2\\), \\(\\theta=\\epsilon_*/8\\), using Proposition \\(4\\) with \\(R=a/a'=N^c\\). A substantial set of norm gaps above \\(aN^{-2\\theta c}\\) would select a group of bad mass \\(B\\ge m_QN^{-O(\\sigma)}\\). Its retained lists have at most \\(N^{dc+O(\\sigma)}\\) indices. If \\(W_\\kappa\\) is the sum of index weights \\(N^{-dc}\\), the normalized marginal multiplier is\n\\[\nN^{O(\\sigma)}m_QW_\\kappa/B=N^{O(\\sigma)}.\n\\]\nChoose every fixed \\(C\\sigma/c\\) below \\(\\epsilon_*/8\\). Proposition \\(4\\) excludes these bad pairs.</p><p>Bin sufficiently many candidate jets at spacing \\(aN^{-\\theta c}\\). Posterior-neighbor lists retain a fixed fraction of aggregate coverage. Merge assignments with the same old chart, short block, subbox, true sign, and candidate jet bin; the original candidate index is omitted.</p><p>Choose a representative equation per bin. Branches differ by \\(O(aN^{-\\theta c})\\) throughout the short piece. For curvature at most \\((aN^{-\\theta c/2})^{-1}\\), normalize by its stable branch derivative and fit at thickness \\(a_QN^{-2\\theta c/3}\\). For larger curvature use its affine center and thickness \\(a_QN^{-\\theta c/3}\\). Select a type carrying fixed success. Impose \\(C'\\sigma&lt;k\\theta c/12\\); the time cost is then below half \\(k\\) times the depth gain.</p><p>Candidate coefficients have polynomial original bounds: curvature bounds the \\(w^2\\) coefficient, and on the initial witness projection value and derivative bounds control the affine and quadratic base coefficients by polynomial Remez. Store only geometric tests, priorities, branch flags, and merger tables. Lemma \\(7.6\\) recovers lists without encoding masks or posterior rules.</p><p>The final original mass is\n\\[\nc_0\\beta_np_{\\mathrm{path}}-O(N_0^{-1})\n&gt;p_{\\mathrm{path}}/\\log N_0.\n\\]\nScenario-dependent complexity polynomials and actual slack powers fit the predeclared guard.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.9", "orig": "Corollary \\(4.13\\), Breadth before sparse selections", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The total residual probability of states whose conditional velocity law puts mass at least \\(\\kappa\\) within distance \\(N^{-u}\\) of an affine line tends to zero.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Version \\(2\\), \\(\\ell=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A substantial residual has a discrete state with actual original observation lists and at most \\(K\\) possibilities given deep end histories.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\kappa,u&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The total residual probability of states whose conditional velocity law puts mass at least \\(\\kappa\\) within distance \\(N^{-u}\\) of an affine line tends to zero. Version \\(1\\) has the corresponding interval conclusion.</p><p>Reciprocal-subpower breadth thresholds may be prepared before sparse selections. Breadth persists under selections of whole states or controlled fixed-fraction selections, not arbitrary velocity-dependent inverse-subpower restrictions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Predicted strips occurring on substantial narrow events must have palette mass at least a reciprocal subpower, by the deep-history joint numerator bound. Take maximal parameter-separated representatives at spacing \\(r_1=N^{-u/2}\\). Intersections of two separated strips either are empty in the bounded velocity range or lie in a ball of diameter at most \\(N^{-u/3}\\). Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n(M_1\\kappa_N)^2\\le M_1+KN^{-Su/3}M_1^2,\n\\]\nso \\(M_1\\le K\\).</p><p>Assign trajectories, by velocity alone, to their first enlarged representative. Outside negligible overlaps, its parameters agree with the state prediction to \\(r_2=N^{-u/4}\\). Bin parameters and transverse midpoint position. These data define a parent atlas whose determinant gains a fixed tangent power, while its test, depth, and time are unchanged. State knowledge gives actual lists; Lemma \\(7.6\\) and the guarded encoding give the forbidden width improvement. In version \\(1\\), the angular cap already excludes interval concentration.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.6\\), Higher-jet polynomial fitting", "depth": 0, "snippet": "A dense set of labeled incidences \\((l,t)\\) admits a row-valued polynomial \\(P\\) of degree at most \\(M\\), coefficients bounded by \\(K\\), such that \\( |F(v)-P(v)|\\le Kh^{M+1/4} \\) on labeled times of \\(l\\) of length at …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(K=N^{o(1)}\\), \\(0\\le d&lt;1\\), \\(j\\in\\{1,2\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A fixed trajectory probability \\(\\nu\\) carries quadratic \\(x_l(t)\\) and affine \\(y_l(t)\\in\\mathbb R^j\\), with coefficients bounded by \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Measurable labels \\(E_l\\subset[0,1]\\) have inverse-subpower \\(\\nu\\,dt\\)-mass.</p><p class=\"li\">A time-only row \\(F(t)\\), \\(|F|\\le K\\), defines\n\\[\nS_l(t)=x_l(t)-F(t)y_l(t),\\qquad\nR_l(t)=x_l'(t)-F(t)y_l'.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Scalar-normal data, original Definition \\(5.2\\), require, for \\(\\zeta\\le q\\le r\\le1\\),\n\\[\nN_q(J\\cap\\{S_l(t):t\\in E_l\\})\\le K(r/q)^d\n\\quad(|J|=r),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\nu\\{l:t\\in E_l,\\ (x_l(t),y_l(t))\\in Q_q\\}\\le Kq^{j+d},\n\\]\n\\[\n|F(t)-F(u)|\\le K(|t-u|+\\zeta),\n\\]\n\\[\n|R_l(t)-R_{\\tilde l}(u)|\n\\le K\\bigl(\\operatorname{dist}((t,x_l,y_l),(u,x_{\\tilde l},y_{\\tilde l}))+\\zeta\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(B\\) bins all displayed polynomial coefficients at side \\(r\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu\\{l\\in B:t\\in E_l,\\ (x_l(t),y_l(t))\\in Q_q\\}}{\\nu(B)}\n\\le K(q/r)^{j+d}.\n\\]\nAll these are absolute labeled masses under the unchanged prior.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(M\\ge2\\), \\(h=N^{-c+o(1)}\\), \\(c&gt;0\\). Put\n\\[\nc_0=.1,\\quad r_i=\\min(.009,c_{i-1}/2),\\quad c_i=r_i/2,\n\\]\n\\[\nr_{\\min}=.009\\,4^{-(M-2)},\\qquad \\alpha_0=(1-d)/64.\n\\]\nChoose dyadic lengths \\(h\\gg H_1\\gg\\cdots\\gg H_{M-1}\\gg H_*\\) with\n\\[\nH_1^{r_{\\min}}\\le h^{M+11},\\quad\nH_i^{r_{\\min}}\\le H_{i-1}^{1.02},\\quad\nH_*^{\\alpha_0}\\le H_{M-1}^{1.02},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\zeta\\le K^{-1}\\min(H_*^3,h^{M+1/2}).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A dense set of labeled incidences \\((l,t)\\) admits a row-valued polynomial \\(P\\) of degree at most \\(M\\), coefficients bounded by \\(K\\), such that\n\\[\n|F(v)-P(v)|\\le Kh^{M+1/4}\n\\]\non labeled times of \\(l\\) of length at least \\(h/K\\) in the \\(h\\)-block containing \\(t\\), including \\(t\\). The conclusion applies anew to every dense restriction satisfying the same upper hypotheses.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma \\(8.4\\) with the hierarchy fixed in the statement. Presample a binary tree of nearby times at scales \\(H_1,\\ldots,H_{M-1}\\), independently of the trajectory; separately sample \\(t'\\) uniformly in the root’s \\(h\\)-block. Repeated Jensen bounds the all-label event below by a fixed power of the single-time label mass. Require pair gaps at scale \\(H_i\\) to be at least \\(H_i/K\\).</p><p>Set \\(F_0=F\\), \\(F_1=A\\). Inductively define time-tree rows and jets\n\\[\nJ_i(t)=x_l^{(i)}(t)-F_i(t)y_l(t)-iF_{i-1}(t)y_l'.\n\\]\nMaintain negligible entropy of \\(J_i(t)_r\\) given \\(T,S_l(t)_r\\) on every further dense event. At a pair \\(t,w\\) of scale \\(H\\), read children at \\(q=H^{1.01}\\). The first-jet Taylor estimate gives\n\\[\nS_l(w)=S_l(t)+(w-t)J_1(t)+O(KH^{1.1}).\n\\]\nDifference quotients satisfy exactly\n\\[\n\\nabla J_i\n=x_l^{(i+1)}(t)-(\\nabla F_i)y_l(t)\n-\\bigl(F_i(w)+i\\nabla F_{i-1}\\bigr)y_l'.\n\\]</p><p>Assume \\(\\nabla F_{i-1}=F_i(t)+O(KH^{c_{i-1}})\\). A power-large tail of \\(\\nabla F_i\\), after division by its norm, would encode a \\(T\\)-known unit projection of \\(y_l(t)\\) with negligible entropy at positive-power accuracy, contradicting Lemma \\(8.2\\). Hence \\(|\\nabla F_i|\\le K\\) after negligible deletion.</p><p>At width \\(H^{r_i}\\), the quotient encodes\n\\[\nx_l^{(i+1)}-(\\nabla F_i)y_l-(i+1)F_i(t)y_l'.\n\\]\nFor \\(i&lt;m\\), subtract \\(J_{i+1}(t)\\); the projection entropy bound forces\n\\[\n\\nabla F_i-F_{i+1}(t)=O(KH^{r_i/2}).\n\\]\nFor \\(i=m\\), define \\(F_{m+1}=\\nabla F_m\\). Coefficient-bin knowledge and the entropy inequality transfer fine-scalar information to the desired reading. All row choices depend only on time-tree data and the world, not the trajectory or \\(t'\\).</p><p>The prescribed scale inequalities ensure root jets are readable at \\(p=h^{M+10}\\), child readings supply their parents, and all projection readings exceed the scalar floor.</p><p>At root \\(u\\), define\n\\[\nP_F(v)=\\sum_{i=0}^M\\frac{F_i(u)}{i!}(v-u)^i,\\qquad\nE=F(t')-P_F(t').\n\\]\nExact quadratic/affine evolution gives\n\\[\nS_l(t')\n=\\sum_{i=0}^M\\frac{J_i(u)}{i!}(t'-u)^i\n-Ey_l(t')+O(Kh^{M+1}).\n\\]\nOn a dense event with \\(|E|\\ge h^{M+1/4}\\), put \\(r=h^{1/4}\\). Local scalar support at width \\(|E|r\\) gives an entropy upper bound \\(d\\log(1/r)+o_*\\) for the projection \\((E/|E|)y_l(u)\\), whereas Lemma \\(8.2\\) gives \\(\\log(1/r)-o_*\\). This is impossible.</p><p>Finally condition on the trajectory and tree excluding \\(t'\\). The polynomial is fixed, and \\(t'\\) was originally uniform in its block. Discard fibers with too little successful fraction. Remaining fibers have labeled length at least \\(h/K\\); projecting the good tuple event gives the asserted dense set of incidences, each included in its witnessing fiber.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.1\\), Scalar coefficient clustering", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Some side-\\(p\\) coefficient bin has prior mass at least \\(p^d/K\\), using only trajectories of labeled length at least \\(K^{-1}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(x_l=x_{0,l}+tx_{1,l}+t^2x_{2,l}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\nu\\) has total mass at most \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(p=N^{-c+o(1)}\\), \\(c&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Dense labeled incidences dominated by \\(K\\nu\\,dt\\) occupy at most \\(Kp^{-1-d}\\) side-\\(p\\) squares in \\((t,x)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Every coefficient ball of radius \\(r\\ge p\\) has \\(\\nu\\)-mass at most \\(Kr^d\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Some side-\\(p\\) coefficient bin has prior mass at least \\(p^d/K\\), using only trajectories of labeled length at least \\(K^{-1}\\). This applies anew to every dense residual with the same upper bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>Prune short labels and adjoin an independent uniform \\(D\\in[0,1]\\). Lift to\n\\[\nz_{l,D}(t)=\\bigl(x_{0,l}+t(x_{1,l}-D),\\,D+tx_{2,l}\\bigr),\n\\qquad (1,t)\\cdot z_{l,D}(t)=x_l(t).\n\\]\nThe lifted support uses at most \\(Kp^{-2-d}\\) cells, with weighted marks at least \\(p^{-1}/K\\). Lemma \\(2.6\\) yields a full-time plank with widths \\(p/K\\le u\\le v\\le K\\) and mass at least\n\\[\nK^{-1}p^{d-1}uv.\n\\]\nIts scalar coefficients lie in one ball of radius \\(Kv\\). Varying \\(D\\) moves the lift by \\(D(-t,1)\\); for a unit narrow normal \\(n\\),\n\\[\n\\max(|n_2|,|n_2-n_1|)\\ge c.\n\\]\nThus a fixed coefficient triple accepts at most \\(Cu\\) of the dummy coordinate. The coefficient cap bounds plank mass by \\(Kuv^d\\). Hence \\(v^{1-d}\\le Kp^{1-d}\\), so \\(v\\le Kp\\). Dividing the lower plank mass by \\(Cu\\), then pigeonholing its \\(K\\) ordinary \\(p\\)-bins, gives \\(p^d/K\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(5.3\\), Entropy on a dense tested event", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At tested times and available scales, \\( H_Q(S(t)_r\\mid T)=d\\log(1/r)+o_*, \\) \\( H_Q(y(t)_r\\mid T,S(t)_r)=j\\log(1/r)+o_*, \\) \\( I_Q(l_r;S(t)_q\\mid T,S(t)_r)=o_*\\quad(q<r). \\) For every \\(T\\)-measurable unit row \\(e\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use scalar-normal data from Proposition \\(8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Presample finitely many times independently of the trajectory within each world, with absolutely continuous tested-time marginals.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(T\\) records the world and entire time tuple.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(A\\) is a dense event on which all tested labels hold; \\(Q\\) is its normalized restriction.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(l_r\\) bins the displayed trajectory coefficients at width \\(r\\); \\(o_* =o(\\log N)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At tested times and available scales,\n\\[\nH_Q(S(t)_r\\mid T)=d\\log(1/r)+o_*,\n\\]\n\\[\nH_Q(y(t)_r\\mid T,S(t)_r)=j\\log(1/r)+o_*,\n\\]\n\\[\nI_Q(l_r;S(t)_q\\mid T,S(t)_r)=o_*\\quad(q&lt;r).\n\\]\nFor every \\(T\\)-measurable unit row \\(e\\), \\(r\\ge q\\),\n\\[\nH_Q((ey(t))_r\\mid T,S(t)_q)\\ge\\log(1/r)-o_*.\n\\]\nThese statements hold anew on every further dense tested event.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(s(T)=\\mathbb P(A\\mid T)\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_Qs(T)^{-1}\\le\\mathbb P(A)^{-1},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_Q\\log s(T)^{-1}\\le\\log\\mathbb P(A)^{-1}=o_*.\n\\]\nThe shear \\((x,y)\\mapsto(S,y)\\) is \\(T\\)-known and has subpower distortion. Joint \\(r\\)-bin probabilities before restriction are at most \\(Kr^{j+d}\\); after restriction divide by \\(s(T)\\). The support bounds \\(Kr^{-d}\\), \\(Kr^{-j}\\) give the first two entropy identities.</p><p>Conditioning also on \\(l_r\\), the parameter-bin cap gives fine scalar probabilities at most \\(K(q/r)^d\\), while the coarse scalar has only \\(K\\) possibilities. Thus\n\\[\nH_Q(S_q\\mid T,l_r,S_r)\\ge d\\log(r/q)-o_*.\n\\]\nSubtract from the local scalar support upper bound to obtain the mutual information assertion.</p><p>A width-\\(r\\) projection slab meets at most \\(Krq^{-j}\\) tangential \\(q\\)-cubes. Subtracting that logarithmic count from the tangential entropy at \\(q\\) proves the last bound. Each argument uses the new event’s probability, not an inherited conditioned average.</p><p>The proof also uses the exact inequality\n\\[\nH(Z\\mid T,C)\n\\le H(Z\\mid T,B)+H(Z\\mid T,A)+I(A;B\\mid T,C),\n\\]\nobtained by chain rule and enlarging \\(Z\\) to \\((A,Z)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "Theorem \\(5.5\\), Planar dot-product alternative; external input from Wang–Zahl \\([31]\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At least one alternative holds:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For every \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), use the corresponding \\(\\eta,\\delta_0&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(0&lt;p\\le\\delta_0\\), \\(A,B\\subset[0,1]^2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(E=A,B\\),\n\\[\nN_s(E\\cap B(z,w))\\le p^{-\\eta}w/s\n\\qquad(p\\le s\\le w).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At least one alternative holds:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Perpendicular affine lines \\(\\ell,\\ell^\\perp\\) capture at least \\(p^{\\epsilon-1}\\) side-\\(p\\) cells of \\(A,B\\), respectively, in their width-\\(p\\) neighborhoods.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every restricted triple set \\(\\mathcal H\\subset A\\times B\\times B\\), \\(N_p(\\mathcal H)\\ge p^{\\eta-3}\\), has a scale \\(s\\ge p\\) and interval \\(J\\), \\(|J|\\ge p^{-\\eta}s\\), such that\n\\[\nN_s\\bigl(J\\cap\\{a\\cdot(b-b'):(a,b,b')\\in\\mathcal H\\}\\bigr)\n\\ge(|J|/s)^{1-\\epsilon}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The paper invokes the restatement of Wang–Zahl’s Theorem \\(5.2\\) at the beginning of their Section \\(8\\), together with their Definition \\(4.2\\), using the numbering of arXiv:2210.09581v2. Only the upper covering condition is assumed; the expansion alternative concerns a restricted triple set.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.4", "orig": "Proposition \\(5.4\\), Scalar-normal first jet", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A dense single-time label restriction admits a fixed bounded time-only row \\(A(t)\\) such that, for retained active \\(u,v\\) in a designated \\(H\\)-interval, \\( |F(v)-F(u)-(v-u)A(u)|\\le KH^{11/10}. \\) For …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use scalar-normal data.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite nested hierarchy of positive-power dyadic time lengths, finest \\(H_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\zeta\\le H_*^3/K\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A dense single-time label restriction admits a fixed bounded time-only row \\(A(t)\\) such that, for retained active \\(u,v\\) in a designated \\(H\\)-interval,\n\\[\n|F(v)-F(u)-(v-u)A(u)|\\le KH^{11/10}.\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\nJ_1(l,t)=x_l'(t)-F(t)y_l'-A(t)y_l(t),\n\\]\nevery later dense tested event as in Lemma \\(8.2\\) satisfies\n\\[\nH(J_1(l,t)_r\\mid T,S_l(t)_r)=o_*\n\\qquad(r\\ge H_*^{(1-d)/64}).\n\\]\nThe row and single-time restriction are fixed before later tuples and events.</p>", "proof": "<p>Insert intermediate lengths with \\(H'\\ge H^{33/32}/2\\). Before fitting each component at each length, discard times of instantaneous mass below a reciprocal subpower and blocks with insufficient mass. Surviving blocks have mass at least \\(H/K\\), and surviving active times have an instantaneous lower floor.</p><p>In a block \\(t=t_I+Hu\\), choose an active \\(t_*\\) by averaging. Set\n\\[\nF_*=F(t_*),\\quad B_l=x_l(t_I)-F_*y_l(t_I).\n\\]\nThe occupied \\(H\\)-bins \\(C\\) of \\(B_l\\) number at most \\(KH^{-d}\\), and each has prior mass at most \\(KH^d\\). In a good group divide by \\(H^d\\), and put\n\\[\nY_l=y_l(t_I),\\quad z_l=(B_l-b_C)/H,\\quad\nq_l=x_l'(t_I)-F_*y_l',\n\\]\n\\[\n\\varphi(u)=(F(t_I+Hu)-F_*)/H,\\qquad\nL_l(u)=z_l+uq_l-\\varphi(u)Y_l.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\frac{S_l(t_I+Hu)-b_C}{H}\n=L_l(u)+H(u^2x_{2,l}-u\\varphi(u)y_l').\n\\]\nAt \\(p=H^{1/4}\\) and \\(p_0=H^{1/8}\\), the error is negligible, scalar supports have exponent \\(d\\), and joint \\((Y,L)\\)-bin masses are at most \\(Kp^{j+d}\\). Residual comparison gives\n\\[\n|q_l-q_{\\tilde l}|\\le K(|Y_l-Y_{\\tilde l}|+H).\n\\]</p><p>Slice the other \\(j-1\\) coordinates of \\(Y\\) at \\(p\\). The remaining coordinate \\(v\\) predicts \\(q\\) by an approximately Lipschitz function \\(G(v)\\). After translation,\n\\[\nL_l^a(u)=z_l+uG(v_l)-\\varphi_1(u)v_l+O(Kp).\n\\]\nChoose \\(u_0\\) by averaging. Scalar-bin collisions at \\(u\\), with one starting scalar bin fixed at \\(u_0\\), give mass at least \\(p^{2d}/K\\) and\n\\[\n(u-u_0)(G(v_l)-G(v_{l'}))\n-(\\varphi_1(u)-\\varphi_1(u_0))(v_l-v_{l'})\n=L_{l'}^a(u_0)-L_l^a(u_0)+O(Kp).\n\\]\nEach \\((u,v_l,v_{l'})\\)-cell has mass at most \\(Kp^{3+2d}\\). Prune time bins with too little active collision-time length, retaining their pre-quadrant time sets.</p><p>Apply Lemma \\(8.3\\) to\n\\[\na=(u-u_0,-\\varphi_1(u)+\\varphi_1(u_0)),\\qquad\nb=(G(v_l),v_l),\\quad b'=(G(v_{l'}),v_{l'}).\n\\]\nThe graphs satisfy its upper covering condition and the restricted triple count is at least \\(p^{-3}/K\\). Expansion contradicts scalar exponent \\(d&lt;1-\\epsilon\\). The line alternative supplies affine approximation to \\(\\varphi_1\\) with error \\(Kp\\) on a time set of length \\(p^\\epsilon/K\\). Let \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\) slowly. Restore all current labels at those prepared active times, using their instantaneous floors. Repeat components and hierarchy lengths.</p><p>The resulting affine fits \\(P_I\\) satisfy \\(|F-P_I|\\le KH^{5/4}\\). Comparing child and parent fits at two separated fitting times gives slope difference\n\\[\nK H^{5/4}/H'\\le KH^{7/32}.\n\\]\nThe finest slope defines \\(A(t)\\), yielding the \\(H^{11/10}\\) estimate.</p><p>At the finest block, the normalized scalar is approximately affine with slope \\(k_l=q_l-A_IY_l\\). If every interval of width \\(p_0^\\gamma\\), \\(\\gamma=(1-d)/4\\), had power-small slope mass, prune short labels and light \\(Y\\)-cells. Close-slope collisions are power-small; separated slopes can collide only for time length \\(Kp_0^{1-\\gamma}=o(p_0^d/K)\\). This contradicts the scalar collision lower bound. Retain one heavy slope interval per group.</p><p>Since\n\\[\nJ_1-k_l\n=H(2ux_{2,l}-\\varphi(u)y_l'-uA_Iy_l')=O(KH),\n\\]\nfine scalar data determine a subpower list of \\(J_1\\)-bins at width \\(H^{(1-d)/64}\\). Coefficient bins also determine such lists. Apply the entropy inequality in Lemma \\(8.2\\) and its mutual-information estimate anew on every later tested event.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Theorem \\(6.1\\), Isotropic improvement; Corollary \\(6.9\\), Zero horizon in the scalar model", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every substantial residual admits, on a subsequence, a packet \\(Q_s\\) of thickness \\(a=N^{-s}\\), time \\(h\\sim_{\\log,N}a^k\\), and a permitted quadratic candidate of thickness \\( a_2=ah^{e_2},\\qquad e_2>0, \\) with …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use prepared tangent data of Proposition \\(7\\), \\(0&lt;k\\le1\\), \\(0\\le d&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Either version \\(1\\), \\(j=1\\), or version \\(2\\), \\(j=2\\), \\(\\ell=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n\\[\nz=(t,y),\\quad m=1+j,\\quad v=(1,V),\\quad W=(z,X).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every substantial residual admits, on a subsequence, a packet \\(Q_s\\) of thickness \\(a=N^{-s}\\), time \\(h\\sim_{\\log,N}a^k\\), and a permitted quadratic candidate of thickness\n\\[\na_2=ah^{e_2},\\qquad e_2&gt;0,\n\\]\nwith whole-block value and derivative upper bounds \\(Ka_2,K\\), curvature at most \\(K/a_2\\), derivative lower bound \\(K^{-1}\\) on counted incidences, and counted mass at least\n\\[\n\\frac{h\\nu_Q}{K}(a_2/a)^d.\n\\]\nConsequently every version-\\(1\\) exact problem in \\(\\mathcal C(d)\\), for fixed admissible angular parameters and \\(d&lt;1\\), has horizon zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Starting anew on any substantial residual, Lemmas \\(9.1\\) and \\(9.2\\) give physical support, parameter estimates, and a substantial frozen graph law. Lemma \\(9.4\\) makes qualification failure relatively \\(o(1)\\). Lemma \\(9.5\\) interpolates on good states and restores their prepared edges. Lemma \\(9.6\\) gives an approximately conservative potential and heavy intercept groups. Lemma \\(9.9\\) restores admissible quadratic degree with candidate gain \\(e_2ks&gt;0\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(7\\) converts these residual candidates into a forbidden actual improvement. Thus the isotropic case is excluded. If a bad version-\\(1\\) problem existed, Proposition \\(6\\) would produce precisely this version-\\(1\\) tangent setting; hence version-\\(1\\) horizons are zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Section \\(6.2\\), physical support and parameter masses", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After negligible finite-grid preparation, \\( \\#W_p\\le Kp^{-(m+d)},\\quad \\lambda(W_p)\\le Kp^{m+d},\\quad \\lambda(z_p)\\le Kp^m. \\) Hereditary support counts are …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Incidence submeasures satisfy \\(d\\lambda=G_{\\mathrm{wt}}d\\nu\\,dt\\), \\(0\\le G_{\\mathrm{wt}}\\le K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">True isotropic packets at thickness \\(p\\), time \\(H\\sim_{\\log,N}p^k\\), have full assignment mass \\(\\nu_Q\\sim_{\\log,N}p^dH^j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B_p\\) bins coefficients of \\(X=x_0+tx_1+t^2x_2\\) and \\(y=y_0+tV\\); extra indices are not binned.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After negligible finite-grid preparation,\n\\[\n\\#W_p\\le Kp^{-(m+d)},\\quad\n\\lambda(W_p)\\le Kp^{m+d},\\quad\n\\lambda(z_p)\\le Kp^m.\n\\]\nHereditary support counts are\n\\[\n\\#\\{W_p\\subset W_{w_0}\\}\\le K(w_0/p)^{m+d},\n\\qquad\n\\#\\{W_p\\text{ above }z_p\\}\\le Kp^{-d}.\n\\]\nOne fixed preceding long-line subprior \\(\\nu^\\circ\\) satisfies\n\\[\nK^{-1}p^{j+d}\\pi([V]_p)\n\\le\\nu^\\circ(B_p)\\le Kp^{j+d}\\pi([V]_p),\n\\]\nand \\((W_p,[V]_p)\\) admits only \\(K\\) participating coefficient bins, hence only \\(K\\) \\(X'\\)-bins at width \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Packet graph entries are holomorphic algebraic predictions \\(X=f(z)+O(Kp)\\). Delete light entries and short line portions using full \\(H\\nu_Q\\) budgets. Lemma \\(4.1\\) on long chords gives\n\\[\n|D_vf-X'|\\le Kp/H.\n\\]\nSince \\(k\\le1\\), this is bounded. A power-large gradient would predict velocity in a power-thin affine strip, contradicting Lemma \\(7.9\\). Remez extends \\(|\\nabla f|\\le K\\) throughout used interiors.</p><p>Each packet occupies at most \\(K(H/p)^m\\) physical cells. Sum packet counts and \\(H^{-1}\\) blocks to obtain the support bound. Delete globally light cells; the mass ceilings then imply hereditary support counts.</p><p>The prepared joint caps give\n\\[\n\\lambda(I,\\ y\\text{-box of volume }J_y,\\ X\\text{-interval }w_0,\\ V\\in J)\n\\le K|I|J_yw_0^d\\pi(J).\n\\]\nLemma \\(7.5\\) gives fixed-time active-prior versions. Restrict the prior to lines of inverse-subpower total occupation, defining \\(\\nu^\\circ\\), and freeze it.</p><p>For an affine \\((y_0,V)\\)-bin \\(\\alpha\\), these caps give\n\\[\n\\nu^\\circ(\\alpha)\\le Kp^j\\pi([V]_p)\n\\]\nand scalar coefficient-ball caps with an additional \\(r^d\\). If the lower coefficient-bin bound failed by a fixed power on substantial residual mass, choose an offending \\(\\alpha\\), divide by \\(p^j\\pi([V]_p)\\), and apply Lemma \\(8.1\\), using the scalar support count. This gives a heavy coefficient bin, a contradiction. Relax deficiencies slowly.</p><p>Finally delete coefficient-bin/physical-cell entries below \\(p\\nu^\\circ(B_p)/K_1\\). Their budgets sum subpowerly. The joint cell cap divided by these floors bounds the number of compatible bins by \\(K\\). Interpolate required neighboring grid widths only after their endpoint preparations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Proposition \\(6.2\\), Physical velocity graph", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(\\kappa>0\\), depending only on \\(m,1-d\\), and a bounded row \\(G=(G_0,G_y)\\), constant on each \\(W_b\\), such that a substantial frozen reference family satisfies \\( |X'(t)-G(W_b)\\cdot v|\\le b^\\kappa. \\) At …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Lemma \\(9.1\\) and sufficiently fine fixed-power \\(b\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\), depending only on \\(m,1-d\\), and a bounded row \\(G=(G_0,G_y)\\), constant on each \\(W_b\\), such that a substantial frozen reference family satisfies\n\\[\n|X'(t)-G(W_b)\\cdot v|\\le b^\\kappa.\n\\]\nAt every required \\(s_0\\ge b^\\kappa\\), a coarse physical cell \\(W_{s_0}\\) contains only \\(K\\) width-\\(s_0\\) row bins of \\(G\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use determinant multilinear Kakeya:\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}\n\\left(\\sum_{T_i}\n|\\det(e_{T_1},\\ldots,e_{T_n})|\n\\prod_iw_{T_i}1_{T_i}\\right)^{1/(n-1)}\n\\le C_nr^n\\prod_iM_i^{1/(n-1)}.\n\\]\nThis is the cited Carbery–Valdimarsson estimate, extended to line measures by rounding.</p><p>Put \\(n'=m+1\\), choose \\(0&lt;\\theta&lt;(1-d)/3\\), \\(0&lt;\\tau&lt;\\theta/(n'-1)\\). If no \\(m\\)-plane captures a fixed large fraction of lifted directions \\((v,X')\\) within \\(b^\\tau\\), successive samples give determinant at least \\(b^\\theta\\) with positive probability.</p><p>Group bad cells into cubes of side \\(w_0\\sim b^{2/3}\\), prune short portions, and approximate trajectories there by affine tangents. Position error \\(Kw_0^2\\ll b\\) and direction error \\(Kw_0\\ll b^\\theta\\). If retained cell masses are \\(m_E\\) and cube mass is \\(M\\), multilinear Kakeya gives\n\\[\n\\sum_E\\left(b^\\theta(m_E/b)^{n'}/K\\right)^{1/(n'-1)}\n\\le K(M/w_0)^{n'/(n'-1)}.\n\\]\nPower mean forces at least \\(K^{-1}b^\\theta(w_0/b)^{n'}\\) cells, exceeding Lemma \\(9.1\\)’s \\(K(w_0/b)^{m+d}\\).</p><p>Thus lifted directions mostly lie near an \\(m\\)-plane. Base breadth supplies a well-conditioned spanning \\(m\\)-tuple, making the plane a bounded graph. Freeze its captured and renewed reference law. For each row bin choose a fine-state spanning witness tuple of product-palette weight at least \\(K^{-1}\\). For a fixed tuple, the \\(X'\\)-lists of Lemma \\(9.1\\) allow only \\(K\\) row bins. Summing product-palette weights bounds the row-list count.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.4\\), A common choice from local lists", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A restriction retains in every world at least \\( \\prod_{i=1}^L(4^DK_i)^{-1} \\) of its mass and satisfies \\( |Y(e)-Y(\\widehat e)| \\le C_{D,q}(A+B)(|Z(e)-Z(\\widehat e)|+r_*). \\) If \\(Z,Y\\) are discrete-state functions …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mu\\) is an unnormalized finite measure on finitely many worlds.</p><p class=\"li\">Incidences carry \\(Z\\in\\mathbb R^D\\), \\(Y\\in\\mathbb R^q\\), \\(|Y|\\le B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Tested widths are \\(1=s_1&gt;\\cdots&gt;s_L=r_*\\), with \\(A=\\max_i s_i/s_{i+1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In each world and each \\(Z\\)-cell of width \\(s_i\\), occurring \\(Y\\)-values meet at most \\(K_i\\) cells of that width.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A restriction retains in every world at least\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=1}^L(4^DK_i)^{-1}\n\\]\nof its mass and satisfies\n\\[\n|Y(e)-Y(\\widehat e)|\n\\le C_{D,q}(A+B)(|Z(e)-Z(\\widehat e)|+r_*).\n\\]\nIf \\(Z,Y\\) are discrete-state functions, whole states are retained or removed.</p>", "proof": "<p>At each width, color coordinate cells by their indices modulo \\(4\\), retain a heaviest color in each world, then retain a heaviest \\(Y\\)-cell in each retained coordinate cell. The mass losses multiply as displayed. Same-color distinct cells are separated; at the smallest tested width above a given distance, retained points use the same \\(Y\\)-cell. Use \\(|Y|\\le B\\) for distances above \\(1\\). Slowly growing fixed grids make both the product loss and \\(A\\) subpower.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.4", "orig": "Proposition \\(6.3\\), Polynomial qualification", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(K_{\\mathrm{qual}}=N^{o(1)}\\) and reference exceptions \\(U_N\\) such that \\( \\Lambda_{\\mathrm{ref}}(U_N)/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\mathrm{all})\\to0, \\) and every other reference incidence has a degree-\\(M\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Freeze the substantial post-renewal graph-reference law \\(\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\) and \\(G\\) from Lemma \\(9.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(a,h,M,\\zeta,\\rho,b\\) before graph failure tests, with the scale margins required for Proposition \\(8\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(K_{\\mathrm{qual}}=N^{o(1)}\\) and reference exceptions \\(U_N\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{ref}}(U_N)/\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\mathrm{all})\\to0,\n\\]\nand every other reference incidence has a degree-\\(M\\), bounded-coefficient polynomial approximating \\(G\\) within \\(h^M\\) on reference-labeled times of its indexed trajectory of duration at least \\(h/K_{\\mathrm{qual}}\\), in the same \\(Q_s\\)-block, including that incidence.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), let \\(U_{N,\\epsilon}\\) be occurrences failing coefficient bound \\(N^\\epsilon\\), duration \\(hN^{-\\epsilon}\\), and error \\(h^M\\). A nonvanishing failure fraction is a substantial residual. Keep all meshes and \\(G\\) fixed and renew breadth there.</p><p>Apply Lemma \\(9.3\\) to select whole fine states with\n\\[\n|G(W_b)-G(\\widehat W_b)|\\le K(|W-\\widehat W|+\\rho).\n\\]\nIn each \\(W_\\rho\\), choose a common row \\(G_C\\), and slice by width-\\(p=\\rho s'\\) strips of \\(X-G_Cz\\). Long-duration pruning and base multilinear Kakeya fill at least \\(K^{-1}(\\rho/p)^m\\) base cells per strip. Divide the physical support count by this filling to obtain\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{occupied }p\\text{-strips in a normal }r\\text{-interval}\\}\n\\le K(r/p)^d.\n\\]</p><p>Condition on full coefficient bins \\(B_\\rho\\) under the unchanged \\(\\nu^\\circ\\), subtract central trajectories, and divide positions by \\(\\rho\\). Choose a time-only row \\(F(t)\\) approximating \\(G_y\\); then\n\\[\n|F-G_y|\\le K\\rho,\\qquad |F(t)-F(u)|\\le K(|t-u|+\\rho).\n\\]\nThe coefficient-bin lower bounds of Lemma \\(9.1\\) and fixed-time upper caps give conditional spatial caps \\(K(q'/r')^{j+d}\\).</p><p>For the residual \\(R=x'-F\\widetilde y'\\), fine states have negligible residual range. Block/cell floors and parameter lists bound the number of residual bins for fixed velocity. Appended-entry pruning gives conditional fine-velocity probabilities at most\n\\[\nK\\pi(J)/\\pi(I)\n\\]\ninside a coarse velocity bin \\(I\\). Summing palette weights removes the velocity condition and leaves \\(K\\) residual bins per zoomed cell.</p><p>Apply Lemma \\(9.3\\) again, now to \\(R\\), yielding residual comparison with \\(\\zeta\\)-slack. Select a positive-mass parameter world. Its fixed conditioned prior, scalar strip counts, spatial and parameter-bin caps, row comparison, and residual comparison are precisely the scalar-normal hypotheses. Proposition \\(8\\) fits \\(F\\), hence \\(G_y\\), with error \\(Kh^{M+1/4}\\), and \\(G_0=X'-G_yV+O(b^\\kappa)\\) is fitted as well.</p><p>For late \\(N\\), these occurrences qualify at the fixed tolerance, contradicting their definition. Hence each fixed-\\(\\epsilon\\) failure fraction tends to zero. Let \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\) slowly. The conclusion is relative \\(o(1)\\) failure for this frozen substantial law, not uniform survival under arbitrary later sparse restriction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.5", "orig": "Section \\(6.5\\), Cartesian interpolation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Some \\(Q_s\\) carries state-selected mass at least \\(h\\nu_Q/K\\), restored with its prepared breadth and palette laws, and a bounded-degree polynomial row \\(T(u)\\), \\(u=(z-z_c)/h\\), satisfying …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(p_*=1/k\\), choose \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1/2\\), and set\n\\[\n\\epsilon_T=h^{p_*-1+\\alpha},\\quad\ne_1=\\alpha/2,\\quad a_1=ah^{e_1},\n\\]\n\\[\nc_0=e_1(1-d)/2,\\quad e_2=c_0/3,\\quad a_2=ah^{e_2}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fine meshes and qualification scales are fixed with positive-power margins.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Some \\(Q_s\\) carries state-selected mass at least \\(h\\nu_Q/K\\), restored with its prepared breadth and palette laws, and a bounded-degree polynomial row \\(T(u)\\), \\(u=(z-z_c)/h\\), satisfying\n\\[\n|G(W_b)-T(u)|\\le K\\epsilon_T.\n\\]\nOn its long lines,\n\\[\n|X_l'-T(u_l)\\cdot v_l|\\le K\\epsilon_T\n\\]\nthroughout the \\(Q_s\\)-block. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\#\\{(\\xi\\text{-bin of }u,a_i\\text{-bin of }X)\\}\n\\le K\\xi^{-m}(a/a_i)^d,\\qquad i=1,2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At states \\(D=(\\text{parent},Q_s,W_b)\\), remove those with qualification-failure fraction above a fixed small threshold. Lemma \\(9.4\\) makes their total mass \\(o(1)\\). A union bound leaves a positive probability of a spanning \\(m\\)-tuple of qualified witnesses. Palette averaging fixes one well-conditioned tuple of velocity bins on inverse-subpower state mass in a heavy packet.</p><p>Use its directions as product-grid axes. On each grid fiber, qualified polynomial fits have only \\(K\\) coefficient bins at width \\(\\epsilon_T\\): sample \\(M+1\\) separated uniform times in each witness set, use coarse field lists at those times, and interpolate. Independently choose one polynomial bin per fiber and one field bin per cell. Averaging retains inverse-subpower mass satisfying every fiber choice.</p><p>Delete light grid cells. Repeated Jensen gives many complete \\((M+2)^m\\) product configurations. Choose separated anchors and many targets. Tensor Lagrange interpolation defines \\(T\\), and successive fiber interpolation bounds its error at the targets. Cell floors restore mass. The resulting property is statewise; restore all prepared edges of those states.</p><p>Long-line Remez gives the whole-block derivative estimate. Finally count true descendants at thickness \\(a_i\\): each has mass \\(a_i^dh_i^j/K\\), uses at most \\(K(h_i/(h\\xi))^m\\) joint cells, and there are \\(h/h_i\\) subblocks. The scale ratios cancel to give the support count.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.6", "orig": "Proposition \\(6.5\\), Conservativity of the interpolated field; subsequent potential and intercept construction in Section \\(6.7\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\|\\operatorname{skew}DT\\|\\le Kh^{p_*-1+c_0}. \\) The polynomial \\( P(u)=h\\int_0^1T(tu)\\cdot u\\,dt \\) satisfies \\( |\\nabla P-hT|\\le Kah^{c_0}. \\) There are at most \\(K(a/a_2)^d\\) retained heavy trajectory-intercept …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(T,Q_s,a_1,a_2,c_0\\) from Lemma \\(9.5\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\|\\operatorname{skew}DT\\|\\le Kh^{p_*-1+c_0}.\n\\]\nThe polynomial\n\\[\nP(u)=h\\int_0^1T(tu)\\cdot u\\,dt\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n|\\nabla P-hT|\\le Kah^{c_0}.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(K(a/a_2)^d\\) retained heavy trajectory-intercept groups \\(C\\), with mass at least\n\\[\nK^{-1}h\\nu_Q(a_2/a)^d,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|X_l(t)-P(u_l(t))-C|\\le Ka_2\n\\]\nthroughout their \\(Q_s\\)-blocks.</p>", "proof": "<p>If skew were larger, polynomial sublevels and the normalized base density retain high-skew starts. Make \\(2m\\) switches: refresh conditional on the fine state, then resample time on the entire indexed line. Both preserve the normalized incidence law. Remove short lines. Prepared breadth gives well-conditioned bases from the first and last \\(m\\) controls with positive probability.</p><p>Conditional on the full past, the unnormalized good transition obeys\n\\[\n\\mathbb P([V]=J,t\\in dt,\\text{long}\\mid\\text{past})\n\\le K\\pi(J)\\,dt/h.\n\\]\nBackward conditional integration gives product domination, without conditioning on future success.</p><p>Simulate signed base increments and integrals of \\(T\\). Mesh and derivative errors are negligible at \\((\\xi,a_1/h)\\) accuracy. The endpoint map is\n\\[\n\\delta\\mapsto(u_0+A\\delta,R(\\delta)).\n\\]\nFor two control bases \\(I,J\\), a maximal minor divided by \\(\\det A_I\\) equals\n\\[\n\\partial_{j'}R-(\\nabla_IR)^TA_I^{-1}A_{j'}.\n\\]\nIf every minor were small, differentiation at zero would make the cross Hessian arise from a symmetric matrix. Direct differentiation gives\n\\[\n(D^2R(0))_{i,j'}=DT(u_0)[v_i]\\cdot v_{j'},\n\\]\nforcing small skew. Thus a minor has norm at least \\(h^{p_*-1+c_0}/K\\).</p><p>Discard its small sublevel set. Bounded regular multiplicity and change of variables give endpoint-cell measure at most\n\\[\nK\\xi^m(a_1/h)h^{-(p_*-1+c_0)}.\n\\]\nSumming the support count of Lemma \\(9.5\\) makes successful path probability at most\n\\[\nK(a/a_1)^d(a_1/h)h^{-(p_*-1+c_0)}\n\\le Kh^{e_1(1-d)/2}\\to0,\n\\]\ncontradicting positive success.</p><p>The integral formula for \\(P\\) leaves only the skew error in its gradient. Along long lines, \\(X-P\\) varies by \\(Kah^{c_0}\\ll a_2\\). Bin its midpoint intercept at width \\(a_2\\). Each fine state has only boundedly many neighboring groups. Perform small fixed-fraction and long-duration prunings so each surviving group retains breadth and its contributing prior is at most \\(KM/h\\).</p><p>Multilinear Kakeya in the \\(m\\)-dimensional base fills at least \\(K^{-1}\\xi^{-m}\\) cells per group. The joint support count at \\(a_2\\) bounds the number of groups by \\(K(a/a_2)^d\\). Delete light groups and extrapolate their fits on the same block.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.7", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.6\\), Seven-direction tensor tests", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The space of homogeneous quadratics vanishing at all \\(p_i\\) has dimension at most \\(1\\); each nonzero member is nonsingular.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(p_i=(1,v_i)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\), \\(1\\le i\\le7\\), and every triple is independent.</p><p class=\"li\">A symmetric rank-\\(r\\) tensor corresponds to \\(F_T(x)=T(x,\\ldots,x)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Three-copy tests mean \\(T(p_i,p_i,p_i,\\cdot,\\ldots,\\cdot)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The space of homogeneous quadratics vanishing at all \\(p_i\\) has dimension at most \\(1\\); each nonzero member is nonsingular. The cubic map\n\\[\nT\\mapsto(T(p_i,p_i,\\cdot))_{i=1}^7\n\\]\nis injective. If a nonzero quadratic \\(q\\) vanishes at all seven points, the three-copy kernels at ranks \\(3,4,5\\) are\n\\[\nq(\\mathbb R^3)^*,\\qquad\\mathbb Rq^2,\\qquad\\{0\\}.\n\\]\nUnder \\(|p_i|\\le A\\), \\(|\\det(p_i,p_j,p_k)|\\ge A^{-1}\\), inverse estimates cost \\(CA^C\\), with the rank-\\(4\\) statement restricted to the conditioned exact-conic family.</p>", "proof": "<p>A line contains at most two tested projective points, so a singular conic cannot contain five. In coordinates given by three points, quadratics through them have form \\(ax_0x_1+bx_0x_2+cx_1x_2\\); two further points give independent conditions. Thus five determine a unique nonsingular conic.</p><p>For a cubic with all two-copy tests zero, each first partial is a multiple of \\(q\\), if such \\(q\\) exists. Euler gives \\(F=qL\\), and \\(\\nabla F(p_i)=L(p_i)\\nabla q(p_i)=0\\). Three independent points force \\(L=0\\).</p><p>A cubic vanishing at seven points on a nonsingular conic is divisible by \\(q\\): after complex change to \\(x_0x_2-x_1^2=0\\), its restriction under \\((s^2,st,t^2)\\) is a binary sextic with seven zeros. For quartics, all first derivatives are divisible by \\(q\\); Euler and relative primeness force \\(F\\in\\mathbb Rq^2\\). For quintics, apply the quartic conclusion to first derivatives, then differentiate \\(q^2L\\) to force \\(L=0\\).</p><p>On the compact semialgebraic conditioned families, the relevant least positive singular values are positive, with constant kernel dimensions. Their semialgebraic dependence on \\(A\\) gives reciprocal-polynomial lower bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.8", "orig": "Lemma \\(6.7\\), Restoration of an approximate real conic", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( |\\det Q|\\ge cA^{-C}, \\) and exact real zeros \\(p_i'=(1,v_i')\\) exist with \\( |p_i'-p_i|\\le CA^C\\epsilon. \\) For sufficiently small \\(\\epsilon\\), triple determinants remain at least \\((2A)^{-1}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The points satisfy the conditioned bounds of Lemma \\(9.7\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(q(x)=x^TQx\\) is real, \\(A^{-1}\\le\\|q\\|\\le A\\), and \\(|q(p_i)|\\le\\epsilon\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\epsilon\\) is below a sufficiently large fixed reciprocal power of \\(A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n|\\det Q|\\ge cA^{-C},\n\\]\nand exact real zeros \\(p_i'=(1,v_i')\\) exist with\n\\[\n|p_i'-p_i|\\le CA^C\\epsilon.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small \\(\\epsilon\\), triple determinants remain at least \\((2A)^{-1}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For normalized \\(q\\), the compact-family function\n\\[\n|\\det Q|+\\sum_i|q(p_i)|\n\\]\nhas a positive minimum, since its vanishing would give a singular conic through seven points. Semialgebraicity gives a reciprocal-polynomial bound. Thus \\(Q\\) has a controlled least singular value. Euler’s identity implies a controlled nonzero spatial gradient \\(\\nabla_vq(1,v_i)\\). Along its unit direction, the quadratic changes sign within distance \\(2|q(p_i)|/|\\nabla_vq(1,v_i)|\\). The intermediate value theorem gives a real zero with first coordinate \\(1\\). Multilinearity controls determinant perturbations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.9", "orig": "Proposition \\(6.8\\), Degree restoration", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The potential fit yields a permitted quadratic candidate with the mass and thickness in Proposition \\(9\\), without reducing \\(e_2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the heavy potential groups of Lemma \\(9.6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(0&lt;e_2&lt;1/24\\), \\(a_2/a=h^{e_2}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The potential fit yields a permitted quadratic candidate with the mass and thickness in Proposition \\(9\\), without reducing \\(e_2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In version \\(1\\), long-line Remez gives \\(|D_v^3P|\\le Ka_2\\). Four separated velocity directions, fixed by conditional sampling and palette averaging on common-state mass, determine a binary cubic. Transfer to common bases and apply polynomial Remez. Thus \\(\\|\\nabla^3P\\|\\le Ka_2\\); the quadratic Taylor polynomial of \\(P+C\\) gives the test.</p><p>In version \\(2\\), consider\n\\[\nP_*(z,w)=P(u)+C,\\qquad z=z_c+hu.\n\\]\nIts discriminant is positive and controlled on observed points. Heavy mass divided by the base ceiling gives inverse-subpower volume; Remez controls discriminant coefficients. Stable cores and signs supply real simple branches \\(f\\) with \\(|w-f(u)|\\le Ka_2\\). Small fixed-fraction and duration prunings retain a choice of mass\n\\[\nK^{-1}h\\nu_Q(a_2/a)^d.\n\\]</p><p>Set\n\\[\nR(u)=P_s(z_c+hu,f(u))/a.\n\\]\nQuadratic Taylor expansion in \\(w\\) bounds \\(R\\) on witnesses by \\(K\\); Lemma \\(4.1\\) bounds all fixed derivatives before tensor inversion. Long chords give\n\\[\n|D_v^2f|\\le Ka_2,\\qquad |D_v^3R|\\le Ka_2/a.\n\\]\nLet \\(S_0=\\inf_{L\\text{ affine}}\\|f-L\\|\\). All derivatives of \\(f\\) of order at least \\(2\\) are at most \\(KS_0\\). Seven general-position velocity bins with common-state witnesses give, after transfer and Remez,\n\\[\n|D_{v_i}^2f|\\le K(a_2+S_0h),\\qquad\n|D_{v_i}^3R|\\le Ka_2/a.\n\\]\nDifferentiate the first tests and use Lemma \\(9.7\\):\n\\[\n\\|\\nabla^3f\\|\\le K(a_2+S_0h).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(S_0\\le a_2/h\\), a quadratic Taylor fit to \\(f\\) gives \\(w-f_2\\). Otherwise let \\(L'\\) be the affine Taylor polynomial of \\(f\\) at \\(u_c\\) and put\n\\[\nq(x)=\\frac1{2S_0}\\nabla^2f(u_c)[x,x].\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n(f-L')/S_0=q(u-u_c)+O(Kh),\\quad\nK^{-1}\\le\\|q\\|\\le K,\\quad |q(v_i)|\\le Kh.\n\\]\nLemma \\(9.8\\) shifts directions by \\(Kh\\) to exact real conic zeros. Independent derivative bounds for \\(R\\) make this shift negligible relative to \\(a_2/a\\). Exact-conic tensor kernels give\n\\[\nR=R_2+qL_1+\\lambda_0q^2+O(Ka_2/a).\n\\]\nThe coefficients of \\(R_2,L_1,\\lambda_0\\) are bounded by \\(K\\).</p><p>Put \\(H'=(w-L'(u))/S_0\\), so on witnesses \\(H'=q+O(Kh)\\). Define\n\\[\nP_{\\mathrm{new}}\n=P_s-a(R_2+H'L_1+\\lambda_0(H')^2).\n\\]\nThis is quadratic in \\((z,w)\\), and has value at most \\(Ka_2\\). Its derivative perturbations are\n\\[\n\\partial_w(P_s-P_{\\mathrm{new}})\n=\\frac a{S_0}(L_1+2\\lambda_0H'),\n\\quad\n\\partial_{ww}(P_s-P_{\\mathrm{new}})\n=\\frac{2a\\lambda_0}{S_0^2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(S_0&gt;a_2/h\\), the first is \\(Kh^{1-e_2}\\ll K^{-1}\\), preserving the old lower derivative, and curvature is at most \\(K/a_2\\).</p><p>Finally extrapolate value and derivative upper bounds from the earlier \\(h/K\\)-witness sets to the same \\(h\\)-block. Along trajectories these are polynomials of degree at most \\(2\\) and \\(1\\). No extrapolation to unit parent time and no bound for the hidden affine part are used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.7\\), Affine–skew row fit. Supporting original labels: Lemma \\(7.1\\), Assumption \\(7.2\\), Lemmas \\(7.3\\)–\\(7.5\\), Proposition \\(7.6\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "A restriction of mass at least \\(K^{-1}\\) satisfies \\( T=B+\\lambda Jz+O(KE),\\qquad J(t,Z)=(-Z,t),\\qquad |B|+|\\lambda|\\le K, \\) with an enlarged subpower factor.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(z(t)=(t,Z(t))\\) is affine with velocity \\((1,v)\\), and bounded positions and velocities.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mu\\) has mass at least \\(K^{-1}\\) and is dominated by \\(K\\) times a trajectory prior times Lebesgue time.</p><p class=\"li\">At a fine fixed-power mesh \\(\\xi\\), a velocity probability \\(\\pi\\) satisfies \\(\\pi(I)\\le K|I|^S\\), \\(S&gt;0\\), for \\(\\xi\\le|I|\\le1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Joint cells satisfy\n\\[\n\\mu(z_\\xi,v_\\xi)\\le K\\xi^2\\pi(v_\\xi).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A row \\(T\\in[-K,K]^2\\) has at most \\(K\\) bins of width \\(E\\ge\\xi\\) per position cell.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(T(1,v)\\) lies within \\(KE\\) of a trajectory-dependent constant.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A restriction of mass at least \\(K^{-1}\\) satisfies\n\\[\nT=B+\\lambda Jz+O(KE),\\qquad\nJ(t,Z)=(-Z,t),\\qquad |B|+|\\lambda|\\le K,\n\\]\nwith an enlarged subpower factor.</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume \\(\\xi\\le E\\le1\\), and set \\(V_0=(\\xi/E)T\\). Occupied \\((z,V_0)_\\xi\\)-cells number at most \\(K\\xi^{-2}\\). For direction \\(u_d=(1,v_d)\\), use\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{u_d}(z,V_0)=(z\\cdot Ju_d,V_0\\cdot u_d).\n\\]\nBoth coordinates are nearly constant along labeled lines. Freeze a covering law and prune short lines; disjoint long witnesses imply each direction’s projection uses at most \\(K\\xi^{-1}\\) cells.</p><p>Choose comparable-weight slope bins, normalize, and freeze a point-mass law. Delete light point cells and point-direction edges. Many surviving points have subpower degree, and every selected point retains a frozen mass floor.</p><p>Separated slope triples exist by the interval cap. Fix two basis slopes and many third slopes. Their projections form endpoint sets \\(A,B\\), and common-neighbor points define original graphs. Writing\n\\[\nu_d=c_1u_1+c_2u_2,\\qquad\n\\kappa=c_2/c_1=\\frac{v_d-v_1}{v_2-v_d},\n\\]\nthe third projection yields the small restricted sumset. The ratio law has a positive-exponent interval cap. Invertibility of the two projection directions gives bounded endpoint multiplicity and the first-coordinate rectangle cap.</p><p>Apply Lemma \\(10.5\\). The selected original edges correspond to many surviving point cells, on which\n\\[\nV_0\\cdot u_i=b_i+\\lambda_0z\\cdot Ju_i+O(K\\xi),\\quad i=1,2.\n\\]\nInvert the basis and undo scaling, obtaining \\(T=B+\\lambda Jz+O(KE)\\). Restore all frozen incidences in these point cells; their floors give dense mass, regardless of direction.</p><p>If \\(\\lambda\\) were power-large, bounded \\(T\\) would confine this dense base mass to a ball of radius \\(K/|\\lambda|\\), whose joint-cell cap bounds mass by \\(K(K/|\\lambda|+\\xi)^2\\), a contradiction. Then one occupied point bounds \\(B\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.1\\), Three paths retain graph edges", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(A'\\subset A,B'\\subset B\\) with \\( |A'|\\ge c\\theta a_0,\\quad |B'|\\ge c\\theta b_0, \\) \\( |G\\cap(A'\\times B')|\\ge c\\theta^2a_0b_0, \\qquad |A'+B'|\\le C_0\\theta^{-5}|C|^3/(a_0b_0). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(A,B\\) are finite nonempty subsets of an abelian group, \\(|A|=a_0\\), \\(|B|=b_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\subset A\\times B\\), \\(|G|\\ge\\theta a_0b_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(C=\\{x+y:(x,y)\\in G\\}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(A'\\subset A,B'\\subset B\\) with\n\\[\n|A'|\\ge c\\theta a_0,\\quad |B'|\\ge c\\theta b_0,\n\\]\n\\[\n|G\\cap(A'\\times B')|\\ge c\\theta^2a_0b_0,\n\\qquad\n|A'+B'|\\le C_0\\theta^{-5}|C|^3/(a_0b_0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Remove \\(A\\)-vertices of degree below \\(\\theta b_0/2\\). Call an ordered pair bad if its common neighborhood has size below \\(\\tau b_0\\), \\(\\tau=\\theta^3/512\\). For a uniform \\(v_0\\in B\\), \\(U=N(v_0)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E|U|^2\\ge\\theta^2a_0^2/4,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{Bad}(U)\\le\\tau a_0^2.\n\\]\nChoose \\(U\\) with sufficiently few bad pairs, and remove vertices having too many bad partners, giving \\(A'\\). Let \\(B'\\) be vertices with at least \\(\\theta|A'|/4\\) neighbors in \\(A'\\). Degree counting gives the retained edge and size bounds.</p><p>Every \\((u,v)\\in A'\\times B'\\) has at least \\(c\\theta^5a_0b_0\\) three-edge walks \\(u,v_1,u_1,v\\). The map to\n\\[\n(u+v_1,u_1+v_1,u_1+v)\\in C^3\n\\]\nis injective for fixed endpoints and has alternating sum \\(u+v\\). Choose one endpoint pair per sum; their triple sets are disjoint. Count \\(C^3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.3\\), A common pair and scalar closure", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After a subpower index restriction there are \\(\\kappa_0,A_*,B_*\\), retaining at least \\(L^2/K\\) original edges of \\(G_{\\kappa_0}\\), such that, with \\( E=A_*,\\quad F=-\\kappa_0B_*,\\quad R=\\{\\kappa/\\kappa_0\\}, \\) every …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(h=N^{-a}\\), \\(a&gt;0\\), \\(K=h^{-o(1)}\\), \\(L=h^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Planar graph data, original Assumption \\(7.2\\): \\(A,B\\subset h\\mathbb Z^2\\cap[-K,K]^2\\), \\(L/K\\le|A|,|B|\\le KL\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A probability on represented indices \\(\\kappa\\) satisfies \\(K^{-1}\\le|\\kappa|\\le K\\), \\(\\nu(I)\\le K|I|^S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Each \\(G_\\kappa\\subset A\\times B\\) has at least \\(L^2/K\\) edges, restricted sum cover at most \\(KL\\), and uniform-edge rectangle cap \\(Kh^2\\) for first-coordinate \\(h\\)-intervals.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After a subpower index restriction there are \\(\\kappa_0,A_*,B_*\\), retaining at least \\(L^2/K\\) original edges of \\(G_{\\kappa_0}\\), such that, with\n\\[\nE=A_*,\\quad F=-\\kappa_0B_*,\\quad R=\\{\\kappa/\\kappa_0\\},\n\\]\nevery fixed sum\n\\[\nc_1E+\\cdots+c_mE+d_1F+\\cdots+d_mF\n\\]\nhas at most \\(K_{m,H}L\\) \\(h\\)-cells when coefficients are sums of at most \\(H\\) signed products of at most \\(H\\) elements of \\(R\\). Common subpower dilation is permitted. The restricted ratio law retains a positive-exponent interval cap.</p>", "proof": "<p>Round \\(A,\\kappa B\\); multiplicities are subpower. Lemma \\(10.1\\) gives subsets \\(A_\\kappa,B_\\kappa\\) with small full cross sum and many original retained edges. Average intersections of their product rectangles in \\(A\\times B\\). One \\(\\kappa_0\\) has large overlap with a subpower fraction of parameters, yielding large intersections in each endpoint set.</p><p>Use covering-number Ruzsa triangle, fixed-iterate Plünnecke–Ruzsa, and the covering lemma:\n\\[\nN_h(U-W)N_h(V)\\le C N_h(U-V)N_h(V-W),\n\\]\n\\[\nN_h(mU-nU)\\le C_{m,n}K^{C_{m,n}}N_h(U)\n\\]\nunder small self-difference, and cover \\(U\\) by \\(CK\\) translates of \\(V-V+B(0,Ch)\\) under small \\(U+V\\).</p><p>The large intersections transfer small cross sums to the fixed pair:\n\\[\nd_h(E,F)=O(\\log K),\\qquad d_h(E,tF)=O(\\log K),\n\\]\nwhere \\(d_h(U,V)=\\log(N_h(U-V)/\\sqrt{N_h(U)N_h(V)})\\). Triangle inequalities and controlled common dilations give the same for product dilates of \\(E,F\\). Small \\(E+E\\) admits negative signs. Cover each signed product dilate by subpowerly many translates of \\(E-E\\); fixed sums have the claimed cover. A sum-of-products coefficient uses only containment in the corresponding Minkowski sum; no potentially cancelling sum is divided by.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.4\\), Flat generated coefficients", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are fixed integers \\(n,k>0\\) and a subpower \\(R_0\\ge1\\) such that, for independent \\(T_{ij}\\sim\\nu_R\\), \\( C=\\sum_{i=1}^k\\prod_{j=1}^n(T_{ij}/R_0) \\) satisfies \\( \\sup_x\\mathbb P(C\\in[x-h,x+h])\\le C_eh^{1-e}. \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\nu_R\\) is the restricted ratio law of Lemma \\(10.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(e&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are fixed integers \\(n,k&gt;0\\) and a subpower \\(R_0\\ge1\\) such that, for independent \\(T_{ij}\\sim\\nu_R\\),\n\\[\nC=\\sum_{i=1}^k\\prod_{j=1}^n(T_{ij}/R_0)\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\sup_x\\mathbb P(C\\in[x-h,x+h])\\le C_eh^{1-e}.\n\\]\nEvery coefficient value belongs to the scalar closure of Lemma \\(10.2\\), with common dilation \\(R_0^{-n}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Scale \\(\\nu_R\\) into \\([-1,1]\\), obtaining \\(\\lambda\\) with interval cap \\(K'u^S\\). Fix \\(0&lt;s&lt;\\min(S,1)\\), \\(n\\ge2\\), \\(ns&gt;1\\). The smoothed energy obeys\n\\[\nI_s^r(\\lambda)\\le C_sK'\n\\qquad(h\\le r\\le1).\n\\]\nThe cited Orponen–de Saxcé–Shmerkin multiplicative-convolution theorem gives Fourier decay \\(|\\widehat{\\lambda^{\\boxtimes n}}(\\xi)|\\le|\\xi|^{-\\tau}\\) for \\(h^{-e_0}\\le|\\xi|\\le h^{-1}\\), with fixed \\(e_0&lt;e/2\\), after subpower losses are absorbed. Choose \\(k\\tau&gt;2\\). A nonnegative squared-sinc majorant with compact Fourier support gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(C\\in[x-h,x+h])\n\\le Ch\\int_{|\\xi|\\le h^{-1}}\n|\\widehat{\\lambda^{\\boxtimes n}}(\\xi)|^k\\,d\\xi\n\\le Ch^{1-e_0}.\n\\]\nThe numbers of products and sums are fixed before \\(h\\to0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(7.5\\), Weighted parallel strips", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Two parallel strips, one in each endpoint plane, of width \\(h^{1-o(1)}\\), capture \\(h^{o(1)}\\) of \\(\\mu\\)-mass.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(E,F\\) are as in Lemma \\(10.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mu\\) is uniform on at least \\(h^{-2}/K\\) retained original edges after \\(y\\mapsto-\\kappa_0y\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Its first-coordinate rectangle cap is \\(Kh^2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Two parallel strips, one in each endpoint plane, of width \\(h^{1-o(1)}\\), capture \\(h^{o(1)}\\) of \\(\\mu\\)-mass. Their common slope is subpower bounded.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(p(w)\\) be maximal mass in a pair of parallel width-\\(w\\) strips. Suppose \\(p(h^{1-\\epsilon})\\le h^c\\). Draw independent pairs of edges and differences \\(v_i=a_i-a_i'\\), \\(u_i=b_i-b_i'\\). Put \\(q=h^{\\epsilon/2}\\), \\(\\Delta=qh^{1-\\epsilon}/10\\). The rectangle cap and independence imply\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\left(\\max_{r,s\\in\\{v_i,u_i\\}}|r\\wedge s|\\le\\Delta\\right)\n\\le[CKD(q+h)]^m+mp(w)^{m-1}.\n\\]\nChoose fixed \\(m\\) making this at most \\(h^3\\).</p><p>Draw independent flat coefficients \\(C_i,D_i\\) from Lemma \\(10.3\\), with \\(e&lt;\\epsilon/8\\), using the same tuple for two independent sums \\(Z,Z'\\). Scalar closure bounds each conditional sum support by \\(K_mh^{-1}\\) cells, so\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(|Z-Z'|_\\infty\\le h)\\ge h/K_m.\n\\]\nOutside the small-wedge exception, fix two difference vectors with wedge \\(&gt;\\Delta\\), then condition on all but their two coefficients. Collision confines them to a parallelogram meeting \\(O((1+D)/\\Delta+1)\\) \\(h\\)-squares. Flatness gives upper bound\n\\[\nh^3+K_mh^{2-2e}/\\Delta\n=h^3+K_mh^{1+\\epsilon/2-2e},\n\\]\ncontradicting the lower bound.</p><p>Let \\(\\epsilon,c\\downarrow0\\) slowly. Finally the rectangle cap applied to first-coordinate projections of the captured strips prevents an almost vertical common direction: if \\(q_0\\) is its unit tangent,\n\\[\nm_0\\le CK(D|(q_0)_1|+w+h)^2.\n\\]\nThus \\(|(q_0)_1|\\ge h^{o(1)}\\), giving a subpower slope.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.5", "orig": "Proposition \\(7.6\\), Retained-edge planar rigidity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a represented \\(\\kappa_0\\), at least \\(h^{-2}/K_*\\) original edges satisfy \\( |x_2-\\lambda x_1-c_A|+|y_2-\\lambda y_1-c_B|\\le K_*h, \\) where \\(|\\lambda|+|c_A|+|c_B|\\le K_*\\), with a new subpower \\(K_*\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the planar graph data in Lemma \\(10.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a represented \\(\\kappa_0\\), at least \\(h^{-2}/K_*\\) original edges satisfy\n\\[\n|x_2-\\lambda x_1-c_A|+|y_2-\\lambda y_1-c_B|\\le K_*h,\n\\]\nwhere \\(|\\lambda|+|c_A|+|c_B|\\le K_*\\), with a new subpower \\(K_*\\). No fixed-power dependence on the initial \\(K\\) is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(10.2\\) retains original edges and supplies scalar closure; Lemma \\(10.3\\) supplies flat coefficients; Lemma \\(10.4\\) supplies parallel strips. Conditioning the uniform edge law and undoing the controlled endpoint dilation enlarge losses only subpowerly. Bounded endpoint ranges and slope bound control intercepts.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Theorem \\(8.2\\), Scalar mesh projection", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After dense restriction and a subsequence, there are disjoint trajectory labels with a common scale \\(\\sigma/K\\le\\beta\\le K\\), each having a moving affine interval of width \\(K\\beta\\) containing its assigned …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A trajectory probability \\(\\nu\\) carries scalar affine \\(y\\), scalar quadratic \\(x\\), with coefficients bounded by \\(K=N^{o(1)}\\); extra trajectory coordinates are permitted.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mu_{\\mathrm{act}}\\le K\\nu\\,dt\\) has mass at least \\(K^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(a=N^{-s}\\), \\(s&gt;0\\), and the \\((t,y)\\)-mesh has width \\(\\sigma\\sim_{\\log,N}N^{-M}\\), fixed \\(M&gt;s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(c,c^{-1}\\) are polynomially bounded, \\(d&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Every base mesh cell and \\(x\\)-bin of width \\(p=N^{-r}\\), \\(0\\le r\\le s\\), has mass at most \\(K\\sigma^2cp^d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Terminal occupied-cell count satisfies\n\\[\n\\sigma^2\\#\\{(\\sigma,\\sigma,a)\\text{-cells}\\}\\le Kc^{-1}a^{-d}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After dense restriction and a subsequence, there are disjoint trajectory labels with a common scale \\(\\sigma/K\\le\\beta\\le K\\), each having a moving affine interval of width \\(K\\beta\\) containing its assigned \\(y\\)-trajectories throughout unit time, a quadratic \\(F(t,y)\\) with\n\\[\n|x-F(t,y)|\\le Ka\n\\]\nthroughout unit time, and assigned active mass at least \\(ca^d\\beta/K\\). The predictor norm is at most \\(K\\) in adapted moving-interval coordinates.</p><p>If the terminal cap gains \\(KN^{-S_1u}\\) upon adjoining a \\(y'\\)-bin of width \\(N^{-u}\\), for fixed \\(S_1,u_1&gt;0\\) and \\(0&lt;u\\le u_1\\), then \\(\\beta\\sim_{\\log,N}1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(11.1\\)–\\(11.3\\) establish smoothing, zero-cost scalar charts, and full-time original-law recovery, respectively. Their compositions prove every asserted mass, width, fit, and norm property.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "smoothing the mesh law", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Coefficient rounding and independent width-\\(\\sigma\\) noise produce a tempered version-\\(1\\) exact problem with dense marked mass, exact-base ceiling \\(Kcp^d\\), terminal support measure \\(Kc^{-1}a^{-d}\\), and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition \\(11\\)’s hypotheses.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Coefficient rounding and independent width-\\(\\sigma\\) noise produce a tempered version-\\(1\\) exact problem with dense marked mass, exact-base ceiling \\(Kcp^d\\), terminal support measure \\(Kc^{-1}a^{-d}\\), and homogeneous terminal density \\(ca^d\\) in logarithmic order. Its profile obeys the endpoint cap \\(d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Round the five coefficients at \\(\\sigma'\\ll\\sigma a^2\\), with tag weights \\(\\omega_j\\). Mark a time \\(\\sigma\\)-bin \\(I\\) when \\(\\mu_{\\mathrm{act}}(j\\times I)\\ge\\omega_j\\sigma/K_1\\). Unmarked mass is negligible. Add one common product noise law in the five coefficients and retain marked times.</p><p>At fixed \\((t,y)\\), contributing marked tags have total weight at most \\(K\\sigma cp^d\\), using enlarged original cells and the mark threshold. Noisy \\(y\\)-intercept density contributes \\(K/\\sigma\\). Slope noise also gives \\(K\\min(1,\\rho/\\sigma)\\) for a slope interval of width \\(\\rho\\). Optional angular ceilings transfer similarly.</p><p>Every marked noisy terminal cell neighbors an originally active cell, giving the support bound. Homogenize. Dense mass, terminal support, and caps yield\n\\[\nn(s)\\sim_{\\log,N}ca^d,\\qquad\nf(r)\\ge dr-C_0,\\qquad\nf(s)-f(r)\\le d(s-r),\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_0=\\lim\\log_Nc\\). The hypotheses force \\(d\\ge0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "obtaining an angular gain and zero time cost", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The scalar zero-horizon conclusion of Proposition \\(9\\), either directly with the optional angular cap or after full-time slope/intercept cropping, gives dense true assignments with time length \\(q\\ge K^{-1}\\), error …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the noisy problem of Lemma \\(11.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The scalar zero-horizon conclusion of Proposition \\(9\\), either directly with the optional angular cap or after full-time slope/intercept cropping, gives dense true assignments with time length \\(q\\ge K^{-1}\\), error \\(Ka\\), and per-label mass at least \\(qca^d\\beta/K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>With the optional angular estimate, the noisy problem belongs to \\(\\mathcal C(d)\\), and Proposition \\(9\\) gives arbitrarily small horizon exponents.</p><p>Otherwise homogenize slope refinements through \\(U=4(M+s+1)\\). Let \\(R=(1-d)/2\\), and maximize\n\\[\n(1-R)r-f(r,u)+\\epsilon u.\n\\]\nSlope-noise ceilings force a maximizer \\((r_0,u_0)\\) with\n\\[\ns-r_0\\le\\epsilon U/R,\\quad\nu_0\\le M/(1-\\epsilon),\\quad\nf(r_0,u_0)\\le f(s,0)+\\epsilon U.\n\\]\nMaximality gives terminal slope cap \\(1-R\\) and angular gain \\(\\epsilon\\).</p><p>Crop \\(y_0,y'\\) at width \\(G\\sim N^{-u_0}\\), condition by crop probabilities \\(p_C=N^{-\\alpha+o(1)}\\), and rescale \\(y\\). The new profile is\n\\[\nf'(r)=f(r,u_0)+u_0-\\alpha.\n\\]\nThe cropped problem belongs to \\(\\mathcal C(1-R)\\). Apply Proposition \\(9\\), then lift; crop probabilities cancel from floors, giving mass per block at least\n\\[\n\\beta ca^dN^{-\\epsilon U-o(1)}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\epsilon\\) and horizon exponents tend to zero sufficiently slowly along successful subsequences.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "full-time fits and return to the original law", "depth": 1, "snippet": "They yield the labels, predictor bounds, widths, and original active masses in Proposition \\(11\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the assignments from Lemma \\(11.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>They yield the labels, predictor bounds, widths, and original active masses in Proposition \\(11\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The block geometry confines slope to width \\(K\\beta/q\\). Integrate the exact-base cap, including slope-noise gain, under the graph over base area \\(Kq\\beta\\); comparison with its floor forces \\(\\beta\\ge\\sigma/K\\). The optional angular gain excludes every fixed-power deficiency of \\(\\beta\\) from \\(1\\).</p><p>Mass floor divided by base cap supplies inverse-subpower base volume on which \\(|F|\\le K\\). Polynomial Remez bounds its adapted norm. Since \\(q\\ge K^{-1}\\), extrapolate geometry and quadratic line errors to unit time at subpower cost.</p><p>Keep one block carrying dense mass. Average over the product noise law to fix one common jitter vector. Assign every original trajectory of each selected rounded tag to that tag’s label. Undo the common affine/quadratic jitter in centers and predictors. Remaining rounding errors are negligible because \\(\\beta\\ge\\sigma/K\\) and \\(\\sigma'\\ll\\sigma a^2\\).</p><p>Restore each successful marked tag-bin’s entire original active part. The mark threshold returns dense original active mass. Delete light labels using the summed \\(ca^d\\beta\\) budget, and homogenize \\(\\beta\\). Uniform subpower conclusions over eligible bounded-exponent ensembles follow by contradiction against sequences of fixed-power failures.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Proposition \\(8.4\\), Wide-interval improvement. Supporting original label: Lemma \\(8.3\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Some true \\(Q_s\\) admits a quadratic predictor in the full base \\((t,Z,Y)\\) for the bounded quadratic-along-lines signal \\(X\\), fitting throughout its block to \\(Ka_2\\), on incidences of mass at least …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in a finite-positive-narrowness parent, \\(y=(Z,Y)\\), \\(v=Z'\\), with\n\\[\na_s=N^{-s},\\quad h_s=N^{-ks},\\quad\ng_s=N^{-\\ell s},\\quad b_s=h_sg_s.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">True packets have tilted boxes and velocity rows\n\\[\n|Y-Y_Q(t,Z)|\\le Kb_s,\\qquad\n|Y'-A_Q(1,v)|\\le Kg_s.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Pure/joint caps and conditional palette domination are prepared.</p><p class=\"li\">Record \\(\\alpha=([v]_\\chi,[Z(0)]_\\chi)\\), \\(w_\\alpha=\\pi([v]_\\chi)\\chi\\), at sufficiently fine fixed-power \\(\\chi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At depths \\(s,s+u,s+c_2\\), projected full-time quadratic labels \\(\\Phi,\\Phi_1,\\Phi_2\\) have interval scales \\(\\beta_i\\), disjoint assignments, and their mass floors from Proposition \\(11\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(a=a_s\\), \\(a_1=aN^{-u}\\), \\(a_2=aN^{-c_2}\\), \\(0&lt;c_2&lt;(1-d)u/4\\), \\(C_d=10/(1-d)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n\\[\n\\beta_{\\max}\\le Kg_s,\\qquad\nb_s/\\beta_{\\min}+h_s\\beta_{\\max}/\\beta_{\\min}\n\\le N^{-(C_d+3)u}\n\\]\nup to subpower factors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Some true \\(Q_s\\) admits a quadratic predictor in the full base \\((t,Z,Y)\\) for the bounded quadratic-along-lines signal \\(X\\), fitting throughout its block to \\(Ka_2\\), on incidences of mass at least\n\\[\nq_s\\nu_Q(a_2/a)^d/K.\n\\]\nFor the native signal, this is a time-improvement candidate.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition \\(11\\) to Lemma \\(12.3\\). Its angular gain gives full horizontal width up to \\(K\\) and a quadratic fit in \\((t,Z)\\), with normalized mass at least \\((a_2/a)^d/K\\). Restore \\(P_{\\mathrm{nc}}\\), which is quadratic in the full base. Lemmas \\(12.1\\) and \\(12.2\\) supply the simultaneous projected data and subtraction, proving the required whole-block fit and original assignment-relative mass.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Section \\(8.2\\); original label: Lemma \\(8.3\\), Comparison on common stable domains", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Projected labels exist with original active floors \\(w_\\alpha a_s^d\\beta_s/K\\), \\(g_s\\sigma/K\\le\\beta_s\\le Kg_s\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the finite-narrowness setting of Proposition \\(12\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Crop \\((Y(0),Y')\\) at width \\(g_s\\), with joint group prior \\(p_{\\alpha\\gamma}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Apply Proposition \\(11\\) after normalizing \\(Y\\), with \\(c=w_\\alpha g_s/p_{\\alpha\\gamma}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Common domains have time\n\\[\nq\\sim_{\\log,N}\\min(h_s,\\beta_s/g_s)\n\\]\nand moving transverse width \\(m\\sim_{\\log,N}qg_s\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Projected labels exist with original active floors \\(w_\\alpha a_s^d\\beta_s/K\\), \\(g_s\\sigma/K\\le\\beta_s\\le Kg_s\\). On a dense restriction, true and projected graph predictions agree to \\(Ka_s\\) on common stable comparison balls. Given \\((\\alpha,D,Q_s)\\), there are at most \\(K\\) retained \\(\\Phi_s\\), and conversely.</p>", "proof": "<p>Integrating the true joint cap while \\(Z\\) tracks \\(Z_\\alpha(t)\\) bounds projected cells by\n\\[\nK\\sigma^2w_\\alpha g_sp^d.\n\\]\nA true packet meets at most \\(Kh_s/\\chi\\) intercept bins per velocity bin and \\(K\\) compatible transverse crops. Its weighted projected support cost is at most \\(Kq_sh_sb_s\\). Summing with \\(\\nu_Q\\sim a_s^dh_sb_s\\) and assignment budgets gives total \\(Ka_s^{-d}\\). Delete oversized-support and poor-success groups and apply Proposition \\(11\\).</p><p>For comparison, use reference weight \\(T_D=qw_\\alpha a_s^dm\\). Slicing either list gives a subpower summed budget. Delete light entries and stable-ball subentries before pairing; retain separate marginal witnesses. Evaluate true graphs at \\(Z_\\alpha(t)\\); the fine \\(\\chi\\)-error is negligible. Jitter only within sufficiently fine common comparison cells.</p><p>A dyadic discrepancy group of norm \\(M_0\\) has at most \\(K(M_0/a_s)^d\\) indices per color, by summing stored floors under a band cap. Choose a bad group with current pair mass \\(B\\ge(W_1+W_2)/K\\), where \\(W_\\kappa\\) are the separate color reference totals. Index-base ceilings \\(KT_i\\) normalize relative to weights \\(T_i/W_\\kappa\\) with multiplier \\(K\\). Proposition \\(4\\) excludes power-excess discrepancies. Send tolerance slowly to zero. At discrepancy \\(Ka_s\\), the same witness-floor count gives the mutual \\(K\\)-lists.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "long-domain comparison and nonconstant coefficient agreement in Proposition \\(8.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On dense mass, finer projected fits have the form \\( X=F_\\Phi(t,Y)+D_i+O(Ka_i), \\) where \\(|D_i|\\le Ka\\) and there are at most \\(K(a/a_i)^d\\) offsets for fixed coarse data.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use all hypotheses of Proposition \\(12\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On dense mass, finer projected fits have the form\n\\[\nX=F_\\Phi(t,Y)+D_i+O(Ka_i),\n\\]\nwhere \\(|D_i|\\le Ka\\) and there are at most \\(K(a/a_i)^d\\) offsets for fixed coarse data. In normalized \\(Q_s\\)-coordinates\n\\[\nz=(t-t_*,Z-Z_*)/q_s,\\qquad\nU=(Y-Y_Q(t,Z))/b_s,\n\\]\nwrite\n\\[\nF_\\Phi=f_0^\\Phi(z)+f_1^\\Phi(z)U+f_2^\\Phi U^2.\n\\]\nThere are one affine \\(C_1(z)\\) and constant \\(C_2\\), bounded by \\(K\\), such that on dense mass\n\\[\n(f_1^\\Phi(z),f_2^\\Phi)\n=(C_1(z),C_2)+O(Ke),\n\\qquad e=aN^{-(1-d)u/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Compare coarse/fine fits on long domains of time \\(\\rho\\sim\\beta_{\\min}/\\beta_{\\max}\\) and width \\(\\beta_{\\min}\\), retaining each color’s reference weight \\(Aw_\\alpha a_i^d\\). A discrepancy \\(M_0\\ge aN^{C_du}\\) has list exponent at most\n\\[\nd+d/C_d&lt;(1+d)/2&lt;1\n\\]\nrelative to \\(M_0/a\\). Proposition \\(4\\), normalized with separate color reference totals, excludes it. The wide inequalities make the discrepancy nearly constant to error \\(a_i\\) on a short true block and its full fiber box. Floor counts give the offset lists.</p><p>At a fine state \\((Q_s,z,U,X)\\), sample source and target posteriors. The target velocity is dominated by \\(K\\pi\\). For fixed source entry and target velocity, comparison lists give only \\(K\\) coarse target polynomials, independent of their at most \\(KN^{du}\\) offsets.</p><p>If nonconstant coefficient gap exceeds \\(e\\), its derivative exceeds \\(eN^{-\\tau}\\), \\(\\tau=(1-d)u/8\\), except on \\(U\\)-length \\(KN^{-\\tau}\\). Outside that set, monotonicity bounds value-matching length by\n\\[\nKN^{du}a_1N^\\tau/e=KN^{-3(1-d)u/8}.\n\\]\nIntegrate against unnormalized finest-entry source ceilings and the target palette; summed reference budgets make mismatches power-small. Conditional averaging gives common statewise coefficient pairs.</p><p>Select two separated fine velocity bins with common witnesses, use their directions as product-grid axes in \\(z\\), and choose one affine/constant pair per fiber from its \\(K\\)-list. Dense \\(3\\times3\\) product configurations and bilinear interpolation give a bilinear first coefficient and constant second coefficient on dense mass.</p><p>Use the earlier unnormalized palette bound to retain a non-axis velocity residual. Some line has inverse-subpower retained duration and one coarse predictor on inverse-subpower duration. Along it the bilinear fit agrees with an affine function. Its two direction components in the chosen basis are inverse-subpower nonzero, so one-variable Remez bounds the mixed coefficient by \\(Ke\\). Remove it, obtaining affine \\(C_1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "scalar support after subtraction in Proposition \\(8.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The signal \\(X-P_{\\mathrm{nc}}\\), projected to \\((t,Z)\\) in a heavy \\(Q_s\\), satisfies Proposition \\(11\\) at width \\(a_2\\), density prefactor \\(a^{-d}\\), and a positive angular gain.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(P_{\\mathrm{nc}}(z,U)=C_1(z)U+C_2U^2\\) from Lemma \\(12.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The signal \\(X-P_{\\mathrm{nc}}\\), projected to \\((t,Z)\\) in a heavy \\(Q_s\\), satisfies Proposition \\(11\\) at width \\(a_2\\), density prefactor \\(a^{-d}\\), and a positive angular gain.</p>", "proof": "<p>At fixed velocity and fine \\(z\\)-cell, the residual values are\n\\[\nf_0^\\Phi(z)+D_2+O(Ka_2),\n\\]\nbecause \\(e\\ll a_2\\). The coarse predictor list and offset count give at most \\(K(a/a_2)^d\\) residual bins. Append the \\(z\\)-cell/residual state to deep histories and prune light entries; each state’s supporting velocity bins have palette mass at least \\(K^{-1}\\). Integrate the fixed-velocity count against \\(\\pi\\) to remove velocity.</p><p>Normalize mass by \\(q_s\\nu_Q\\). Integrating the old cap across the bounded \\(U\\)-range gives scalar-bin ceiling \\(K(p/a)^d\\), including the angular factor. The prior remains the full true assignment of \\(Q_s\\), conditioned only as needed to bounded participating trajectory coefficients.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition \\(8.5\\), Reduction to the horizontal model. Supporting original labels: Lemmas \\(8.6\\), \\(8.7\\), \\(8.8\\), \\(8.9\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every substantial residual either supplies a strip candidate, or a true-parent normalization yields \\(k=\\ell\\) and, for fixed \\(e_0>0\\), \\( |Y'-Jz\\cdot(1,v)|\\le N^{-e_0}, \\qquad z=(t,Z),\\quad Jz=(-Z,t). \\) At …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in a finite-positive-narrowness parent.</p><p class=\"li\">Projected interval scales are obtained as in Lemma \\(12.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If the wide case fails, a selected scale has\n\\[\n\\beta_s=N^{-B_0+o(1)},\\qquad \\ell s&lt;B_0\\le\\ell s+M.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every substantial residual either supplies a strip candidate, or a true-parent normalization yields \\(k=\\ell\\) and, for fixed \\(e_0&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n|Y'-Jz\\cdot(1,v)|\\le N^{-e_0},\n\\qquad z=(t,Z),\\quad Jz=(-Z,t).\n\\]\nAt sufficiently small required relative depths, whole true boxes have horizontal scale \\(H\\) and transverse scale \\(H^2\\).</p><p>After selecting a parent affine hidden fit and putting \\(x=(w-L)/a_r\\), the residual is affine along trajectories and \\(|x|+|x'|\\le K\\), with arbitrary prescribed finite-mesh pure/joint caps and deep-history state lists. True descendants satisfy\n\\[\n\\nu^\\flat(Q_s)\\sim_{\\log,N}a_s^dh_s^3,\\qquad\nm_{Q_s}=H_s\\nu^\\flat(Q_s),\\qquad\\sum_{Q_s}m_{Q_s}\\le1,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\nu^\\flat\\) is the full prior conditioned on the true parent assignment, not on success of \\(L\\).</p><p>If \\(k\\le1/2\\), the full-time coefficient counts of Lemma \\(13.5\\) hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>If the three projected widths are all full in logarithmic order, Proposition \\(12\\) already gives a candidate. Otherwise Lemma \\(13.1\\) gives a strip candidate or the horizontal model with \\(k=\\ell\\). Lemmas \\(13.2\\)–\\(13.4\\) establish affine hidden fits, bounded residual normalization, fine-state caps, and nested slope bounds. Lemma \\(13.5\\) supplies the additional coefficient estimates when \\(k\\le1/2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Proposition \\(8.5\\), horizontal reduction portion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Either a strip candidate exists or the normalized parent satisfies the horizontal equation and \\(k=\\ell\\), with whole-box \\(H/H^2\\) geometry.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the deficient projected scale in Proposition \\(13\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(r&gt;s\\) with \\((k+\\ell)r&gt;B_0&gt;\\ell r\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Either a strip candidate exists or the normalized parent satisfies the horizontal equation and \\(k=\\ell\\), with whole-box \\(H/H^2\\) geometry.</p>", "proof": "<p>True/projected comparison lists imply only \\(K\\) width-\\(\\beta_s\\) predictions for \\(Y'\\) given \\(Q_r\\) and fine horizontal position/velocity. Normalize \\(Q_r\\); the transverse velocity is predicted at width \\(E=N^{-(B_0-\\ell r)}\\) by deep rows\n\\[\nT=(A_{\\mathrm{deep}}-A_Q)/g_r.\n\\]\nFine-state palette domination supplies separated witness pairs. Interpolation bounds rows by \\(K\\) and gives \\(K\\) row bins per horizontal cell. The horizontal joint cell cap is \\(K\\xi^2\\pi(v_\\xi)\\) after division by the full parent budget. Proposition \\(10\\) gives\n\\[\nT=B+\\lambda Jz+O(KE).\n\\]\nPower-small \\(\\lambda\\) is a strip candidate. Otherwise \\(|\\lambda|\\sim_{\\log,N}1\\); subtract \\(Bz\\), divide the transverse coordinate by \\(\\lambda\\), and obtain the horizontal equation.</p><p>At a small child depth, its fixed true row approximates \\(Jz\\) to \\(K(N^{-\\ell s'}+N^{-e_0})\\). Current floors and prior-times-time domination force active time extent at least \\(H/K\\), so \\(k\\ge\\ell\\). If \\(k&gt;\\ell\\), replace \\(Jz\\) by its value at an active center to obtain a strip candidate. Hence \\(k=\\ell\\).</p><p>For an occupied center \\((z_c,Y_c)\\), use\n\\[\n\\widehat z=(z-z_c)/H,\\qquad\n\\widehat Y=(Y-Y_c-Jz_c\\cdot(z-z_c))/H^2.\n\\]\nThe true row differs from \\(Jz_c\\) by \\(KH\\), so changing centered planes costs \\(KH^2\\). These coordinates contain the entire true box.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(8.6\\), Affine approximation of hidden sheets", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are at most \\(K\\) affine hidden fits per true label, with error \\(K\\Delta\\) throughout its block.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the horizontal model at finitely many sufficiently small relative depths \\(s'\\), including \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Hidden fitting width is \\(\\Delta=a_ra_{s'}\\), up to \\(K\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are at most \\(K\\) affine hidden fits per true label, with error \\(K\\Delta\\) throughout its block. No bound on the original sheet’s affine part is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use stable graph cores; high curvature already gives affine centers. Prune light entries and short same-core/branch line portions using full assignment budgets, retaining their witnesses and inverse-subpower base filling.</p><p>Let \\(M_f\\) be the best affine approximation error. Lemma \\(4.1\\) bounds every fixed derivative of order at least \\(2\\) by \\(KM_f\\). Long chords give actual-direction Hessian tests \\(K\\Delta\\). Conditional palette domination supplies three separated velocities at common fine states. Transfer to horizontal directions with error \\(KM_fN^{-e}\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\mathcal X=\\partial_t-Z\\partial_Y,\\qquad\n\\mathcal Z=\\partial_Z+t\\partial_Y,\\qquad\n\\mathcal T=[\\mathcal X,\\mathcal Z]=2\\partial_Y.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(v\\), \\((\\mathcal X+v\\mathcal Z)^2f\\) is the frozen directional Hessian, because \\((\\mathcal X+v\\mathcal Z)(-Z+tv)=0\\). Interpolation in \\(v\\), then algebraic Remez, controls\n\\[\n\\mathcal X^2f,\\quad\\mathcal Z^2f,\\quad\n(\\mathcal X\\mathcal Z+\\mathcal Z\\mathcal X)f\n\\]\nand fixed derivatives by \\(K(\\Delta+M_fN^{-e})\\).</p><p>The identities\n\\[\n\\mathcal X(\\mathcal X\\mathcal Z+\\mathcal Z\\mathcal X)-2\\mathcal Z\\mathcal X^2\n=3\\mathcal T\\mathcal X,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal Z(\\mathcal X\\mathcal Z+\\mathcal Z\\mathcal X)-2\\mathcal X\\mathcal Z^2\n=-3\\mathcal T\\mathcal Z,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal T^2f=\\mathcal X\\mathcal T\\mathcal Zf-\\mathcal Z\\mathcal T\\mathcal Xf\n\\]\nrecover the full ordinary Hessian. Taylor approximation gives\n\\[\nM_f\\le K\\Delta+KM_fN^{-e}.\n\\]\nAbsorb the second term without assuming a power bound on \\(M_f\\). Extrapolate the affine line difference from its earlier long witnesses to the same full block.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(8.7\\), Residual normalization and fine-state bounds", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(x\\) is affine and \\(|x|+|x'|\\le K\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Select a successful parent affine fit \\(L\\) from Lemma \\(13.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(x=(w-L)/a_r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize incidence mass by parent time times full assigned prior, without renormalizing success.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(x\\) is affine and \\(|x|+|x'|\\le K\\). For arbitrary prescribed finite fixed-power meshes,\n\\[\n\\mu(C,x\\in J_p)\\le K|C|p^d,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mu(C,x\\in J_p,v\\in b_\\chi)\\le K|C|p^d\\pi(b_\\chi).\n\\]\nDeep true labels determine these base and residual bins with \\(K\\) choices, even beyond horizontal-equation accuracy.</p>", "proof": "<p>Freeze the bounded-residual law. At sufficiently deep true levels, prune light graph entries using full true budgets. Their normalized analytic residuals \\((f_R-L)/a_r\\) are bounded on inverse-subpower filled base sets; Lemma \\(4.1\\) bounds their derivatives even if \\(L\\) has large coefficients. Deeper labels therefore recover fine residual states without using horizontal reconstruction.</p><p>Append these states to earlier end histories, whose reference weights sum to the parent budget. Prune light appended entries before conditional division. The persistent joint numerator gives palette domination. Integrate the original hidden cap at width \\(pa_r\\) about the exact offset \\(L\\), with the parent Jacobian and full-prior denominator, to obtain the pure cap. Combining it with the transferred numerator gives the joint cap. Later restrictions preserve these upper bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(8.8\\), Slopes of nested affine fits", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On active boxes, a dense preparation gives \\( |\\nabla_zL_s+(\\partial_YL_s)Jz|\\le K(1+a_s/h_s), \\) \\( |\\partial_YL_s|\\le K(1+a_s/h_s^2). \\) There are \\(K\\) subentries per true label.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the bounded residual \\(x\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(L_s(t,Z,Y)\\) is a true descendant affine prediction, error \\(Ka_s\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On active boxes, a dense preparation gives\n\\[\n|\\nabla_zL_s+(\\partial_YL_s)Jz|\\le K(1+a_s/h_s),\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\partial_YL_s|\\le K(1+a_s/h_s^2).\n\\]\nThere are \\(K\\) subentries per true label.</p>", "proof": "<p>Pair ancestor/child affine entries along a nested depth grid. Delete light pairs against child full-assignment budgets; only \\(K\\) pair choices occur per true child. Their retained full-domain witnesses have inverse-subpower filling. Remez bounds each difference by the ancestor fitting width on the whole child box, hence horizontal slope increment \\(Ka_{\\mathrm{ancestor}}/h_{\\mathrm{child}}\\) and transverse increment \\(Ka_{\\mathrm{ancestor}}/h_{\\mathrm{child}}^2\\).</p><p>Start from the zero prediction and \\(|x|\\le K\\). Telescope over a slowly densifying depth grid. For \\(p=1,2\\), earlier increments are bounded by \\(K(1+a_s/h_s^p)\\); the slowly growing count remains subpower.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.5", "orig": "Lemma \\(8.9\\), Horizontal coefficient count", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On a dense restriction, typical groups have active and prior mass comparable to \\(W_G\\), and \\((t,x)\\)-support at most \\(Ka_s^{-1-d}\\) side-\\(a_s\\) squares.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(k=\\ell\\le1/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix sufficiently small \\(s&gt;0\\), bounded \\(u\\ge1\\), \\(D=a_{us}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(P=(p,q_0)=(Z(0),Y(0))\\), and group by a sufficiently fine velocity bin \\(d_0\\) and \\(P_D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W_G=\\pi(d_0)D^2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On a dense restriction, typical groups have active and prior mass comparable to \\(W_G\\), and \\((t,x)\\)-support at most \\(Ka_s^{-1-d}\\) side-\\(a_s\\) squares. They retain at most \\(Ka_s^{-d}\\) full-time affine coefficient bins of width \\(a_s\\), each with active mass at least \\(W_Ga_s^d/K\\). After time subdivision at \\(\\eta\\ll a_s\\) and summable pruning, group, time subbin, and \\(x_{a_s}\\) determine only \\(K\\) coefficient bins.</p>", "proof": "<p>The horizontal equation gives\n\\[\nZ=p+tv,\\qquad Y=q_0-tp+O(N^{-e_0}).\n\\]\nJoint caps integrated along a group’s moving spatial area \\(KD^2\\) give\n\\[\n\\mu(G,I,x\\text{-band of width }\\varrho)\\le K|I|W_G\\varrho^d.\n\\]\nDelete light groups and poor success fractions using disjointness and \\(\\sum_GW_G\\le K\\).</p><p>Freeze true-label witnesses of mass \\(m_{Q_s}/K\\). Since \\(k\\le1/2\\), Lemma \\(13.4\\) bounds ordinary affine slopes by \\(K\\). Boxes meeting a group motion during an \\(a_s\\)-time bin lie in one enlarged horizontal box of base volume \\(Kh_s^4\\), because \\(D\\le a_s\\le h_s^2\\). Disjoint witness floors \\(h_s^4a_s^d/K\\), charged under the same bounded-residual base cap, bound their number by \\(Ka_s^{-d}\\). Each affine entry supplies \\(K\\) scalar bins, proving the support count.</p><p>Within a typical group, normalize by \\(W_G\\), prune short occupation, and use the band cap to obtain scalar coefficient-ball caps \\(K\\varrho^d\\). Lemma \\(8.1\\), applied anew on any dense residual of light bins, forces a heavy bin. Thus retain only heavy bins and count at most \\(Ka_s^{-d}\\).</p><p>Time-subbin floor numerators are \\(\\eta W_Ga_s^d\\); their sum is subpower. Every entry meeting an \\(x_{a_s}\\)-bin has its entire stored subbin witness inside an enlarged \\(Ka_s\\)-band, so band ceiling divided by floors gives a \\(K\\)-list.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Proposition \\(9.2\\), Critical-rate improvement", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every substantial residual supplies a true \\(Q_s\\) and an affine base predictor whose restored hidden test fits throughout its block at width \\(Ka_ra_{s+c}\\), has hidden derivative \\(1\\), and counted mass in initial …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the prepared horizontal model of Proposition \\(13\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(2k=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose sufficiently small fixed \\(s&gt;0\\) and \\(0&lt;c&lt;ks/2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every substantial residual supplies a true \\(Q_s\\) and an affine base predictor whose restored hidden test fits throughout its block at width \\(Ka_ra_{s+c}\\), has hidden derivative \\(1\\), and counted mass in initial tangent-parent units at least\n\\[\nK^{-1}q_r\\nu(Q_r)m_{Q_s}N^{-dc}.\n\\]\nIt is a time-improvement candidate.</p>", "proof": "<p>Combine Lemma \\(14.2\\)’s upper joint-cell count with Lemma \\(14.3\\)’s lower base-cell count. There are at most \\(K(a/a')^d\\) bins carrying almost all fitted mass. Since retained mass before probability normalization is at least \\(m_{Q_s}/K\\), some bin has mass at least\n\\[\nm_{Q_s}(a'/a)^d/K=m_{Q_s}N^{-dc}/K.\n\\]\nIts offset test fits throughout the block. Restore \\(w=L+a_rx\\) and original mass factor \\(q_r\\nu(Q_r)\\). The resulting affine hidden test has derivative \\(1\\), as required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.1\\), Simultaneous marginal floors", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently large subpower \\(K_1\\), retain at least \\(9M/10\\) so every surviving label has current mass at least \\(T_j/K_1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A finite measure has mass \\(M\\ge K^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Finitely many label families partition incidences up to subpower multiplicity.</p><p class=\"li\">Proposed floor numerators \\(T_j\\) sum to at most \\(K\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large subpower \\(K_1\\), retain at least \\(9M/10\\) so every surviving label has current mass at least \\(T_j/K_1\\). Earlier witnesses and priors remain unchanged.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the fixed-threshold deletion process of Lemma \\(2.3\\). Choose \\(K_1\\) with total thresholds, including multiplicities, below \\(M/10\\). Each deleted label becomes permanently empty and is charged once. Increase finite grids slowly.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "affine primitive and intercept upper count in Proposition \\(9.2\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Some heavy \\(Q_s\\) admits an affine \\(F_*(z,Y)\\), slopes at most \\(K/H_s\\), with \\( |x'-dF_*/dt|\\le Ka \\) on dense mass.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(a=a_s=h_s^2\\), \\(a'=aN^{-c}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Prepare depths \\(s,3s,s+c\\) and sufficiently fine meshes.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Some heavy \\(Q_s\\) admits an affine \\(F_*(z,Y)\\), slopes at most \\(K/H_s\\), with\n\\[\n|x'-dF_*/dt|\\le Ka\n\\]\non dense mass. The midpoint intercept bins \\(C=[(x-F_*)(t_{\\mathrm{mid}})]_{a'}\\) fit throughout the block with error \\(Ka'\\), and satisfy\n\\[\n\\#\\{(\\omega\\text{-base cell},C)\\}\\le K\\omega^{-3}(a/a')^d\n\\]\nin centered \\(Q_s\\)-coordinates.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(13.5\\), with parameter width \\(a=h_s^2\\), gives \\(K\\) \\(x'\\)-bins given \\(Q_s\\), fine horizontal location and velocity. The row\n\\[\nG_*=\\nabla_zL_{3s}+(\\partial_YL_{3s})Jz\n\\]\nis bounded and predicts \\(x'\\) to \\(Ka\\). Deep-history state conditioning and separated palette witnesses turn derivative evaluation lists into \\(K\\) row bins per horizontal cell. Integrating joint caps across transverse width \\(KH_s^2\\) gives the joint cap needed for Proposition \\(10\\).</p><p>Apply that proposition in centered horizontal coordinates:\n\\[\nG_*=B+\\lambda J\\widehat z+O(Ka).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nF_*(z,Y)=(B-\\lambda Jz_c/H_s)\\cdot z+\\lambda Y/H_s\n\\]\nhas the asserted trajectory derivative, using the horizontal equation. These derivatives are constant along each line.</p><p>Thus \\(x-F_*\\) varies by \\(Kah_s\\ll a'\\). Count true descendants \\(Q_{s+c}\\): their number is at most\n\\[\nK(a/a')^d(h_s/h_{s+c})^4,\n\\]\nand each has centered volume at most \\(K(h_{s+c}/h_s)^4\\). At sufficiently fine \\(\\omega\\), these volume factors cancel. Affine fits and slope bounds allow only \\(K\\) intercept bins per descendant/base cell.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.3", "orig": "three horizontal traversals, equations \\((9.2)\\)–\\((9.4)\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Intercept bins carrying \\(1-o(1)\\) of \\(\\widetilde\\mu\\)-mass each occupy at least \\(K^{-1}\\omega^{-3}\\) base cells.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use intercept bins from Lemma \\(14.2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The switching state records \\(C\\), fine centered base, and fine residual \\(x\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Prune light appended states to obtain conditional palette domination.</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize the retained incidence law to \\(\\widetilde\\mu\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Intercept bins carrying \\(1-o(1)\\) of \\(\\widetilde\\mu\\)-mass each occupy at least \\(K^{-1}\\omega^{-3}\\) base cells.</p>", "proof": "<p>Make three repetitions of state-conditional refresh followed by indexed-line-conditional time resampling. Both preserve \\(\\widetilde\\mu\\) and \\(C\\). Discard short trajectories and require consecutive velocity differences and the first two internal-time gap to exceed an inverse subpower. Stationarity, palette nonconcentration, and long-line time-density bounds make failure \\(o(1)\\).</p><p>Conditional on the full past, the unnormalized good kernel of velocity and time is bounded by \\(K\\pi\\otimes d\\tau\\). Backward iteration gives product domination. Up to \\(o(\\omega)\\), the switched trajectories obey\n\\[\n\\frac{d\\widehat Z}{d\\widehat t}=c_i,\\qquad\n\\frac{d\\widehat Y}{d\\widehat t}=-\\widehat Z+\\widehat t\\,c_i.\n\\]\nFor \\(D_j=c_{j+1}-c_j\\),\n\\[\nZ_f=Z_0+c_1(\\tau_3-\\tau_0)+\\sum_{j=1}^2D_j(\\tau_3-\\tau_j),\n\\]\n\\[\nY_f=Y_0+(-Z_0+\\tau_0c_1)(\\tau_3-\\tau_0)\n+\\sum_{j=1}^2D_j\\tau_j(\\tau_3-\\tau_j).\n\\]\nThe Jacobian is\n\\[\n\\left|\\det\\frac{\\partial(\\tau_3,Z_f,Y_f)}\n{\\partial(\\tau_1,\\tau_2,\\tau_3)}\\right|\n=|2D_1D_2(\\tau_2-\\tau_1)|\\ge K^{-1}.\n\\]\nRegular multiplicity is at most \\(2\\): at fixed \\(\\tau_3,Z_f\\), one linear equation remains, and \\(Y_f\\) is a nonconstant quadratic at regular solutions. Hence each endpoint cell has probability at most \\(K\\omega^3\\), uniformly in start and controls.</p><p>If \\(e_C\\) is failure probability conditional on \\(C\\), its weighted average is \\(o(1)\\). Bins with \\(e_C\\le1/2\\) carry \\(1-o(1)\\) mass. Their regular endpoints remain in \\(C\\) with probability at least \\(1/2\\), proving the lower cell count.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Proposition \\(9.5\\), Parameter bootstrap below the critical rate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For a fixed sufficiently small \\(s_{\\max}>0\\), \\(\\mathcal G(2k)\\) holds anew on every dense input and subsequence.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the horizontal model with \\(2k&lt;1\\), bounded affine residual \\(x\\), and fixed fine velocity grid \\(d_0\\), width \\(\\chi\\le N^{-e_0}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Original Definition \\(9.3\\): for \\(2k\\le u\\le1\\), property \\(\\mathcal G(u)\\) means that, for every sufficiently small fixed depth \\(s\\) and every dense input, a further dense restriction and subsequence has disjoint full-time labels in groups \\((d_0,P_D)\\), \\(D=a_s^u\\), each of active mass at least\n\\[\nK^{-1}\\pi(d_0)D^2a_s^d,\n\\]\nand predictor \\(x=H(t,P)+O(Ka_s)\\) throughout unit time. \\(H\\) is affine in \\(P\\), with coefficients affine in \\(t\\), and bounded by \\(K\\) on the scaled group box.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a fixed sufficiently small \\(s_{\\max}&gt;0\\), \\(\\mathcal G(2k)\\) holds anew on every dense input and subsequence. Finitely many prescribed depths can be prepared simultaneously, with current floors, unchanged earlier full-domain witnesses, and original true assignment priors.</p>", "proof": "<p>The coefficient count starts \\(\\mathcal G(1)\\). Lemma \\(15.1\\) supplies whole-domain comparisons and lists. Lemma \\(15.2\\) improves each exponent strictly above \\(2k\\); Lemma \\(15.3\\) constructs the endpoint labels and simultaneous current floors. This proves \\(\\mathcal G(2k)\\) with the stated quantifiers.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma \\(9.4\\), Comparison with full-time predictors", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After dense restriction, \\( \\|\\mathcal L_{s_0}-H_0\\|\\le Ka_0 \\) on the entire time-block/parameter product domain.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(2k&lt;u\\le1\\) or \\(u=2k\\), and \\(\\mathcal G(u)\\) holds.</p><p class=\"li\">At depth \\(s_0\\), put \\(a_0=a_{s_0}\\), \\(D_0=a_0^u\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Prepare true affine entries at \\(s_0\\) and finitely many nearby deeper depths before applying \\(\\mathcal G(u)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a true entry, write\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_s(t,P)=L_s(t,p+tv_{d_0},q_0-tp).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After dense restriction,\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal L_{s_0}-H_0\\|\\le Ka_0\n\\]\non the entire time-block/parameter product domain. Given \\(H_0\\) and the block, only \\(K\\) true affine entries occur. Deeper paired entries have stored full-domain witnesses of mass \\(m_{Q_s}/K\\), and \\(L_s-L_{s_0}\\) has norm at most \\(Ka_0\\) on the whole child box.</p>", "proof": "<p>Prepare ancestor/child pair witnesses using full child budgets and the finite subentry count. Their base filling and affine Remez give the whole-child comparison.</p><p>Slice by \\(G=(d_0,P_{D_0})\\) and time block \\(I\\), with reference numerator\n\\[\nT_{GI}=|I|\\pi(d_0)D_0^2a_0^d.\n\\]\nFor a true label and direction, at most \\(Kh_{s_0}^3/D_0^2\\) parameter cells meet its spatial box. Thus both lists’ reference sums are subpower. Prune light entries and retain their separate witnesses.</p><p>On sufficiently fine internal \\((t,P)\\)-meshes, reconstruction error \\(K(\\chi+N^{-e_0})\\) is below mesh widths, and graph evaluation errors are negligible. Jitter paired points to realize index-base ceilings \\(KT_{GI}\\) times box-uniform measure. Group power-excess discrepancies by neighboring jets. Stored floors under a band cap give list size \\(K(M_0/a_0)^d\\). A group with bad mass comparable to summed reference weights satisfies Proposition \\(4\\), a contradiction. Send tolerance slowly to zero; the \\(Ka_0\\)-band count gives the \\(K\\)-list.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "strict bootstrap improvement in Proposition \\(9.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\mathcal G\\bigl(u/(1+\\theta)\\bigr) \\) holds, with a fixed reconstruction buffer independent of the distance \\(u-2k\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(2k&lt;u\\le1\\), and \\(\\mathcal G(u)\\) holds.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\n\\theta=(1-d)(u-2k)/10000.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathcal G\\bigl(u/(1+\\theta)\\bigr)\n\\]\nholds, with a fixed reconstruction buffer independent of the distance \\(u-2k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For target \\(s_1\\), set \\(s_0=s_1/(1+\\theta)\\), \\(D_0=a_0^u\\). Compare \\(H_0\\) to true affine entries by Lemma \\(15.1\\), choosing one true entry per \\(H_0\\)/time block at subpower cost. Prune poor success fractions while keeping the full conditioned prior \\(\\nu_H\\), with denominator\n\\[\n\\nu^\\flat(H_0)\\sim_{\\log,N}\\pi(d_0)D_0^2a_0^d.\n\\]\nNormalize\n\\[\ny=(P-P_c)/D_0,\\qquad\n\\bar x=(x-H_0)/a_0,\\qquad\nF(t)=\\nabla_y(\\mathcal L_{s_0}-H_0)/a_0.\n\\]\nHere \\(y\\) is static and \\(\\bar x\\) affine and bounded.</p><p>Set \\(E=(u-2k)s_0/10\\), \\(\\zeta=N^{-E}\\). Integrated caps give \\(K|I|r^2w^d\\) for parameter \\(r\\)-squares and scalar width \\(w\\ge\\zeta\\). Threshold the weighted density to indicator labels, then prune line/time-bin pairs with insufficient duration. The duration argument yields instantaneous caps \\(Kr^2w^d\\) under the unchanged \\(\\nu_H\\).</p><p>Earlier full-domain true-pair witnesses count scalar residual support. At depth \\(s_0+s'\\), compatible boxes lie in a common horizontal box. Their floors and the old \\(a_0\\)-band cap give at most \\(K(a_0/a_{s_0+s'})^d\\) possibilities. The variation across \\(D_0\\) is negligible because\n\\[\nD_0/h_{s_0+E}^2\\ll\\zeta.\n\\]\nDelete light instantaneous scalar bins to obtain local \\(d\\)-support counts.</p><p>Regularize the scalar-normal input on finite width grids. Shared-line time-pair mass is dense by Jensen. If \\(|F(t)-F(t')|=M\\gg r\\), scalar support and mixed caps bound shared-line mass by \\(K(r/M)^{1-d}\\). Anchor-time selection and separated interval colors therefore give \\(F\\)-comparison with \\(\\zeta\\)-slack.</p><p>In typical static \\(y_r\\)-groups, long-duration support and Lemma \\(8.1\\) give heavy joint parameter/coefficient bins of active mass at least \\(r^{2+d}/K\\). Time-subentry floors give \\(K\\) slope lists per spacetime cell. Choose one slope and separated cell colors at every tested scale to obtain residual comparison. The fixed-prior bin floors imply the conditional parameter-bin cap\n\\[\nK(q/r)^{2+d}.\n\\]\nThese are all scalar-normal hypotheses.</p><p>Apply Lemma \\(8.4\\) with finest fit length \\(H_*=N^{-E/4}\\), reading \\(w=N^{-\\theta s_0}\\). Its entropy conclusion gives subpower residual-slope lists conditional on time and scalar bin. Prune short lines and choose one active \\(t_*\\) with dense line weight. At \\(t_*\\), only \\(Kw^{-d}\\) value/slope bin pairs occur. Exact affine evolution gives full-time predictors\n\\[\n\\bar x(t)=b+F(t_*)y+(t-t_*)\\bigl(b'+A(t_*)y\\bigr)+O(Kw).\n\\]\nRestore \\(H_0\\): thickness is \\(a_0w=a_{s_1}\\), while\n\\[\nD_0=a_{s_1}^{u/(1+\\theta)}.\n\\]\nDelete light new labels using their count and original denominator.</p><p>All internal physical reconstruction meshes need only fixed multiples of \\(s_{\\max}\\): normalized widths are above \\(N^{-E}\\), and coefficient amplification is at most \\(K/(D_0a_0)\\le KN^{2s_{\\max}}\\). Choose the fixed \\(s_{\\max}\\) below the reconstruction buffer \\(e_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.3", "orig": "endpoint sequence and actual labels in Proposition \\(9.5\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The bootstrap produces actual full-time labels at endpoint parameter scale \\(D_*=a_s^{2k}\\), not merely limiting exponents.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\alpha=(1-d)/10000\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nu_0=1,\\qquad u_{j+1}=\\frac{u_j}{1+\\alpha(u_j-2k)}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The bootstrap produces actual full-time labels at endpoint parameter scale \\(D_*=a_s^{2k}\\), not merely limiting exponents.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(13.5\\) gives \\(\\mathcal G(1)\\). Lemma \\(15.2\\) iterates the displayed recurrence. Direct calculation shows \\(u_j&gt;2k\\), \\(u_{j+1}&lt;u_j\\), and \\(u_j\\to2k\\).</p><p>For each finite \\(j\\), perform its finite bootstrap chain on a further subsequence. Choose late samples so all accumulated losses and retained reciprocal masses have logarithmic exponent at most \\(1/j\\); diagonalize only afterward.</p><p>Let \\(D_j=a_s^{u_j}\\), \\(R_j=D_*/D_j=N^{o(1)}\\). Keep old predictor labels distinct and split by the new \\(D_*\\)-grid. Their affine parameter norms grow by at most \\(CK_j(1+R_j)\\). The endpoint floor numerator is \\(R_j^2\\) times the old numerator, whose sum is subpower. Delete light split labels with a sufficiently enlarged subpower denominator. This gives actual endpoint norms and floors.</p><p>For finitely many depths, repeat on current dense inputs, preserve earlier witnesses, and impose all final floor families simultaneously by Lemma \\(14.1\\), including full-time chart prior-mass floors. Priors are not replaced by the final incidence law.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Proposition \\(9.6\\), Return to wide intervals", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every substantial residual supplies three nearby depths meeting Proposition \\(12\\)’s wide-interval hypotheses.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the horizontal model from Proposition \\(13\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(2k\\ne1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every substantial residual supplies three nearby depths meeting Proposition \\(12\\)’s wide-interval hypotheses. It consequently supplies a quadratic time-improvement candidate with fixed positive depth gain, whole-block fit, hidden derivative \\(1\\), and mass measured against the original full true-descendant assignment.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(16.1\\) and \\(16.2\\) construct three wide projected layers in both noncritical regimes. Lemma \\(16.3\\) verifies all current conditional estimates, invokes Proposition \\(12\\), and restores the candidate to original tangent-parent mass and fitting units. The construction applies anew on each substantial residual, so Proposition \\(7\\) excludes this regime.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "parameter charts and fiber correction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a bounded correction \\(H_{\\mathrm{corr}}(t,\\widetilde P)\\), affine in parameters with affine time coefficients, and three nearby depths \\(s_i\\), such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix sufficiently small \\(s_0&gt;0\\), \\(D_0=h_{s_0}^2\\), and \\(\\widetilde P=(P-P_c)/D_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(2k&gt;1\\), use parameter groups directly with \\(H_0=0,A_0=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(2k&lt;1\\), use Proposition \\(15\\) at \\(s_0\\) and \\(s_b=3s_0/2\\), with \\(A_0=a_{s_0}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Typical chart prior denominators are comparable to \\(\\pi(d_0)D_0^2A_0^d\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a bounded correction \\(H_{\\mathrm{corr}}(t,\\widetilde P)\\), affine in parameters with affine time coefficients, and three nearby depths \\(s_i\\), such that\n\\[\n\\nabla_{\\widetilde P}\n\\left((\\mathcal L_{s_i}-H_0)/A_0-H_{\\mathrm{corr}}\\right)\n\\cdot(1,2t)\n\\le K a_{s_i}/A_0\n\\]\nin absolute value on paired events.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use scalar parameter\n\\[\nR(t)=\\widetilde P_2-2t\\widetilde P_1,\n\\]\nwhose fibers have direction \\(W(t)=(1,2t)\\).</p><p>For \\(2k&lt;1\\), the shared time signal\n\\[\nG_0(t)=(D_0/A_0)\\nabla_P(\\mathcal L_{s_0}-H_0)\\cdot W(t)\n\\]\nagrees with the quadratic time polynomial from \\(H_b-H_0\\) to \\(N^{-\\kappa}\\), with\n\\[\n\\kappa=\\frac{s_0}{4}\\min(k,1-2k)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe two errors are \\(KD_0/h_{s_b}\\) and \\(K(D_0/A_0)a_{s_b}/h_{s_b}^2\\).</p><p>Retain times where the paired time marginal exceeds half its total mass. Prior-times-time domination supplies one line whose paired time set has inverse-subpower length; its \\(H_b\\)-label is fixed. Remez bounds the corresponding quadratic coefficients. Restore every paired line at those same times, retaining dense mass. Realize \\(b_0+b_1t+b_2t^2\\) as the \\(W\\)-derivative of\n\\[\n(b_0+b_1t)\\widetilde P_1+(b_2t/2)\\widetilde P_2.\n\\]\nFor \\(2k&gt;1\\), take \\(H_{\\mathrm{corr}}=0\\).</p><p>Put \\(C=C_d+3\\), and\n\\[\nB_*=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\min(s_0/2,\\kappa,ks_0/(1+k)),&amp;2k&lt;1,\\\\\n\\min(s_0/2,(2k-1)s_0),&amp;2k&gt;1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nChoose\n\\[\n\\delta=B_*/4,\\quad s=s_0+\\delta,\\quad\nu=\\min(\\delta/4,k\\delta/(4C)),\\quad\nc_2=(1-d)u/8,\n\\]\nand \\(s_i\\in\\{s,s+u,s+c_2\\}\\). These strict positive margins make the whole-child comparison errors, correction error, and horizontal slope errors smaller than \\(a_{s_i}/A_0\\), proving the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "scalar parameter projection and return to transverse intervals", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Proposition \\(11\\) gives full-time quadratic predictors for \\(x^\\circ\\) in \\((t,R)\\), at width \\(w_i\\), with full interval scale and conditional mass at least \\(w_i^d/K\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Lemma \\(16.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nx^\\circ=(x-H_0(t,P))/A_0-H_{\\mathrm{corr}}(t,\\widetilde P),\n\\qquad w_i=a_{s_i}/A_0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Proposition \\(11\\) gives full-time quadratic predictors for \\(x^\\circ\\) in \\((t,R)\\), at width \\(w_i\\), with full interval scale and conditional mass at least \\(w_i^d/K\\). Returning to true coordinates and slicing \\(p=Z(0)\\) gives projected \\((t,Y)\\)-labels of common width \\(D_0\\), with floors\n\\[\nK^{-1}a_{s_i}^dw_\\alpha D_0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At fixed time, the map \\(\\widetilde P\\mapsto(R,R')\\) has determinant magnitude \\(2\\). Reconstructing \\(P\\) from \\((Z,Y)\\) has error \\(K(N^{-e_0}+\\chi)\\). Use a fixed scalar base mesh of depth \\(20s_0\\), and integrate true joint caps on much finer meshes. After division by the fixed chart denominator, scalar-bin mass is at most \\(K\\sigma_R^2\\varrho^d\\), gaining a positive angular power when \\(R'\\) is restricted.</p><p>For support, compatible true boxes at a \\((t,R)\\)-cell lie in one common enlarged horizontal box of volume \\(Kh_{s_i}^4\\). Sum earlier whole-domain witnesses: for \\(2k&gt;1\\), use the bounded-residual base cap; for \\(2k&lt;1\\), use the shared \\(a_{s_0}\\)-band cap. Their floors give at most \\(Kw_i^{-d}\\) labels.</p><p>Lemma \\(16.1\\) allows evaluation at \\(\\widetilde P=(0,R)\\) with error \\(Kw_i\\). Slope bounds and the fine fixed reconstruction buffer make variation across a scalar base cell negligible. Thus terminal support has at most \\(Kw_i^{-d}\\) scalar bins per cell. Apply Proposition \\(11\\), including angular gain.</p><p>Slice \\(p\\) at \\(\\chi\\), and replace\n\\[\nP\\text{ by }P^\\#=(p_\\alpha,Y+tp_\\alpha)\n\\]\nin the restored predictor. The resulting expression is quadratic in \\((t,Y)\\) and has norm \\(K\\) on a moving width-\\(D_0\\) interval. Reconstruction errors are below fitted widths. Light-slice deletion is summable because each unsliced numerator\n\\[\n\\pi(d_0)D_0^2a_{s_i}^d\n\\]\nsplits into at most \\(KD_0/\\chi\\) numerators \\(\\pi(d_0)\\chi D_0a_{s_i}^d\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.3", "orig": "final wide hypotheses and mass restoration", "depth": 1, "snippet": "They satisfy Proposition \\(12\\) on a final dense law with renewed conditional velocity domination.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the three projected layers from Lemma \\(16.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>They satisfy Proposition \\(12\\) on a final dense law with renewed conditional velocity domination. Its resulting mass restores to\n\\[\nq_{\\mathrm{desc}}\\nu_{\\mathrm{desc}}N^{-dc_2}/K\n\\]\nin initial tangent-parent units.</p>", "proof": "<p>Prepare fine states containing true \\(Q_s\\), true base, and residual bins, but no projected label. Deep histories have \\(K\\) appended choices. Their earlier reference numerators sum to the true budgets. Lemma \\(14.1\\) simultaneously restores all three projected floors and history-state floors, giving final conditional palette domination without changing earlier witnesses.</p><p>All interval widths equal \\(D_0\\), and\n\\[\nb_s/D_0\\sim_{\\log,N}N^{-2k(s-s_0)},\\qquad\nh_s\\beta_{\\max}/\\beta_{\\min}\\sim_{\\log,N}N^{-ks}.\n\\]\nThe choices in Lemma \\(16.1\\) give\n\\[\n(C_d+3)u&lt;\\min(2k(s-s_0),ks),\n\\]\nwith strict slack. True affine entries have bounded norms in their \\(H/H^2\\) boxes by Lemma \\(13.4\\). Thus Proposition \\(12\\) applies.</p><p>If its relative descendant has time \\(H_s\\) and prior \\(\\nu^\\flat(Q_s)\\), normalization gives\n\\[\nq_{\\mathrm{desc}}=q_rH_s,\\qquad\n\\nu_{\\mathrm{desc}}=\\nu(Q_r)\\nu^\\flat(Q_s).\n\\]\nRestore \\(q_r\\nu(Q_r)\\) and \\(w=L+a_rx\\). This gives the displayed mass and a permitted hidden test of derivative \\(1\\), fitting throughout the assigned block.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Proposition \\(10.1\\), Exclusion of unbounded optimized narrowness", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The assumption \\(\\ell=\\infty\\) is impossible.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use version \\(2\\)’s extremal setup in Proposition \\(6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose, for contradiction, that its global optimized narrowness is \\(\\ell=\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Start after a true intermediate normalization, with length-one maximizing problems satisfying\n\\[\nn_i(0)\\sim_{\\log}1,\\qquad\nf_i(r)\\to dr\\text{ uniformly},\\quad d_i\\to d=d_*&lt;1,\\quad H(E_i)\\to k&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Within fixed \\(E_i\\), \\(K_0=N_0^{o(1)}\\) is an actual subpower factor. On the outer diagonal, \\(K=N^{o(1)}\\) is only an analytical factor.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The assumption \\(\\ell=\\infty\\) is impossible. Therefore the global optimized narrowness is finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(17.1\\) and \\(17.2\\) give full-time localization and balanced packets with time exponents tending to zero. Lemmas \\(17.3\\) and \\(17.4\\) establish scalar caps and support under unchanged fixed budgets. Lemma \\(17.5\\) constructs an actual atlas at fixed positive depth \\(c'\\).</p><p>For sufficiently large fixed \\(i\\), such an atlas on an exact subsequence would imply, by normalization and terminal completion,\n\\[\nH(E_i)\\le kc'/2+k(d_i)(1-c').\n\\]\nThe right side tends to \\(k-kc'/2&lt;k\\), contradicting maximization. As in Lemma \\(7.3\\), bounded-complexity outputs with actual subpower bounds and mass \\(p_{\\mathrm{path}}/\\log N_0\\) are therefore excluded at every sufficiently late sample of that fixed exact problem. Choose the diagonal beyond those cutoffs before the analytical searches. Lemma \\(17.5\\) contradicts this exclusion. Hence \\(\\ell&lt;\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.1", "orig": "Proposition \\(10.2\\), Full-time localization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A permitted selection admits full-time depth-zero known charts \\(C\\), with tangential width \\(1\\), normal width \\( g_C=g_2N_0^{2u}, \\) native test \\(P_*\\), and actual original \\(p_1\\)-lists.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Choose\n\\[\n0&lt;u&lt;\\eta,\\qquad0&lt;s_1&lt;s_2&lt;1,\\qquad(d+2k)s_2&lt;Su/4.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Nested true nodes \\(Q_1,Q_2\\) have depths tending to \\(s_1,s_2\\), times \\(q_j=N_0^{-h_{j,i}+o(1)}\\), \\(h_{j,i}\\to ks_j\\), and spatial determinants \\(q_j^2g_j\\), \\(g_j=N_0^{-\\alpha_{j,i}}\\), with \\(\\alpha_{2,i}-\\alpha_{1,i}\\to\\infty\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A permitted selection admits full-time depth-zero known charts \\(C\\), with tangential width \\(1\\), normal width\n\\[\ng_C=g_2N_0^{2u},\n\\]\nnative test \\(P_*\\), and actual original \\(p_1\\)-lists. For retained earlier incidences, normals and center velocities satisfy\n\\[\n|n_1-n_C|\\le K_0g_1,\\qquad\n|n_C\\cdot(y_1'-y_C')|\\le K_0g_1\n\\]\nafter consistent signs.</p>", "proof": "<p>The nested systems come from successive maximizing segments and \\(\\ell=\\infty\\). Saturation gives\n\\[\n\\nu_\\gamma(Q_j)\\sim_{\\log}n_i(r_{j,i})q_j^2g_j.\n\\]\nEndpoint bounds confine normal positions to \\(K_0q_jg_j\\) and normal velocities to \\(K_0g_j\\). Composition and largest-axis fullness give \\(|\\sin\\angle(n_1,n_2)|\\le K_0g_1\\).</p><p>Sample two independent times on the same original trajectory, requiring path incidence at each. Jensen gives inverse-subpower pair mass. Remove angular and pure-density exceptions before counting. For fixed first \\(Q_2\\), if opposite normals differ by more than \\(g_2N_0^u\\), velocity is confined to a ball of radius \\(K_0N_0^{-u}\\). The partner’s actual terminal list gives only subpower angular entries. Integrate good angular numerators over the first label’s full-time extrapolated strip of area \\(K_0g_2\\), and sum at most\n\\[\nK_0/(n_i(r_{2,i})q_2^2g_2)\n\\]\nfirst labels. Failure mass is at most\n\\[\nN_0^{-Su/2+(d+2k)s_2+o_i(1)+o_{N_0}(1)},\n\\]\nwhich is power-small.</p><p>Fix the first time. Bin its \\(Q_2\\)-normal and affine normal-center intercept/velocity at width \\(g_C\\), omitting the old label identity. These data define full-time thin charts. The opposite \\(Q_2\\)-label lists neighboring bins for all three data, so the new labels are actually known by \\(p_1\\). Since \\(g_C/g_1\\to0\\) by a power along large \\(i\\), the earlier-node comparisons follow.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.2", "orig": "Lemma \\(10.3\\), Normalization and relative density cancellation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "They contain terminal-known charts \\(A\\) of time \\(q_A=N_0^{-h_{A,i}+o(1)}\\), \\(h_{A,i}\\to0\\), with \\( \\frac{\\nu_\\gamma(A)}{J_A} =N_0^{-db_i+\\epsilon_i+o_{N_0}(1)} =N_0^{o_i(1)+o_{N_0}(1)}, \\qquad b_i,\\epsilon_i\\to0. \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the full-time charts \\(C\\) of Lemma \\(17.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>They contain terminal-known charts \\(A\\) of time \\(q_A=N_0^{-h_{A,i}+o(1)}\\), \\(h_{A,i}\\to0\\), with\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu_\\gamma(A)}{J_A}\n=N_0^{-db_i+\\epsilon_i+o_{N_0}(1)}\n=N_0^{o_i(1)+o_{N_0}(1)},\n\\qquad b_i,\\epsilon_i\\to0.\n\\]\nThis is a two-limit statement. If \\(e_Z\\) is tangent to \\(C\\),\n\\[\n\\|D_A^Te_Z\\|\\ge q_A/K_0,\\qquad\n\\|D_A^Tn_C\\|\\le K_0g_Cq_A.\n\\]\nActual label lists and geometric/native derivative bounds remain subpower within fixed \\(i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize spatially inside \\(C\\), retaining hidden coordinate, time, thickness, and native test. New world weights are proportional to \\(\\omega_\\gamma\\nu_\\gamma(C)\\), with total normalization between \\(K_0^{-1}\\) and \\(1\\). Terminal density is\n\\[\nn_i'(1)\\sim_{\\log}n_i(1)g_C/\\nu_\\gamma(C).\n\\]\nIntermediate upper bounds preserve the endpoint cap; angular cells map into controlled old angular cells. Lifting gives\n\\[\nH(E_i)\\le H(E_i')\\le k(d_i),\n\\]\nso the normalized sequence maximizes despite arbitrary absolute density shifts.</p><p>Choose \\(b_i\\downarrow0\\) slowly and true systems in the \\(b_i\\)-coarsenings with time cost tending to zero, using Proposition \\(2\\). Relative-profile linearity gives\n\\[\nf_i'(1)-f_i'(b_i)=d(1-b_i)+o_i(1).\n\\]\nSaturation and composition give\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu_\\gamma(A)}{J_A}\n\\sim_{\\log}n_i(1)\\frac{n_i'(b_i)}{n_i'(1)}.\n\\]\nThe absolute primed shift cancels, leaving exponent \\(-db_i+o_i(1)\\).</p><p>Keep original assignment budgets\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\gamma,I,A}\\omega_\\gamma q_A\\nu_\\gamma(A)\\le1.\n\\]\nCompose actual \\(C\\)-lists and own-depth primed lists at \\(p_1\\). The normal matrix bound follows by composition. If the tangential row were power-deficient, these actual lists and the old angular cap would force power-small total mass, as in Lemma \\(2.4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(10.4\\), Graph descriptions on the earlier charts", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After analytical loss \\(o(p_{\\mathrm{path}})\\), every used \\(Q_1\\) has a frozen disjoint earlier witness of mass at least \\(q_1\\nu_\\gamma(Q_1)/K\\), satisfying the prepared zero-depth cap.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the original tempered path reference \\(\\mu_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\), mass \\(p_{\\mathrm{path}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Prepare pure and angular masks before sparse searches.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(a=N^{-s_1}\\) on a sufficiently late outer diagonal.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After analytical loss \\(o(p_{\\mathrm{path}})\\), every used \\(Q_1\\) has a frozen disjoint earlier witness of mass at least \\(q_1\\nu_\\gamma(Q_1)/K\\), satisfying the prepared zero-depth cap. In normalized \\(Q_1\\)-base coordinates, retained incidences have at most \\(K\\) stable core/algebraic-graph choices with\n\\[\n|X-G(\\widehat u)|\\le Ka,\\qquad |G|+|\\nabla G|\\le K\n\\]\non the used interiors. The fixed prepared measure has mass \\((1-o(1))p_{\\mathrm{path}}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Delete labels below the full \\(q_1\\nu_\\gamma(Q_1)\\) budget floor and freeze the survivors before core restrictions. Their base density ceiling gives inverse-subpower projection volume.</p><p>Write \\(z=Bw\\). The true test has\n\\[\n|P|\\le K_0a_1^{\\mathrm{fit}},\\quad\nK_0^{-1}\\le|P_z|\\le K_0,\\quad\n|P_{zz}|\\le K_0/a_1^{\\mathrm{fit}}.\n\\]\nThe discriminant \\(P_z^2-2P_{zz}P\\) is bounded on the filled projection, hence its coefficients and derivatives are bounded by \\(K\\). Above curvature cutoff \\((a_1^{\\mathrm{fit}}K')^{-1}\\), use the affine critical center; below it, discriminant positivity gives simple holomorphic branches on inverse-subpower cores. Delete light core/branch entries using the same assignment budgets.</p><p>Compose these branches with \\(P_*\\). Native derivative and curvature bounds give error \\(Ka_1^{\\mathrm{fit}}\\), and \\(|X|\\le K_0\\) bounds the composed graph on inverse-subpower real volume. Lemma \\(4.1\\) yields uniform graph and derivative bounds. The earlier whole-label witnesses remain separate from the final core-restricted law.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.4", "orig": "Lemma \\(10.5\\), Projected cap and support bounds", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every used projected law has scalar-bin density at most \\(Kp^d\\) per unit \\((\\tau,\\zeta)\\)-area for \\(p=N^{-r}\\), \\(0\\le r\\le s_1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In an \\(A\\)-block, let\n\\[\n\\tau=(t-t_A)/q_A,\\qquad\n\\zeta=(e_Z\\cdot y-Z_*)/q_A.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the fixed-budget law\n\\[\n\\lambda_A\n=\\frac{(T_A)_*(\\mu_{\\mathrm{prep},\\gamma}|_{I,A})}\n{q_A\\nu_\\gamma(A)},\n\\qquad\nR_A=\\omega_\\gamma q_A\\nu_\\gamma(A).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\sum_AR_A\\le1\\) and\n\\[\n\\|\\mu_{\\mathrm{prep}}\\|=\\sum_AR_A\\|\\lambda_A\\|.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every used projected law has scalar-bin density at most \\(Kp^d\\) per unit \\((\\tau,\\zeta)\\)-area for \\(p=N^{-r}\\), \\(0\\le r\\le s_1\\). At \\(p=a\\), adjoining a \\(\\zeta'\\)-bin of width \\(N^{-v}\\), \\(0&lt;v\\le u_1&lt;u\\), gains \\(KN^{-Sv}\\).</p><p>For sufficiently large fixed \\(M\\), \\(\\sigma\\asymp N^{-M}\\), each \\((\\tau,\\zeta)\\)-cell meets at most \\(Ka^{-d}\\) occupied \\(X_a\\)-bins; total terminal support is at most \\(Ka^{-d}\\sigma^{-2}\\). These bounds persist under restriction.</p>", "proof": "<p>Tangential fullness implies that a \\(\\zeta\\)-interval of length \\(b\\) cuts area at most \\(K_0J_Ab\\) in the \\(A\\)-domain. A scalar \\(X_p\\)-bin allows only \\(K_0\\) hidden bins by native monotonicity. Integrate pure caps, change time, and divide by the fixed denominator. The result is\n\\[\nKp^dJ_A/\\nu_\\gamma(A)\\le Kp^d\n\\]\non the chosen diagonal. The \\(C\\)-label specifies normal velocity to \\(K_0g_C\\); a tangential velocity bin therefore confines full velocity to controlled old angular bins. Sum good terminal numerators only through permitted ancestors to obtain the angular factor.</p><p>For support, fix \\(A\\), a projected cell, and a meeting \\(Q_1\\)-time block. Every compatible whole \\(Q_1\\)-domain lies in one moving box of area \\(K_0q_1^2g_1\\), using the normal and center-velocity comparisons of Lemma \\(17.1\\). Charge its entire earlier witness there. Disjoint floors and the same zero-depth cap imply\n\\[\nL_1\\le K/n_i(r_{1,i})\\le KN^{ds_1}\n\\]\ncompatible labels.</p><p>For two same-cell occurrences,\n\\[\n|\\Delta t|+|\\Delta Z|\\le K_0q_A\\sigma,\n\\]\nand, after subtracting the \\(Q_1\\)-center,\n\\[\n|n_1\\cdot(\\Delta y-y_1'\\Delta t)|\n\\le K_0(g_1q_A\\sigma+g_C).\n\\]\nThus normalized \\(Q_1\\)-base difference is at most\n\\[\nK_0\\left(\\frac{q_A\\sigma}{q_1}+\\frac{g_C}{q_1g_1}\\right).\n\\]\nThe first narrow factor cancels before inversion. Its diagonal bound is\n\\[\nN^{-M+ks_1+o(1)}\n+N^{-(\\alpha_{2,i}-\\alpha_{1,i})+2u+ks_1+o(1)}\\ll a.\n\\]\nLemma \\(17.3\\) therefore gives only \\(K\\) scalar bins per graph per compatible label. Multiply by \\(L_1\\) and sum the bounded projected range.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.5", "orig": "Sections \\(10.4\\) and \\(10.7\\), scalar candidates and actual improvement", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is an actual original \\(p_1\\)-known atlas at depth \\(c'>0\\), with time \\(q_A\\), original mass at least \\(p_{\\mathrm{path}}/\\log N_0\\), actual subpower slacks and lists, and the fixed complexity bounds required for …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed prepared law and projections of Lemma \\(17.4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d&lt;d_0&lt;1\\) and Proposition \\(4\\)’s tolerance \\(\\epsilon_*\\) for quadratic graphs in two base variables.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose\n\\[\n0&lt;\\theta&lt;\\min(1,\\epsilon_*/4),\\qquad\n2\\theta&lt;\\epsilon&lt;\\epsilon_*,\n\\]\n\\[\n0&lt;\\delta&lt;\\min\\left(\\theta s_1/4,\\epsilon s_1/(2C_{\\mathrm{match}})\\right),\n\\qquad c'=\\theta s_1/2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an actual original \\(p_1\\)-known atlas at depth \\(c'&gt;0\\), with time \\(q_A\\), original mass at least \\(p_{\\mathrm{path}}/\\log N_0\\), actual subpower slacks and lists, and the fixed complexity bounds required for finite-sample exclusion.</p>", "proof": "<p>On every substantial residual, the budget identity selects an \\(A\\) whose residual \\(\\lambda_A\\)-mass is inverse subpower. Proposition \\(11\\), applied with \\(c=1\\) and Lemma \\(17.4\\)’s angular gain, gives a full-time quadratic \\(F(\\tau,\\zeta)\\), ordinary coefficients bounded by \\(K\\), fitting \\(X\\) to \\(Ka\\), with original within-world mass at least\n\\[\nq_A\\nu_\\gamma(A)a^d/K.\n\\]</p><p>Greedily insert tests with coefficient bound \\(N^\\delta\\), fit \\(aN^\\delta\\), and residual mass at least \\(q_A\\nu_\\gamma(A)a^dN^{-\\delta}\\). Assign all still-free eligible trajectories. There are at most \\(N^\\delta a^{-d}\\) tests per \\(A\\). Maximality and the residual assertion force coverage \\(\\|\\mu_c\\|\\ge\\kappa p_{\\mathrm{path}}\\) on a subsequence.</p><p>Condition on \\(O=(p_1,A)\\) and take independent posteriors:\n\\[\n\\Pi=\\int(\\pi_o\\otimes\\pi_o)\\,d(O_*\\mu_c)(o).\n\\]\nBoth marginals equal the unnormalized \\(\\mu_c\\). The common base and terminal hidden bin imply\n\\[\n|F(\\tau,\\zeta)-G(\\tau,\\zeta)|\\le aN^{O(\\delta)}.\n\\]</p><p>Suppose coefficient discrepancies above \\(a^{2\\theta}\\) carry a positive relative pair mass. Budgets select an \\(A\\) where its within-world bad-pair mass is at least \\(q_A\\nu_\\gamma(A)/K\\). Normalize only this pair law. Each polynomial index has base density at most \\(KN^{O(\\delta)}a^d\\); uniform index weights on at most \\(N^\\delta a^{-d}\\) tests give the required marginal domination. Rescale the subpower base range to a fixed domain. Proposition \\(4\\), with \\(M=1,\\Delta=a,R=a^{-1}\\), applies: all upper losses fit \\(R^\\epsilon\\), while the norm gap \\(a^{2\\theta}/K&gt;R^{-\\epsilon}\\). Contradiction.</p><p>Bin the six quadratic coefficients at width \\(a^\\theta\\). Posterior-neighbor lists retain a fixed fraction of covered mass; compose with actual \\(A\\)-lists. Merge assignments by coefficient bin and use\n\\[\nP_{\\mathrm{new}}(t,y,w)\n=P_*(t,y,w)\n-\\widetilde F_{A,b}\\left(\n\\frac{t-t_A}{q_A},\n\\frac{e_Z\\cdot y-Z_*}{q_A}\n\\right).\n\\]\nIts error is at most \\(aN^\\delta+Ka^\\theta\\), below \\(N_0^{-c'}\\) by fixed positive-power margins. The subtracted polynomial does not involve \\(w\\), so native derivative and curvature bounds remain actual original bounds. Keep the actual \\(A\\)-frame and time.</p><p>Store ordered geometric fit tests, priorities, and merger bins. They have polynomial complexity within each fixed original exact problem. Apply Lemma \\(7.6\\) to the original tempered reference law and analytical covered submeasure. This gives actual tempered list tables, loses only \\(O(N_0^{-1})\\), and preserves more than \\(p_{\\mathrm{path}}/\\log N_0\\) mass at late samples.</p>"}], "edges": [[9, 8, "part"], [10, 8, "part"], [11, 8, "part"], [12, 8, "part"], [14, 13, "part"], [15, 13, "part"], [16, 13, "part"], [17, 13, "part"], [18, 13, "part"], [19, 13, "part"], [21, 20, "part"], [22, 20, "part"], [23, 20, "part"], [25, 24, "part"], [26, 24, "part"], [27, 24, "part"], [29, 28, "part"], [30, 28, "part"], [31, 28, "part"], [33, 32, "part"], [34, 32, "part"], [35, 32, "part"], [36, 32, "part"], [38, 37, "part"], [39, 37, "part"], [40, 37, "part"], [41, 37, "part"], [42, 37, "part"], [43, 37, "part"], [44, 37, "part"], [45, 37, "part"], [46, 37, "part"], [48, 47, "part"], [49, 47, "part"], [50, 47, "part"], [51, 47, "part"], [53, 52, "part"], [54, 52, "part"], [55, 52, "part"], [56, 52, "part"], [57, 52, "part"], [58, 52, "part"], [59, 52, "part"], [60, 52, "part"], [61, 52, "part"], [63, 62, "part"], [64, 62, "part"], [65, 62, "part"], [66, 62, "part"], [67, 62, "part"], [69, 68, "part"], [70, 68, "part"], [71, 68, "part"], [73, 72, "part"], [74, 72, "part"], [75, 72, "part"], [77, 76, "part"], [78, 76, "part"], [79, 76, "part"], [80, 76, "part"], [81, 76, "part"], [83, 82, "part"], [84, 82, "part"], [85, 82, "part"], [87, 86, "part"], [88, 86, "part"], [89, 86, "part"], [91, 90, "part"], [92, 90, "part"], [93, 90, "part"], [95, 94, "part"], [96, 94, "part"], [97, 94, "part"], [98, 94, "part"], [99, 94, "part"]]});
